HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:2425:     "note": "THE LAST THREE UNPANELLED ROWS -- the final panel of a nine-panel campaign that took the backlog from 44 open single-seat rows to zero.\n\n*** THE SEATS SPLIT ON `tfreq` IN THE EXACT SHAPE THE BRIEF WARNED ABOUT, AND THE MEASUREMENT SETTLED IT. ***  agy and deepseek said tfreq is ALREADY FIXED and should be closed, not worked; minimax said do it FIRST, as a FIX+DETECTOR round. A direct contradiction about whether a defect still exists -- so it was CHECKED rather than voted.\n\nMEASURED, and it is decisive. BOTH write paths clamp before storing:\n  timer.c:130-136  '#427: clamp before anything is stored, so freq and interval agree' --\n                   freq = timer_clamp_freq(...) THEN newtimer->freq = freq.\n  timer_update_frequency()  '#427/#429: CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store. The order is load-bearing and the shipped order was wrong twice over. THE OLD CODE STORED THE RAW VALUE INTO t->freq AND CLAMPED ONLY THE LOCAL, SO t->freq NAMED A RATE THE TIMER DID NOT RUN AT.'\nThat last sentence IS tfreq's title, in the source, marked fixed. tfreq is FIXED; its row is `active` with commits 863d238/b9daca1/cdc4849 and its hold says 'reviewed; regression batched to Sunday night'. It is awaiting a BATCHED REGRESS REVIEW, not open work.\n\nTHE SEAT-CLASS DOCTRINE PREDICTED THIS EXACTLY: the two seats that could OPEN THE FILE got it right; the packet-fed seat, reasoning from the row's text alone, would have sent a round at an already-closed defect. That is the fourth time in one night that a row's own text pointed the wrong way, and the second time a packet seat was overruled by one grep.\n\nes438: DROP AS A DEFECT ROW -- three seats (agy, deepseek, minimax), and agy's version is the useful one: CLOSE it as a completed review record while SPLITTING OUT the luna fuse-write defect it contains. A process record is not a defect row, but a real defect buried inside one must not be dropped with its container.\n\nrtcnarrow: deepseek would do it first. It is the design item filed earlier tonight, whose own three-device premise was already re-derived and largely dissolved.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 9.5 KB, grok 9.8 KB, kimi 81.4 KB, glm 8.4 KB, deepseek 4.0 KB, minimax 12.3 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, ninth consecutive wall, proven arithmetically again: '21348b = brief 20660b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: *** MEASURED ALREADY FIXED -- close it, do not work it. ***  Two seats said so, one said do it FIRST, and the source settles it: both timer.c write paths clamp BEFORE storing, and timer_update_frequency's own comment states this row's title as the defect it fixed. The row is `active` with commits and its hold already says 'reviewed; regression batched to Sunday night' -- so it is awaiting its BATCHED REGRESS REVIEW, which is a queue position, not open work. NOT CLOSED HERE: codex-held, and a closure needs the full panel.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:5894:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as 4dfc716 (local only).  gate 14 PASS 910 checks at the 43 pin (regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/gate_arm_43rows.log); all four trees cleared both parts after cpu_arm_instr.c was propagated (three trees held an older comment).  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  State stays `held`: the closure records are owed. ***\n\n*** GATE PIN RAISED 39 -> 43 (2026-09-12).  THE GATE MUST BE RE-RUN; ITS LAST GREEN WAS AT 39. ***\n\nTable 7 added four rows to close the floor -- the three store-side modes that had NO ROW AT ALL\n(`ARM_STM_IB_R7`, `ARM_STM_DA_R7`, `ARM_STM_S_R7`) and the tenth cell (`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7`) -- so\nthe probe is 43 rows and the literal moved by hand with its reason.  **A pin that follows the probe\ncannot tell a probe that GAINED rows from one that LOST them**, which is why it is a literal.\n\nOWED FOR `#524`, IN ORDER:\n  1. re-run `gate_arm.sh` at the 43 pin;\n  2. re-add the `arm_bdt_probe.py` MANIFEST entry to `check_probe_wiring.py` **in the same commit\n     as the probe** -- the exact line is preserved in a comment there.  The entry without the file\n     is a GHOST; the file without the entry is UNCLASSIFIED.  Both are red, in opposite\n     directions, and I hit each in turn today;\n  3. restore the `#524` CHANGELOG block (`python _scratchpad/land_shared.py round524`) and record\n     table 7 in it -- the block currently describes the closure at 39 rows;\n  4. commit, AFTER `#525`.\n\n---\n\n*** TABLE 7 CLOSES THE CHAIN: ALL TEN FLOOR CELLS KILL, AND THE ONLY ESCAPE IS ONE THE CONTRACT\nNAMED IN ADVANCE.  2026-09-12 02:32. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/SCORES.txt\n\n    BASE            43/43  CONTROL=OK  FAILING none\n    -- regression, the six from table g: all still die --\n    M_POPP_NONE     41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_POPP_WEAK  41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2\n    M_NPMASK        41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_SBIT_NONE  (dies)\n    M_POPW_WEAK     40/43  DB_P2, S_P2, MULTI_P2_R1          M_POPU_WEAK  (dies)\n    -- the six NEW floor mutants: ALL DIE --\n    M_PUSHS_NONE    42/43  ARM_STM_S_R7          <- E1, the MEASURED L2 breach\n    M_POPP_MASK2    41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2          <- the ~2 gap, live until residue 7\n    M_PUSH_IB_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7         <- a mode with NO ROW AT ALL\n    M_PUSH_DA_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_DA_R7         <- likewise\n    M_PUSHPC_NONE   42/43  ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7      <- the tenth cell\n    M_PUSHP_OVER7   41/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7, ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7   <- E4\n    -- disclosed under clause (e), EXPECTED to escape --\n    D_PUW_WEAK      43/43  FAILING none          <- ESCAPED, exactly as disclosed\n    D_POPSW_WEAK    42/43  ARM_LDM_S_P2          <- DIED, better than disclosed\n    RESTORE         cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED (both parts)\n\n**`D_POPSW_WEAK` dying is the contract being CONSERVATIVE rather than wrong**: upgrading `S_P2` to\n`LDMIA r0!,{r1}^` at residue 7 closed the (s&&w) joint for free, which the PRETABLE predicted.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES THIS TABLE DIFFERENT FROM THE SIX BEFORE IT IS WHERE THE ROWS CAME FROM. ***  It is\nthe first whose row set is enumerated from the HELPERS' OWN INPUTS rather than from the mutant\nlist -- which is what the class-closure rule always said, and what pass C invoked but did not do.\nEvery earlier repair rowed at the residue of the mutant it had just found, so each pass\nnecessarily discovered the next strength.  **Residue 7 (mod 8) discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` AND\n`~7` from ONE access**, so no future strength needs a new residue.\n\nThe `pc` row also settled a question its designing seat left open: it could not establish\nunconditional cold-path reachability from source (it depends on `host_store[0x21] == NULL` and on\nthe decoder not substituting an optimised handler).  **`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7` is GREEN on BASE, so the\ncold path was taken** -- the measurement answered what the reading could not.\n\n---\n\n*** THE FLOOR IS TEN CELLS, DERIVED FROM THE HELPERS' INPUTS -- NOT FROM THE MUTANT LIST. ***\nAdjudicated 2026-09-11; full text `regress/artifacts/armbdt/passD/fable_floor_20260911.md`.\n\n    FLOOR = {cold first access} x {pop, push} x {IA, IB, DA, DB}\n          + {push} x {s = 1}\n          + {push} x {pc in list}          = TEN CELLS, SEVEN OPEN\n    one cold DATA row per cell, access address = 7 (mod 8)\n\n**RESIDUE 7 MOD 8 IS THE WHOLE GEOMETRIC FIX**: it discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` and `~7` at once,\nso no future strength needs a new residue.  Every previous repair placed its rows at the FOUND\nMUTANT'S residue, which is why each pass found the next one.\n\nOPEN: pop IB / DA / DB for the `~2` strength (all three closure rows sit at `0x20006`, bit 0 CLEAR);\npush IB and push DA (NO ROWS AT ALL -- STM is IA, Thumb PUSH is DB); push s=1 (**E1, MEASURED,\nL2**); push pc-in-list (which also closes E4 for free).\n\n---\n\n*** PASS D DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN EITHER. FIVE MORE ESCAPES, MEASURED AT 39/39. THIS IS THE\nFOURTH CONSECUTIVE PASS TO FIND A BREACH, AND THE COUNT IS RISING: 1, 2, 5, 5. ***\n\n    E1 M_PUSHS_NONE   s_bit ? addr : (addr & ~3)               PUSH only   39/39\n    E2 M_POPSW_WEAK   addr & ((s_bit && w_bit) ? ~1 : ~3)      POP         39/39\n    E3 M_POPS_OVER7   addr & (s_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 POP         39/39\n    E4 M_PUSHP_OVER7  addr & (p_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 PUSH only   39/39\n    E5 M_PUW_WEAK     addr & ((p&&u&&w) ? ~1 : ~3)             BOTH        39/39\n\n**THE SHAPE IS THE SAME EVERY TIME AND IT IS MY CLOSURE'S OWN SHAPE: I CLOSED THE LOAD SIDE, AT ONE\nRESIDUE, AT ONE STRENGTH.**  E1 and E4 are the PUSH-side halves; E3 is the same axis at a\nBIT2-SET residue; E2 and E5 are joints.  Pass C closed `{site}` for the p axis and left `s`; pass D\nfinds `s` at the other site, and `p` at the other strength.\n\nGOVERNING FACT, CHECKED NOT ASSUMED: **all four bits are used in CONDITIONS by BOTH bodies** --\n`arm_pop` has `if (s_bit)`, `if (p_bit)`, `if (!p_bit)`, `u_bit? :` and `w_bit`; `arm_push`\nlikewise.  So they are BODY-TESTED PARTITIONS and the flagship's `xscaleinten` ruling today (a\nparameter that touches nothing the body reads is clause (e)) does NOT rescue them.\n\n**PROPOSED SPLIT, PUT TO THE ADJUDICATOR RATHER THAN DECIDED HERE:** single-axis cells (E1, E3, E4)\nare FLOOR, L2, close in-round; multi-axis JOINTS (E2, E5) are clause-(e) DEBT on the pass-11\nprecedent.  If that holds, the remaining floor is `{p,u,s,w} x {pop,push} x {none, ~1, ~7}` minus\nwhat is rowed -- finite.  **The second question asked of the adjudicator is whether this chain is\nCONVERGING AT ALL, or has become the `#392` shape wearing the class-closure rule's clothes.**\n\n*** ONE OF MY SEVEN CLOSURE ROWS IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and the reason is sharper than the\n'kills nothing' I disclosed: `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2` CANNOT fail under a slow-path mutant, because\nTHE FIRST COLD ACCESS WARMS THE PAGE and the second register's access takes the FAST PATH. ***\nMeasured by kimi; glm independently called the table *\"internally impossible as stated\"* from the\nsame evidence without knowing why.  **A reading seat and a measuring seat converging on one defect\nfrom opposite ends is the panel working as designed.**\n\n---\n\n*** GATE GREEN AT THE NEW 39-ROW PIN, 2026-09-11 19:4x. ***  `/tmp/gate_arm_524g.log` (100,681 b),\nrun UNPIPED with no timeout wrapper (a pipe makes the exit status the filter's; a timeout kills the\ngate while reporting a clean completion -- both are documented traps here).\n\n    GATE_EXIT=0\n    ok   bdt rows run       39      <- the class-closure pin, moved 32 -> 39 by hand\n    ok   bdt rows correct   39\n    ^FAIL rows: 0     SKIP occurrences: 0     <- no silent coverage loss\n    gate 14: ARM/Thumb flags (#311/#312/#319-#322/#328/#329): PASS (910 checks)\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE COMMIT: **PASS D on the closure diff.**  Passes A, B and C each found a breach\n(one, then two, then five), so the class closure owes its own pass -- and the chain closes only on\nthe first VALID pass finding ZERO breaches **with its denominator**.  A bare zero does not close\nit.\n\n---\n\n*** THE CLASS CLOSURE IS MEASURED AND ALL FIVE PASS-C ESCAPES ARE DEAD. 2026-09-11 23:37. ***\nCited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911g_closure/SCORES.txt  (frozen 39-row probe, one instrument for every leg)\n\n    LEG            was      now      killed by\n    BASE                    39/39    -- CONTROL=OK, FAILING none\n    M_POPP_NONE    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPP_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPW_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_DB_P2, ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R1\n    M_NPMASK       32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_SBIT_NONE    32/32    38/39    ARM_LDM_S_P2\n    RESTORE                          cmp-vs-SEC=CLEAN, binary=FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED\n\n**Every mutant that was a TOTAL ESCAPE now reds with NAMED rows, and BASE is clean at 39/39.**\nSeven rows closed the load side of the p/u/s/w x site product: `ARM_LDM_IB_P2` (p=1,u=1),\n`ARM_LDM_DA_P2` (u=0), `ARM_LDM_DB_P2` (p=1,u=0), `ARM_LDM_S_P2` (s=1), and the three-assertion\n`ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_*` leg.  **The multi leg is the bargain: ONE session, THREE assertions, closing\nw_bit x residue-2 AND register-list-length together.**  It had to sit at residue 2 -- at the\nexisting `WB_UNAL2` base (0x20001/0x20005) the low bits are `01`, where `~1` and `~3` AGREE, so\nthat row is structurally blind to a w_bit-conditioned weakening.\n\n*** THREE OF THE SEVEN NEW ROWS KILL NOTHING IN THIS TABLE, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN\nGLOSSED: `ARM_LDM_DA_P2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_WB`. ***  Their channels are\nreal but no BUILT mutant varies them -- `DA_P2` exists for the `u_bit=0` cell, which the pass-C\nseat ENUMERATED and explicitly did NOT build (*\"derived (same legs)\"*).  A condemning prediction\ngets built, so `M_POPU_WEAK` -- `addr & (u_bit ? 0xfffffffc : 0xfffffffe)` -- WAS BUILT AND\nMEASURED: **37/39, FAILING ARM_LDM_DA_P2 and ARM_LDM_DB_P2.**  `DA_P2` is therefore\nDISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL and the u_bit cell is closed rather than derived.\n*Six mutants built, six dead.*  The other two are secondary assertions on a leg whose primary assertion\nalready kills; they are disclosed under clause (e), not claimed as coverage.\n\nTHE REPAIR-CHAIN STATUS: passes A, B and C each found a breach (M_MASK1; M_MASK7 + M_PBITCOND;\nthen five at once).  **This diff is the CLASS CLOSURE the rule demands after two consecutive\npasses of one class -- the full enumeration in ONE diff rather than another point row -- and it\nowes its own pass.  A zero does not close the chain unless it carries its denominator; the\ndenominator is the table above plus the enumeration the pass-C seat swept (mask value, placement,\nsite, predicate x 2 sites x 4 bits x 2 strengths = 16 cells, operand, joint (p,u), reg-list\nlength, byte order, null medium).**\n\n---\n\n*** PASS C DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN. 2026-09-11 17:52. FOUR SEATS OF SIX FOUND A CLAUSE-(d)\nESCAPE; TWO DID NOT.  `#524` DOES NOT COMMIT. ***\n\n**`M_POP_PBITCOND` -- found INDEPENDENTLY by codex and grok**, the read-side counterpart of the\nmeasured `M_PBITCOND`:\n    /*  at the arm_pop() read call, fix present at BOTH sites, then:  */\n    addr & (p_bit ? 0xfffffffe : 0xfffffffc)\n\n**Predicted 32/32, a TOTAL SWEEP**, because every tested load carries p_bit=0 -- established by\nDECODING the probe's literal instruction words (`0xe8900002`, `0xe8b00002`, `0xe8b00006`, all IA;\nThumb `0xbc02`), not by assertion.\n\n*** THIS IS THE `#499` SHAPE FOR THE THIRD TIME IN ONE DAY, AND THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION PREDICTED\nIT HOURS EARLIER: *\"Each pass today found a new cell; extrapolate.\"* ***  Pass A found `M_MASK1`;\npass B found `M_MASK7`/`M_PBITCOND`; pass C finds the SYMMETRIC CELL OF PASS B'S OWN REPAIR -- the\nclass closure repaired the store-side cell `M_PBITCOND` used and left the load-side open.\n\n**grok named the enumeration that was missing, in one sentence:** *\"Mask-width (~1 / ~7 / ~15) is\nCLOSED; the predicate x site product is NOT.\"*  So the owed closure is\n**{unconditional, p_bit-conditioned} x {arm_pop, arm_push}** in ONE diff, not another single cell --\nthe class-closure rule, which pass B already invoked and applied to only one axis.\n\n**AN EQUIVALENT-MUTANT TRAP INSIDE THE MUTANT, from grok:** the form must be truthy `p_bit ?`, NOT\n`p_bit == 1`.  `bdt_load` passes `iw & 0x01000000`, so `== 1` is DEAD on every ARM encoding and\nwould measure EQUIVALENT for the wrong reason.\n\n*** IT IS A PREDICTION AND IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  Both seats said so plainly --\ngrok: *\"I could not exec a scratch rebuild (WSL was blocked in this seat)... treat the 32/32 as a\npredicted sweep that still owes a clone run.\"*  Per the flagship ruling on predictions: **a\nCONDEMNING prediction gets BUILT FIRST** (a wrong condemnation costs one build; a wrong excuse ships\na hole).  Build `M_POP_PBITCOND`, score it, and if it sweeps that is **L2 -- repair in-round**.\n\nOpus 5 was fired on the same brief and is still running; agy deliberately not fired.\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 INVESTIGATION -- **CONFIRMED AT HEAD, AND THE SPLICE IS TRACED TO THE memcpy, NOT ASSERTED.  The row is right in every particular and the artifact genuinely does not exist.** ***\n\n**THE MECHANISM, read at HEAD (`cpu_arm_instr.c`, unmodified in `git status`):**\n  * `arm_pop()` `:1602-1764` and `arm_push()` `:1770-1859` each hold one running `addr = *np`, the RAW base-register value, and branch on host page residency per register.\n  * **FAST path** (page resident): `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]` -- the `>>2` truncates the low two bits, so it is **architecturally correct** for the implemented A=0/U=0 state (DDI0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190: *ignore the least significant two bits of the address*).\n  * **SLOW path** (cold): `cpu->memory_rw(..., addr, data, 4, ...)` with the **RAW, UNMASKED addr** (`:1653-1654`, `:1842-1843`).  **Traced into `memory_rw.c:728-782`: `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity, NOT word-rounded, and the transfer is a raw `memcpy` at `:770-772`/`:780-782`.  So an unaligned addr genuinely SPLICES BYTES ACROSS AN ARCHITECTURAL WORD BOUNDARY** -- confirmed at the lowest level rather than inferred.\n\n> **The same instruction, the same unaligned base, produces two different byte patterns purely according to HOST PAGE-CACHE WARMTH -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n*** AND THE WRITEBACK IS CORRECT AS IT STANDS, WHICH IS WHY THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS: *** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` uses the unmasked running addr, and **Mode 4 writeback DERIVES from unmasked Rn**.  **So the repair must mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites and NEVER at entry** -- masking at entry corrupts writeback.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT FOR EXACTLY THAT SHAPE ALREADY EXISTS:** `src/cpus/generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;` -- the code-generated fast dispatch already masks the ACCESS while leaving writeback unmasked.  *A transferred warrant, verified at the cited line.*\n\n**FOUR CALLERS, all confirmed:** `X(bdt_load)` `:1889`, `X(bdt_store)` `:1921`, and Thumb PUSH/POP at `cpu_arm.c:1670`/`:1677`.  **Thumb inherits the defect and has ZERO probe coverage of any kind.**\n\n=== NO ARTIFACT EXISTS.  SEARCHED BY THE DEFECT'S VOCABULARY, NEVER BY GUESSING A FILENAME ===\n*(the brief warned against filename invention because I did it twice today; the search was done by function names and mnemonics instead)*\n  * filename patterns `*armbdt*`, `*bdt*`, `*block*transfer*`, `*blockdata*`: **0 hits**\n  * **127** artifact directories: none for this item\n  * content-grep of all **93** `regress/*.py` for LDM/STM mnemonics: **2 files**, both testing something else\n  * `bdt_load|bdt_store|arm_pop|arm_push`: **1 hit**, a comment\n  * `CHANGELOG.md`: **0** occurrences -- **no round has ever shipped against it**\n\n**GATE 14's 358 IS CORRECT AND CURRENT**, confirmed two independent ways -- `gate_arm.sh:561-563`'s comment AND commit `6014b7e`'s message recording the gate's own run output.  *(The carrier records this figure was stale once at 261, so both were treated as claims.)*  **And it does not cover this defect under any name:**\n  * `arm_writeback_probe.py` scopes itself explicitly to *the single-register path* -- a different generator entirely;\n  * `arm_endian_probe.py` DOES reach `arm_pop`/`arm_push`, but only on byte order and **with a word-aligned base (`0x10000`), where masking is a no-op** -- so it cannot discriminate this defect whichever path a row takes;\n  * measured corroboration: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 identical** on `arm_writeback_probe.py`, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback**;\n  * and the regress adjudication of `6014b7e` states it plainly -- *gate_arm passed 16/16 WITH armbdt PRESENT.*\n\n*** THE MANDATORY ROW: a WRITEBACK row threading an unaligned base through a W=1 transfer and asserting the base register equals the UNMASKED arithmetic result. ***  It is the row `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to, and **the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant.**\n\n**CITATION DRIFT, noted rather than silently fixed:** the 2026-08-19 entries cite `:1563`/`:1752`; HEAD has the same code at `:1653`/`:1842` -- about 90 lines, from unrelated intervening edits.  The mechanism is unchanged; only the numbers moved.\n\n**STILL LIVE AT HEAD**, checked from source rather than from the row: `git log -10` on that file shows the recent touches are all unrelated to the `memory_rw` calls.\n\n*Investigated by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  The work is of the right standard for a READING task, which is the half of that row's claim this supports; it says nothing about measuring equivalence.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat, independent transport).  **THE WITNESS EXISTS.  RUNG 3, UNMODIFIED testarm, 17/25 PRE-FIX AND 25/25 POST-FIX -- after three weeks in which this row's own record said the artifact genuinely did not exist.** ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS UNCHANGED AND IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN DERIVED.**  The fast path (`p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`) truncates the low two bits and is architecturally CORRECT per DDI 0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190; the slow path passes the **RAW, UNMASKED `addr`** to `memory_rw`, whose `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity and whose transfer is a raw `memcpy`.  **So the identical instruction at the identical unaligned base yields DIFFERENT BYTES according to host page-cache warmth -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n=== THE WITNESS, AND IT CLEARS EVERY LADDER CLAUSE ===\n**Rung 3, cold interactive debugger, on the UNMODIFIED in-tree `testarm`** (`gxemul -V -A -T -E testarm -M 64`) driven through a pty.  **No `device_add`, no custom machine -- which is the laundering clause, and it is met by construction rather than by assertion.**\n\n*** THE PAGE-CACHE CONDITIONING IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A WITNESS AT ALL, and it turns on a fact this project got BACKWARDS once and had two shipped rounds leaning on the wrong version: *** `put b` writes byte-wise with `CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS`, populating physical RAM **without** updating the translation tables, so `host_load`/`host_store` stay NULL -- the COLD seed; `put w` writes words with `CACHE_DATA` and **WARMS** the host page -- the WARM seed.  *The carrier records `put w` WARMS and `put b` does not; the witness uses exactly that asymmetry as its instrument.*\n\n**AND IT VERIFIES THE PATH RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT:** before every transfer the probe runs `tlbdump 0,r` to confirm the target is genuinely ABSENT from the host cache on a cold run and RESIDENT on a warm one.  **That is the row establishing which path each run took -- without it the core comparison proves nothing**, which is the one thing the brief insisted on.\n\n=== SCORES, and the artifact for every one of them is named ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` sentinel present, each score read from its own `probe_<name>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix applied) | **25/25** | clean |\n| `HEAD` (committed, pre-fix) | **17/25** | all four cold-data rows AND all four divergence checks red -- *test-first satisfied: the symptom is shown on the COMMITTED build and goes green post-fix with the same script* |\n| `M_ENTRY` (mask at entry) | **20/25** | *** KILLED, AND KILLED BY THE WRITEBACK ROWS SPECIFICALLY *** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` (one site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` (other site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (identity) | **6/25** | 19 rows red -- the rows genuinely observe block transfer, discharging clause (c) |\n| `RESTORED` | **17/25** | *** IDENTICAL TO `HEAD`, which is the clean-restore proof: the clone came back to the committed baseline exactly. *** |\n\n*** `M_ENTRY` IS THE VINDICATION OF THE MANDATORY ROW. ***  The brief said the writeback row *'is the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant'*, and predicted `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to it -- which had already been measured: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 IDENTICAL** on that probe, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback.**  The new instrument kills it 20/25.  *A row specified from the defect's own control flow, before any table ran, and it is the row that carries the whole discrimination.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `PRETABLE.out` written **06:46:22**, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` **06:50:06** -- 3m44s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.\n\n=== THE FIX SHAPE IS CONFIRMED, AND THE TRAP WITH IT ===\n**Mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites** -- `addr & ~3` at `cpu_arm_instr.c:1653` and `:1842`.  **NEVER at entry:** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` (`:1715`, `:1858`) uses the unmasked running `addr`, and Mode 4 writeback derives from unmasked `Rn`, so an entry mask corrupts writeback -- which is precisely what `M_ENTRY` measures.  In-tree precedent verified at the cited line: `generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;`, masking the ACCESS while leaving writeback alone.\n\n*** HOUSE RULES OBSERVED, VERIFIED BY ME RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE REPORT: `git status` shows `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c` UNMODIFIED in GXEMUL-SEC. ***  The seat worked in `/tmp/gxarmbdt_clone` and wrote only into `regress/artifacts/armbdt/`, which is the measuring-seat rule met exactly.  The live `/tmp/gxop519p2` tail was not touched.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is SPECIFIED AND MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  What remains is the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass-2 panel on the real diff, and the gates -- none of which could be done in this window, because the `#519` regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles.  *Deliberately left rather than rushed: a measurement taken against a loaded host is the false-FAIL this project has already paid for once.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#524`, MASKING THE ACCESS AND NOT THE RUNNING addr** ***\n\n**Round number `#524`, chosen after checking: `#523` is STAKED by `regress/artifacts/wdcerrfamily/codex_design523_20260911.txt` even though it appears in no CHANGELOG entry, source file or commit.**  *An artifact filename is a claim on a number, and taking a staked one would have collided with a round already in design.*\n\n**THE EDIT, both sites, `uint32_t addr` masked with a hex literal to match the file's own idiom (`addr & 0xfff`, `addr &= 0xffc`):**\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_pop()  : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_READ, ...)\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_push() : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_WRITE, ...)\n\n**THE TWO WRITEBACK SITES WERE VERIFIED UNTOUCHED AFTER THE EDIT** -- `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` still reads the bare unmasked running value at both.  *That is the whole discrimination: an entry-mask scores 20/25 on this round's detector and **22/22 -- identical to a correct build -- on the pre-existing `arm_writeback_probe.py`.*  The comment records that measurement at the site, so a future edit tempted to hoist the mask meets the evidence rather than the rule.\n\n*Other `memory_rw(..., addr, ...)` calls in this file (the single-register load/store paths and the instruction fetch) were left alone -- checked by grep after the edit: 7 remain bare and none is on the block-data-transfer path.*\n\n**PROPAGATION, and it is deliberately PARTIAL:**\n  * `est/` -- **DONE and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL by `cmp`.**  It was confirmed equal to SEC@HEAD BEFORE the copy, so it is genuinely a shared file rather than a silent divergence being overwritten.  *est/ is a SOURCE tree with no binary, so propagating without rebuilding creates no stale-binary trap.*\n  * `build/`, `build-asan/`, `/tmp/gxsec-build` -- **NOT propagated.**  Three codex jobs are in flight and a rebuild is CPU-heavy; **propagating into a build tree without rebuilding is exactly the trap recorded on [[xscaleinten]] this morning**, where all three trees report `FRESH` over a source that is simply the wrong source.  Recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` so a fresh session finds the debt.\n\n**STILL OWED BEFORE THIS COMMITS: propagate+rebuild the three build trees together, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  The witness and the kill table are already done (HEAD 17/25, fix 25/25, `M_ENTRY` 20/25, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 6/25, `RESTORED` = HEAD).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#524` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- THE MASKS ARE RIGHT AND 'ONLY TWO SITES' WAS WRONG.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- the two masks are locally correct, but other BDT implementations still use unrounded addresses, and the architectural justification needs qualification.*\n\n**THE MASK AND THE LITERAL ARE CORRECT.**  `addr & 0xfffffffc` clears exactly bits 1 and 0; the unsuffixed hex literal has type `unsigned int` on ordinary implementations (its value does not fit signed `int`), so the result stays unsigned and widens correctly into `memory_rw`'s `uint64_t` parameter.  *A wider host pointer does not change this.*\n\n**FOR AN ALIGNED BASE BOTH CHANGES ARE STRICT NO-OPS** -- every running-address adjustment is a multiple of four, so an initially aligned `addr` stays aligned **including across 32-bit wraparound**, and old and new arguments are identical at both call sites.\n\n=== *** MY 'ONLY TWO SITES ON THE BDT PATH' PREMISE WAS WRONG, AND THE COUNTEREXAMPLES ARE TWO HANDLERS THAT REPLACE LDM/STM OUTRIGHT *** ===\n*'yes for the generic helpers' `memory_rw` calls; no for all implementations of the BDT access rule.'*  **Verified here before filing, all three quotes read from the file:**\n  * **`netbsd_memset`** replaces STM instructions and does **`memset(page + (addr & 0xfff), 0, 128);`** at `:2127` -- raw page offset, no word rounding.  Its runtime guards check page bounds, zero source values and page availability; **none rejects an unaligned address.**\n  * **`netbsd_memcpy`** replaces LDM/STM and copies through raw `& 0xfff` source AND destination offsets (`:2274-2275`), **while its register-publication reads use `& 0xffc`** -- *two different address rules inside one handler.*  Its own comment at `:2252` already acknowledges *'the overlap and unaligned-copy divergences'*.\n  * Their matchers recognise instruction patterns **without establishing address alignment**, and the combined bodies **bypass both edited calls entirely.**\n\n**These predate the `#524` hunks, so they are FILED rather than fixed in-round** per the stopping rule -- [[armbdtcombined]].  *But they mean the round may not claim to have closed the class, only the generic helpers.*\n\n**AND MY CLASSIFICATION OF THE SEVEN REMAINING CALLS WAS WRONG IN DETAIL WHILE RIGHT IN CONCLUSION** -- the count is correct and *'none is a missed generic LDM/STM call'*, but they are not 'generated single-register' calls: `1498/1521` are SWP (already word-rounded at `:1497`), `1540/1547` are SWPB (one-byte, **low bits legitimately select the byte**), `1982/2042` are LDREX/STREX (unaligned rejected before the call), `4009` is the instruction fetch.\n\n=== THUMB: THE FIX REACHES IT, AND A DIFFERENT DEFECT DOES NOT ===\n*'Every transferred register in either helper goes through the same aligned host-word access or the newly masked slow call.  There is no Thumb-specific raw-address branch inside either transfer loop.'*  **So Thumb callers that enter these helpers receive the access fix automatically.**\n\n*Coverage unverified because `cpu_arm.c` was outside the permitted set -- my omission again, the second brief today to exclude the file its own question needed.*  The seat declined to certify the PUSH/POP call sites, whether Thumb LDMIA/STMIA use these helpers, or the reported absence of Thumb probes.\n\n> *** AND IT NAMED A SEPARATE THUMB DEFECT THAT MY PATCH DOES NOT TOUCH: the helper's PC handling CLEARS BOTH LOW PC BITS and calls the ARM pointer-update routine, while DDI0100I's Thumb POP (`ddi0100i.txt:24256`) PRESERVES THE HALFWORD ADDRESS and specifies interworking. ***  An external caller might compensate; that is not establishable from these files.  **Unchanged by `#524` and not claimed by it.**\n\n=== TWO QUALIFICATIONS TO THE ARCHITECTURAL WARRANT, both of which I had not considered ===\n  * **THE HELPERS ARE NOT RESTRICTED TO RAM.**  An unaligned address can reach DEVICE DISPATCH, and *'rounding can change a device-relative offset or even the selected mapping near a device boundary'* -- device selection uses the translated address before the RAM-copy path.\n  * **`ddi0100i.txt:3256` (A2-42) specifies additional UNPREDICTABLE behaviour for ARMv6 unaligned multiword accesses to Device or Strongly Ordered memory**, so *'the unconditional claim that all callers simply ignore those bits is too broad.'*\n\n**AND THE EDIT ROUNDS THE VIRTUAL ADDRESS, BEFORE TRANSLATION** -- `memory_rw` splits translated accesses at **1 KB** (`MRW_SPLIT_MASK == 0x3ff`) while its RAM worker uses a 4 KB offset mask for ARM -- *'the old call can touch, and fault on, a page that the corrected call never accesses.'*  **That is a real behavioural difference beyond the fix**, and it is the intended one (the architecture says ignore the low bits), but it was not in the round's stated reasoning and is recorded now rather than discovered later.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **HOLD for ONE MEASUREMENT, then repair IN-ROUND.  Grok MIS-GRADED this: it is not L3, it is CLAUSE (d).** ===\n\n*** IT IS THE CARRIER'S OWN WORKED EXAMPLE -- `#461`'s `m_align1`, a mask narrowed `& 3` -> `& 1`. The fix IS the mask, so a weakened mask is the canonical (d) form, and naming it (d) matters because **a (d) breach cannot be argued around as 'the round does not claim that'.** ***\n\n**CHECKED BY THE ADJUDICATOR, not inferred: every unaligned base in `agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` is `+1`** -- `:218` `0x20001`, `:281` `0x21001`, POP `0x22001`, PUSH `0x23005 -> 0x23001`.  **For every row `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3`, so `0xfffffffc -> 0xfffffffe` at both sites is 25/25 BY ARITHMETIC.**\n\n> **But it must still be BUILT AND SCORED, not asserted.**  Grok said plainly it had not built it, and this project's own rule is that *'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.*  **Build `M_MASK1` on the clone, score it, and when it escapes add a `+2` DATA row for LDM and one for STM**, re-run all legs, and `M_MASK1` must go red.\n\n**`+3` BUYS NOTHING ENUMERABLE and is DROPPED** -- rows are owed per ENUMERATED MUTANT: `~1` dies at `+2`, `~2` already dies at `+1`.  **Disclose residue 3 in the contract instead of rowing it.**  *That is the row-demand bound doing its job against my instinct to add more rows.*\n\n**Operative licence is L1** (nothing has shipped), not L2.\n\n=== *** THE `PRETABLE` 4/25-vs-6/25 GAP IS EXPLAINED, AND THE EXPLANATION IS A REAL DEFECT *** ===\nThe two extra survivors are **`ARM_STM_COLD_CLOBBER` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_CLOBBER`**, found by diffing the row list against the FAILING line.  **A 'neighbour not clobbered' row is an IDENTITY ROW under a medium that writes nothing** -- so `PRETABLE.out`'s claim that *every row genuinely observes block transfer* is **FALSE for those two.**  *The prediction was not merely two off; it rested on a wrong statement about the rows.*\n\n**Write `PRETABLE2.out` for the re-table -- do NOT append to `PRETABLE.out`**: this ledger already records an appended section destroying a PRETABLE's mtime, which is the artifact whose whole evidentiary value is its timestamp.\n\n**TWO L4s IN MY OWN COMMENT, BOTH APPLIED:**\n  1. *'DDI0100I says to ignore the low two bits'* was UNQUALIFIED.  **Verified at `ddi0100i.txt:7954-7959`: that holds for `CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0` AND alignment checking DISABLED; `Ubit == 1` faults on every non-word-aligned access.**  And the fork is internally inconsistent -- `cpu_arm.c:129` SETS `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` in the default control register, `:474` only PRINTS it, and **nothing consults it.**  *** THE ROW'S OWN 2026-08-29 fable5 ENTRY ALREADY SAID 'the record must NOT say unqualifiedly' -- so the comment I wrote today broke an obligation this row had already recorded. ***\n  2. *'Measured: ... `arm_writeback_probe.py` scores it 22/22'* -- **`22/22` appears in NO measurement artifact.**  Verified: it occurs only in briefs and seat answers; `probe_M_ENTRY.raw` backs the 20/25 alone.  **Restated BY CONSTRUCTION** (that probe scopes itself to the single-register path and never reaches these helpers), with the word *Measured* removed from it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLES 2 AND 3.  **THE CLAUSE-(d) BREACH IS MEASURED, CLOSED AND RE-MEASURED: M_MASK1 25/25 -> 27/29.** ***\n\n=== TABLE 2 (`killtable_20260911b/`): THE BREACH, MEASURED ===\n**`M_MASK1: 25/25` -- a TOTAL ESCAPE.**  The fix present, its mask weakened `0xfffffffc` -> `0xfffffffe`: the carrier's own `#461 m_align1` shape (`& 3` -> `& 1`), and **the canonical clause-(d) form for this round because the fix IS the mask.**\n\n*Fable predicted it by arithmetic -- every unaligned base the probe drove was `+1`, and `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3` -- and ruled that it be BUILT anyway, because this project's rule is that 'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.  It was right to insist: the prediction was correct, and a round that had accepted it unmeasured would have shipped a floor claim it had not tested.*\n\n*** AND TABLE 1's NUMBERS REPRODUCED EXACTLY ON THE REPAIRED DRIVER -- 7 of 7 legs match. ***  **So table 1's MEASUREMENT was sound even though its DRIVER was defective**; what [[killtablebaseline]] cost was reproducibility, not correctness.  *That is worth knowing before discarding anything from it, and it is the opposite of what a defective-driver finding usually implies.*\n\n=== TABLE 3 (`killtable_20260911c/`): THE CLOSURE, RE-MEASURED ===\n\n| leg | score | rows it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (the fix) | **29/29** | none |\n| `HEAD` | 19/29 | 10 -- the defect rows, including BOTH new `+2` rows |\n| **`M_MASK1`** | **27/29** | *** exactly 2: `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` *** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 24/29 | 5 -- **ALL FIVE WRITEBACK ROWS AND NOTHING ELSE** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the STM-side rows |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the LDM-side MIRROR of those |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/29 | 21, including BOTH control rows |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/29 | = `HEAD`, the clean-restore proof (now against GIT) |\n\n> *** EVERY LEG DIES ON A DISTINCT, SEMANTICALLY CORRECT SET, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH MORE THAN THE SCORES. ***  `M_ENTRY` fails ONLY writeback rows; `M_POP_ONLY` and `M_PUSH_ONLY` fail mirror-image sets; `M_MASK1` fails ONLY the two rows written for it.  *Compare `wdcaltdigin`, where four legs all scored 14/16 and only the row identity distinguished them -- here the row identity and the score agree, which is what a well-separated instrument looks like.*\n\n**THE CLOSURE IS TIGHT, NOT OVER-ROWED: `M_MASK1` fails exactly TWO rows -- the minimum.**  *Rows are owed per enumerated mutant, and two mutant-killing rows plus their two PATH controls is what the enumeration demanded.*\n\n=== THE PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT TO THE BYTE, WHICH IS THE CHANNEL RULE WORKING ===\n`PRETABLE2.out` named, before the run, the observable each new row would show under `M_MASK1`:\n  * `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x77881122`, **measured `0x77881122`**;\n  * `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x33440000`, **measured `0x33440000`**.\n\n*The channel line requires naming the mutant a row kills AND the observable that differs under it, at writing time, before any table runs.  Both were named and both were exact.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE2.out` 12:15:13, first table run 12:16:31 -- **78 s earlier**.  Written as a SEPARATE FILE rather than appended to `PRETABLE.out`, because this ledger records an append destroying a PRETABLE's mtime once, and the mtime is the artifact's entire evidentiary value.\n\n*** A PREDICTION OF MINE THAT MISSED, recorded because it is the same class I spent today cataloguing: I predicted `BASE 30/30` and the measurement is `29/29`. ***  I derived 30 from `grep -c 'record('` -- **a CORRELATE of the scored-row count, not the count itself** (one `record()` call is not scored).  *The deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, in my own pre-table prediction, on the same day I recorded it against three other censuses.  The error is harmless here because predictions and measurements are in separate sections and every citation names `SCORES.txt` -- which is exactly why they are kept apart.*\n\n**AND THUMB IS COVERED AFTER ALL, which settles part of an open question:** the failing-row lists name `THUMB_POP_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_PUSH_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_POP_COLD_DATA` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA` -- so the instrument does exercise the Thumb callers, and `M_ENTRY` kills the Thumb writeback rows alongside the ARM ones.  *The `#524` pass-2 seat could not verify this because `cpu_arm.c` was outside its permitted set; the table settles it from the other direction.*\n\n**FLOOR STATUS FOR `#524`: (a) HEAD red, (b) reinstatement dies, (c) null medium 8/29, (d) `M_MASK1` KILLED at 27/29.  The breach is closed in-round under L1** -- nothing had shipped, so this is pre-ship construction rather than an L2 repair.  **Residue 3 disclosed in the contract rather than rowed**, because `~1` dies at `+2` and `~2` already dies at `+1`, so a `+3` row kills no enumerated mutant.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 4 (`killtable_20260911d/`).  **TABLE 4: THE CLASS CLOSURE WORKS AND **BASE IS RED ON ONE OF MY OWN NEW ROWS** -- ROUND HELD.** ***\n\n=== THE REPAIR-CHAIN PASS FOUND A FLOOR BREACH AND REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN ===\n*VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH FOUND; the repair correctly detects `M_MASK1` but does not close the repair chain.*  **Two more clean-sweep candidates against the 29-row composite, both STATIC DEDUCTIONS rather than measured -- which is why each became a leg:**\n  * **`M_MASK7` (`& ~7`, OVER-alignment):** every base the instrument drove -- `0x10000`, `0x2000{1,2}`, `0x2100{1,2}` -- sits in the **FIRST WORD of its 8-byte block, where `~3` and `~7` AGREE.**  *And the two-register LDM does not rescue it: it asserts only `r0` writeback, never either loaded register.*\n  * **`M_PBITCOND` (a CONDITIONAL weakening on an EXISTING dominating predicate):** mask `~1` when `p_bit`, `~3` otherwise, at the STORE site only.  ARM STM is IA (`p_bit=0`, correct mask); Thumb PUSH is DB (`p_bit=1`, weakened) **but ran at exactly ONE residue, `+1`, where `~1` and `~3` agree.**  *The predicate axis was exercised at a single point.*\n\n**TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FOUND MASK-WEAKENING BREACHES, so the next repair owed CLASS CLOSURE rather than a third point fix.**  Space from the helpers' own inputs: **{site} x {residue} x {word position in the 8-byte block} x {p_bit}**.  Three uncovered cells closed in ONE diff.\n\n=== AND BOTH NEW MUTANTS DIED, ON THE CELLS WRITTEN FOR THEM ===\n\n| leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|\n| `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | its two P2 rows **plus both new ones** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | the writeback rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 21+ rows incl. both controls |\n| `HEAD` / `RESTORED` | 19/32 | identical -- clean restore against GIT |\n\n*** BUT `BASE: 31/32`.  THE FIX ITSELF FAILS `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` -- one of the rows I ADDED THIS PASS -- and a red BASE row means the round DOES NOT SHIP. ***\n\n**The row reports `w0=0x00000000 w1=0x00000000` on the FIXED build.**  Expected: base `0x21005` masks to `0x21004`, so the word lands in `PAGE_STM+4`.  **Nothing landed in either word.**\n\n> **AND I COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE BY REASONING.**  Under HEAD the same row reports `w1=0x00000011`, which implies the stored bytes ENDED at `0x21004` -- i.e. a base of `0x21001`, not the `0x21005` my literal sets.  That contradicts `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA`, which calibrates correctly at base `0x21001` giving `0x22334400`.  *Two readings of the same mechanism that cannot both be right.*\n\n**SO I STOPPED REASONING AND INSTRUMENTED**, which is this project's own rule when a value is surprising: a diagnostic rebuilds BASE and **dumps a WIDE WINDOW around `PAGE_STM`, so the bytes can be LOCATED rather than guessed at.**  *I had been deriving where the store went without looking at where it actually is.*\n\n**TWO POSSIBILITIES, AND WHICH ONE IS NOT YET KNOWN:** my row is mis-specified -- most likely, since every other BASE row passes including `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2`, which uses the same mechanism at `+2` -- or it has found something real about the store path at bit-2-set addresses.  **Recorded as open rather than resolved in either direction.**\n\n*Also fixed this pass, from the panel: the mutator now emits **`0xfffffffc`, the EXACT shipped literal**, so `BASE` measures the working-tree text rather than a semantic equivalent -- grok raised that the table had not measured what would be committed.*\n\n*** DISCLOSED: THE PATH ROWS ARE FIXTURE CHECKS, NOT MUTATION DISCRIMINATORS, AND THE REPAIR PASS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE ONES I ADDED. ***  Every `in_tlb_*` is captured BEFORE `run_prog`, so no helper-local mutation of a mask, operand or predicate can retroactively change it.  They establish the run was cold -- which the core comparison needs -- and discriminate NO enumerated mutant.  Kept for that purpose, disclosed rather than counted toward the floor.\n\n*And my `'a +3 row buys no enumerated mutant'` was TOO BROAD as a claim about the COMPOSITE: it holds within a fixed cell, but the missing distinction is the PREDICATE/RESIDUE COMBINATION, and residue 3 is one possible witness among several rather than one that buys nothing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 5 (`killtable_20260911e/`).  **THE RED BASE ROW WAS A DEFECT IN THE WITNESS, NOT THE FIX -- AND IT HAD CORRUPTED 8 OF 10 LEGS.  BASE IS NOW 32/32 AND THE FLOOR IS CLEAN.** ***\n\n=== HOW IT WAS FOUND, INCLUDING THE PART I DID BADLY ===\nTable 4 reported `BASE 31/32` -- the FIX failing `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`.  **A red BASE row blocks the round, so it was diagnosed rather than explained away.**\n\n*** I FIRST TRIED TO REASON IT OUT FROM THE BYTE PATTERNS AND COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE. ***  Under HEAD the row implied the store ended at `0x21004` -- a base of `0x21001` -- while `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA` calibrates correctly at that same base.  **Two readings of one mechanism that cannot both be true**, and I spent four rounds of inference on it before instrumenting.\n\n**The dump settled it in ONE run:**\n    r0 = 0x21005                                 <- the literal loaded fine\n    0x00021000  00000000 44332211 00000000 ...   <- the store LANDED at 0x21004\n\n> **THE STORE WAS CORRECT ALL ALONG.  THE READ WAS WRONG.**\n\n=== THE INSTRUMENT DEFECT, MEASURED DIRECTLY ===\n    dump from an ALIGNED start:\n      0x00021000  00000000 44332211\n    dump from an UNALIGNED start -- what `read_mem32` actually issued:\n      0x00021000           44332211 00000000\n                  ^^^^^^^^ the leading group is BLANK-PADDED\n\n`read_mem32()` searched for the line labelled `(addr & ~0xF)` -- **which matches in BOTH cases** -- then indexed `line[12:48].split()` by `(addr & 0xF) // 4`.  **When the dump starts unaligned the leading groups are ABSENT and `.split()` collapses the padding, so every index shifts and `parts[idx]` silently returns a NEIGHBOURING WORD.**\n\n    read_mem32(0x21004) -> parts == ['44332211', '00000000'], idx 1 -> 0\n\n*This is the padded-column trap this project already records, in a new place: the label matched, so every check that looked for the right line passed, and the CONTENT came from the wrong column.*\n\n**REPAIR: dump from `(addr & ~0xF)` for 16 bytes, so all four groups are always present -- which is what the index arithmetic already assumed.  One line.**\n\n*** SCOPE, AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE ONE RED ROW: 8 OF 10 LEGS CHANGED VALUE WHEN THE INSTRUMENT WAS REPAIRED. ***\n\n| leg | table 4 | table 5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` | 31/32 | **32/32** |\n| `HEAD` | 19/32 | **17/32** -- the pre-fix build was failing MORE rows than reported |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/32 | 17/32 (= HEAD) |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | 27/32 |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | 29/32 |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | 31/32 |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 25/32 | 23/32 |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 25/32 | 26/32 |\n| `M_MASK7`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | unchanged | unchanged |\n\n**The defect was in the probe AS ORIGINALLY BUILT**, so it affected every `read_mem32` call at a nonzero 16-byte offset -- the STM and Thumb PUSH clobber rows -- across tables 1 through 4.  **Every number those rows produced before this table is superseded.**  *Grepped: no other probe in the tree shares the shape, so the blast radius is this round's instrument alone.*\n\n=== THE FLOOR, NOW CLEAN ===\n\n| clause | leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| -- | `BASE` | **32/32** | **nothing -- clean** |\n| (a) pre-fix red | `HEAD` | 17/32 | the defect rows |\n| (b) reinstatement | `RESTORED` | 17/32 | = HEAD exactly |\n| (c) null medium | `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 24 rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK1` | 29/32 | the three `P2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | the two `BIT2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_PBITCOND` | 31/32 | **`THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` alone -- the minimum** |\n| de-escalation | `M_ENTRY` | 27/32 | **all five writeback rows, nothing else** |\n\n**Every mutant dies on exactly the cells written for it, and `M_PBITCOND` on precisely one.**  *The class closure holds under a correct instrument, which is the thing table 4 could not establish.*\n\n*A small one of mine, recorded because I spent today cataloguing this class: my own shell check for a clean BASE tested `[ -z \"$B\" ]`, but the probe prints `FAILING: none` when clean -- a NON-EMPTY string -- so the check took its else branch and printed 'BASE still red on: none'.  The score `32/32` is what settled it.  A check that reports the opposite of the truth on a green run is the same family as the ones I filed against other people's scripts today.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROMOTED TO regress/ AND WIRED INTO gate_arm.sh -- 32/32 AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY. ***\n\n**This discharges the obligation the round could not previously claim.**  Fable's adjudication found that *neither of today's probes is under `regress/` proper, so 'shipped a detector with the fix' is not yet true of either round.*  For `#524` it is true now.\n\n=== THE PROMOTION IS A COPY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ===\n`regress/artifacts/armbdt/agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` **stays in place as the AS-RUN record of tables 1-5**; `regress/arm_bdt_probe.py` is the copy that evolves and that the gate executes.  *Editing the artifact copy would falsify what those tables actually measured* -- the same reason the repaired kill-table driver was written as a new file rather than over the old one.\n\n**The promoted copy differs only by two PRINT-ONLY lines (`ARMBDT_CONTROL=`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=`, matching the gate idiom the other ARM probes use) and a header note.  Verified: the `record()` count is unchanged, so no row was added or lost in the copy.**  Its header also carries the `read_mem32` alignment warning, so the next probe copied from it does not inherit [[probereadalign]].\n\n=== MEASURED AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY, NOT THE SCRATCH CLONE ===\n`$PMAX` is `build/gxemul`.  After the propagate+rebuild, the promoted probe run directly against it returns **`SCORE 32/32`, `ARMBDT_CONTROL=OK`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=32/32`, `FAILING: none`**.  *A pre-check worth the thirty seconds: wiring the probe in BEFORE the rebuild would have run it against a pre-fix binary and produced a red gate that meant nothing about the fix.*\n\n=== THE GATE SECTION, AND WHY THE DENOMINATOR IS A LITERAL ===\nFollows `arm_writeback_probe.py`'s shape exactly: run -> `gate_skip` if no result line -> print the ok/FAIL rows -> check the control -> **`check \"bdt rows run\" \"$bwant\" 32`** -> check correct == run.\n\n> **The denominator is pinned to a LITERAL deliberately.  The probe grew 25 -> 29 -> 32 rows across three kill tables as clause-(d) mutants were closed, and a gate that trusts the probe's own denominator cannot tell a probe that LOST rows from one that never had them.**\n\nThe section also records all five measured-dead mutants with their scores and the row family that kills each (`M_ENTRY` 27/32 writeback rows, `M_MASK1` 29/32 the `+2` rows, `M_MASK7` 30/32 the bit-2 rows, `M_PBITCOND` 31/32 Thumb PUSH `+2`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 8/32), **so a future reader meets the evidence at the gate rather than having to find the artifacts.**\n\n*Targeted gate run in flight.  Nothing committed; the owner's hold stands until it is green.*",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:9418:     "note": "8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.\n\n*** Q0 ANSWERED UNANIMOUSLY: NO MERGES. FIVE SEPARATE ROUNDS. ***  agy: 'split pitflip, pitlatch, pitlsb and isaorder into separate candidate rounds'. glm: 'none merge under the one-fix-design test'. deepseek: 'no merges'. minimax sequenced them rather than combining. **This OVERRULES the measure seat's own suggestion that pitflip was 'the natural companion' to i8253zero** -- it argued same-device and same-semantic-class (counter-load byte routing), and the panel applied the remaining prongs and said no. The measuring seat found the defects; the panel scoped them, and the two jobs came apart cleanly.\n\nWHAT IS FIRST -- pitlatch, 3 to 1:\n  pitlatch    deepseek, glm, minimax. glm's clause is the decisive one: 'a real guest reaches it in microtime()'. That is this project's ordering rule applied exactly -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- and pitlatch is the only one of the five demonstrated to be on a path a shipped guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: THIRD by the panel's most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row's main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:12201:     "note": "*** VERDICT: KEEP OPEN -- and it ANSWERED the question the hold was written to ask. I asked for the smallest edit that survives every existing row and dies under a composed one, saying that if none could be named the row should close. **One was named AND MEASURED: a ONE-IDENTIFIER change sweeps BOTH offline detectors clean at 79/79 and dies under a composed row.** *** THE MUTANT: `dev_m8820x.c:108`, `cmmu[d->cmmu_nr]->reg` -> `cmmu[0]->reg`. Denominators self-reported by the harnesses: diff_m8820x 46 rows + diff_m8invread 33 rows = 79, with two deliberate @@SELFCHECK@@ FAIL lines in each file as negative controls, so a raw grep of 2 IS the green baseline. *** THE CONTROLS ARE WHAT MAKE THIS EVIDENCE RATHER THAN AN ASSERTION, and they show the blindness is STRUCTURAL: *** M3 (`sar = 0`) reddens D1/D3, proving the harness CAN see a consumer-side SAR defect; M4 (drop the SAR store) reddens E7/E8/G6/G7, proving it CAN see a producer-side one. **Both ends are covered; only the SEAM is blind** -- because every SCR-flush row runs after `fresh()` (which memsets the device, so cpu_nr = cmmu_nr = 0) and issues from cpu0, and at (0,0) every candidate base for `regs` coincides. **M1 IS A LIVE-DEFECT CLASS ON THE SHIPPED RIG, NOT A SHAPE PIN**, and the seat verified the load-bearing fact from source rather than trusting a harness comment: `dev_luna88k.c:926-957` registers BOTH CMMUs on every boot, and `dev_m8820x.c:542-543` maps `addr2 & 1` -> cmmu_nr, so cmmu_nr = 1 is guest-addressable on every luna88k boot -- with the data CMMU taking 318,457 of 613,342 SAR writes (51.9%%). Under M1 **half the machine's PATC flushes never dispatch at all**, which is the silently-under-invalidating TLB the file's own comment calls 'not a cosmetic gap'. (M2, the `(cpu, d)` swap, is by contrast a shape pin: the booting rig is single-CPU.) *** THE COMPOSED ROW IS BUILT AND MEASURED -- THREE ROWS, EACH WITH ITS OWN KILL, NONE DECORATION: *** K1 (matching entry dropped) reddens under M1/M1b; K2 (non-matching entry retained) under M5; K3 (flush from a NON-OWNER cpu) under M2. **The two axes are independent: K1 does not catch M2 and K3 does not catch M1.** The original filing asked for 'cmmu_nr 1 AND from a non-owner CPU'; the measurement shows both halves are load-bearing and neither subsumes the other -- sharper than the filing knew. Goes in diff_m8invread.c, which has the two-cpu/two-cmmu fixture; ~20 lines; prototype committed to the artifact dir. PREMISE AUDIT: codex's 2026-08-16 premises hold, with one INCOMPLETE -- diff_m8invread also checks the STORE (G6/G7 assert the value lands in the right cmmu), rows that postdate the filing, so the producer side is better covered than the filing says. L4 FOUND IN PASSING AND FIXED: `diff_m8invread.c:639` cited `dev_luna88k.c:1001-1004` for the both-CMMUs-every-boot claim; that range is an INTERRUPT-TEMPLATE LOOP. Claim true, citation ~70 lines off; corrected to `:926-957 (add_cmmu_for_cpu)`. Verified by reading before the edit. RANKING: coverage debt on a closed round whose shipped code is correct -- below live defects per the severity rule. But it is now a 20-line row spec with three measured kills rather than a paragraph.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:12382:     "note": "AUTHOR SELF-REVIEW, badge A -- a filing, not a reviewed stage.  Section H fails a stage that proceeded short, but reads a HELD marker as 'waiting', and waiting is indistinguishable from forgotten once the round scrolls away.  Three rules: a short post-cutoff stage must be HELD or grandfathered; every HELD marker's row must be NAMED IN THE QUEUE; a closed row that changed code with no regress entry must be queued.  Five negative controls, one per rule plus baseline, each asserted on the RIGHT MESSAGE and refusing to score a traceback as a working rule -- 5 of 5 behaved.  ON ITS FIRST RUN it found three closed rows owing a regression review, and the split mattered: 2458cfb IS R5 and the batched brief's scope table names it, so b47/b27 were reviewed and had only lost the ATTRIBUTION; 9494c6a (m8online) is in no scope table -- R7 is #433 -- so it is genuinely unreviewed and queued.  I HAD ASSERTED ALL THREE WERE COVERED, from the brief's 'R1-R9' title read as a date range.  One blanket entry would have rubber-stamped an unreviewed commit -- the exact failure the file exists to prevent, by its own author.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:12424:     "note": "RECORDS / PIPELINE CLUSTER TRIAGE: nine rows about the bookkeeping itself. The brief carried an explicit caution drawn from earlier the same night -- 'a panel voted 5-0 to drop a row as already fixed and every seat was reading the SAME STALE SENTENCE; if a row here claims something is already done, treat that claim as the thing most in need of checking'.\n\n*** ONE SEAT ACTED ON THAT CAUTION AND IT PAID IMMEDIATELY. ***  grok opened files AGAINST the brief's own 'do not plan to read files' line, disclosed that it was doing so, and gave the reason: the 'already shipped / already covered' claims 'are the ones a stale hold can launder'. Its FIRST reported observation was not about any row -- it was 'Checkpoint is stale versus git log'. TRUE: CHECKPOINT.md named 5bd2ffa while HEAD was 9bf3be3. That file carries its own staleness rule and nothing enforced it; it had already drifted once tonight by six commits. Now checked mechanically (tools/pipeline/check_checkpoint.py, precommit section M, SOFT because the file is untracked and outside the git root). A seat that breaks the brief's letter to serve its purpose, and says so, is doing the job.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- a clean two-way split:\n  floodclass  agy ('the only guest-reachable defect with host-harm potential') and minimax, which scoped it EXACTLY right: 'a DETECTOR round on WHICH OF THE 543 CALLS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE'. That is precisely the gap the census declared and declined to fill -- it does no reachability analysis and says so on every run. NOTE agy's ranking rests on a reachability claim STRONGER than the census itself makes; the census's numbers are bounds, not defect counts.\n  smdatefmt   glm ('the only live measured false pass that one line closes') and deepseek. grok's reading refines it: the severity is a GATE BLIND-SPOT, not guest data -- the expiry check is a single string compare with no format predicate, so a missing date or an unpadded 2026-8-1 never expires.\n\nDROP: ledgerwitness -- THREE seats (glm, deepseek, minimax).\n\ngrok also declined to rank ieeeupgrade highly with a distinction worth keeping: it is 'the louder STORY (largest differential, weakest control, far-future date) but it is NOT a false pass of a check -- the 2099 date is doing what it was written to do'. A loud story and a live defect are different things.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 22.8 KB, grok 11.9 KB, kimi 46.1 KB, glm 10.7 KB, deepseek 4.8 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY -- the seat check identified it arithmetically this time: '16694b = brief 16006b + 688b'. Eighth consecutive wall.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: No seat prioritised or dropped it. Largely delivered: sections I and I2 now enforce both halves -- that a held stage is named, and that the queue's summary of held stages is current.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:12507:     "outcome_note": "The claim that tracking by copy and section J delivered this row is confirmed by fable5 assess 2026-08-22 and its two negative controls. [graded by codex gpt-6-astra max, n4, 2026-09-19]"
