HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:14688:     "note": "RECORDS / PIPELINE CLUSTER TRIAGE: nine rows about the bookkeeping itself. The brief carried an explicit caution drawn from earlier the same night -- 'a panel voted 5-0 to drop a row as already fixed and every seat was reading the SAME STALE SENTENCE; if a row here claims something is already done, treat that claim as the thing most in need of checking'.\n\n*** ONE SEAT ACTED ON THAT CAUTION AND IT PAID IMMEDIATELY. ***  grok opened files AGAINST the brief's own 'do not plan to read files' line, disclosed that it was doing so, and gave the reason: the 'already shipped / already covered' claims 'are the ones a stale hold can launder'. Its FIRST reported observation was not about any row -- it was 'Checkpoint is stale versus git log'. TRUE: CHECKPOINT.md named 5bd2ffa while HEAD was 9bf3be3. That file carries its own staleness rule and nothing enforced it; it had already drifted once tonight by six commits. Now checked mechanically (tools/pipeline/check_checkpoint.py, precommit section M, SOFT because the file is untracked and outside the git root). A seat that breaks the brief's letter to serve its purpose, and says so, is doing the job.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- a clean two-way split:\n  floodclass  agy ('the only guest-reachable defect with host-harm potential') and minimax, which scoped it EXACTLY right: 'a DETECTOR round on WHICH OF THE 543 CALLS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE'. That is precisely the gap the census declared and declined to fill -- it does no reachability analysis and says so on every run. NOTE agy's ranking rests on a reachability claim STRONGER than the census itself makes; the census's numbers are bounds, not defect counts.\n  smdatefmt   glm ('the only live measured false pass that one line closes') and deepseek. grok's reading refines it: the severity is a GATE BLIND-SPOT, not guest data -- the expiry check is a single string compare with no format predicate, so a missing date or an unpadded 2026-8-1 never expires.\n\nDROP: ledgerwitness -- THREE seats (glm, deepseek, minimax).\n\ngrok also declined to rank ieeeupgrade highly with a distinction worth keeping: it is 'the louder STORY (largest differential, weakest control, far-future date) but it is NOT a false pass of a check -- the 2099 date is doing what it was written to do'. A loud story and a live defect are different things.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 22.8 KB, grok 11.9 KB, kimi 46.1 KB, glm 10.7 KB, deepseek 4.8 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY -- the seat check identified it arithmetically this time: '16694b = brief 16006b + 688b'. Eighth consecutive wall.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: LEAD CANDIDATE, two seats: 'the only live measured false pass that one line closes'. grok refined the severity -- a GATE BLIND-SPOT, not guest data: the expiry check is a single string compare with no format predicate, so a missing date or an unpadded `2026-8-1` never expires and still satisfies the coverage check.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:14788:     "note": "RECORDS / PIPELINE CLUSTER TRIAGE: nine rows about the bookkeeping itself. The brief carried an explicit caution drawn from earlier the same night -- 'a panel voted 5-0 to drop a row as already fixed and every seat was reading the SAME STALE SENTENCE; if a row here claims something is already done, treat that claim as the thing most in need of checking'.\n\n*** ONE SEAT ACTED ON THAT CAUTION AND IT PAID IMMEDIATELY. ***  grok opened files AGAINST the brief's own 'do not plan to read files' line, disclosed that it was doing so, and gave the reason: the 'already shipped / already covered' claims 'are the ones a stale hold can launder'. Its FIRST reported observation was not about any row -- it was 'Checkpoint is stale versus git log'. TRUE: CHECKPOINT.md named 5bd2ffa while HEAD was 9bf3be3. That file carries its own staleness rule and nothing enforced it; it had already drifted once tonight by six commits. Now checked mechanically (tools/pipeline/check_checkpoint.py, precommit section M, SOFT because the file is untracked and outside the git root). A seat that breaks the brief's letter to serve its purpose, and says so, is doing the job.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- a clean two-way split:\n  floodclass  agy ('the only guest-reachable defect with host-harm potential') and minimax, which scoped it EXACTLY right: 'a DETECTOR round on WHICH OF THE 543 CALLS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE'. That is precisely the gap the census declared and declined to fill -- it does no reachability analysis and says so on every run. NOTE agy's ranking rests on a reachability claim STRONGER than the census itself makes; the census's numbers are bounds, not defect counts.\n  smdatefmt   glm ('the only live measured false pass that one line closes') and deepseek. grok's reading refines it: the severity is a GATE BLIND-SPOT, not guest data -- the expiry check is a single string compare with no format predicate, so a missing date or an unpadded 2026-8-1 never expires.\n\nDROP: ledgerwitness -- THREE seats (glm, deepseek, minimax).\n\ngrok also declined to rank ieeeupgrade highly with a distinction worth keeping: it is 'the louder STORY (largest differential, weakest control, far-future date) but it is NOT a false pass of a check -- the 2099 date is doing what it was written to do'. A loud story and a live defect are different things.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 22.8 KB, grok 11.9 KB, kimi 46.1 KB, glm 10.7 KB, deepseek 4.8 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY -- the seat check identified it arithmetically this time: '16694b = brief 16006b + 688b'. Eighth consecutive wall.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: NOT prioritised, and grok's reason is the keeper: it is 'the louder STORY -- largest differential, weakest control, far-future date -- but NOT a false pass of a check. The 2099 date is doing what it was written to do.' A loud story and a live defect are different things.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:14942:     "note": "THE LAST THREE UNPANELLED ROWS -- the final panel of a nine-panel campaign that took the backlog from 44 open single-seat rows to zero.\n\n*** THE SEATS SPLIT ON `tfreq` IN THE EXACT SHAPE THE BRIEF WARNED ABOUT, AND THE MEASUREMENT SETTLED IT. ***  agy and deepseek said tfreq is ALREADY FIXED and should be closed, not worked; minimax said do it FIRST, as a FIX+DETECTOR round. A direct contradiction about whether a defect still exists -- so it was CHECKED rather than voted.\n\nMEASURED, and it is decisive. BOTH write paths clamp before storing:\n  timer.c:130-136  '#427: clamp before anything is stored, so freq and interval agree' --\n                   freq = timer_clamp_freq(...) THEN newtimer->freq = freq.\n  timer_update_frequency()  '#427/#429: CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store. The order is load-bearing and the shipped order was wrong twice over. THE OLD CODE STORED THE RAW VALUE INTO t->freq AND CLAMPED ONLY THE LOCAL, SO t->freq NAMED A RATE THE TIMER DID NOT RUN AT.'\nThat last sentence IS tfreq's title, in the source, marked fixed. tfreq is FIXED; its row is `active` with commits 863d238/b9daca1/cdc4849 and its hold says 'reviewed; regression batched to Sunday night'. It is awaiting a BATCHED REGRESS REVIEW, not open work.\n\nTHE SEAT-CLASS DOCTRINE PREDICTED THIS EXACTLY: the two seats that could OPEN THE FILE got it right; the packet-fed seat, reasoning from the row's text alone, would have sent a round at an already-closed defect. That is the fourth time in one night that a row's own text pointed the wrong way, and the second time a packet seat was overruled by one grep.\n\nes438: DROP AS A DEFECT ROW -- three seats (agy, deepseek, minimax), and agy's version is the useful one: CLOSE it as a completed review record while SPLITTING OUT the luna fuse-write defect it contains. A process record is not a defect row, but a real defect buried inside one must not be dropped with its container.\n\nrtcnarrow: deepseek would do it first. It is the design item filed earlier tonight, whose own three-device premise was already re-derived and largely dissolved.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 9.5 KB, grok 9.8 KB, kimi 81.4 KB, glm 8.4 KB, deepseek 4.0 KB, minimax 12.3 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, ninth consecutive wall, proven arithmetically again: '21348b = brief 20660b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: DROP AS A DEFECT ROW -- three seats. It is a RECORD of the flagship seat's review of the #438 round, not a defect. agy's version is the one to take: close it as a completed review record while SPLITTING OUT the luna fuse-write defect it contains -- a real defect buried in a process record must not be dropped with its container. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:15092:     "note": "THE LAST THREE UNPANELLED ROWS -- the final panel of a nine-panel campaign that took the backlog from 44 open single-seat rows to zero.\n\n*** THE SEATS SPLIT ON `tfreq` IN THE EXACT SHAPE THE BRIEF WARNED ABOUT, AND THE MEASUREMENT SETTLED IT. ***  agy and deepseek said tfreq is ALREADY FIXED and should be closed, not worked; minimax said do it FIRST, as a FIX+DETECTOR round. A direct contradiction about whether a defect still exists -- so it was CHECKED rather than voted.\n\nMEASURED, and it is decisive. BOTH write paths clamp before storing:\n  timer.c:130-136  '#427: clamp before anything is stored, so freq and interval agree' --\n                   freq = timer_clamp_freq(...) THEN newtimer->freq = freq.\n  timer_update_frequency()  '#427/#429: CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store. The order is load-bearing and the shipped order was wrong twice over. THE OLD CODE STORED THE RAW VALUE INTO t->freq AND CLAMPED ONLY THE LOCAL, SO t->freq NAMED A RATE THE TIMER DID NOT RUN AT.'\nThat last sentence IS tfreq's title, in the source, marked fixed. tfreq is FIXED; its row is `active` with commits 863d238/b9daca1/cdc4849 and its hold says 'reviewed; regression batched to Sunday night'. It is awaiting a BATCHED REGRESS REVIEW, not open work.\n\nTHE SEAT-CLASS DOCTRINE PREDICTED THIS EXACTLY: the two seats that could OPEN THE FILE got it right; the packet-fed seat, reasoning from the row's text alone, would have sent a round at an already-closed defect. That is the fourth time in one night that a row's own text pointed the wrong way, and the second time a packet seat was overruled by one grep.\n\nes438: DROP AS A DEFECT ROW -- three seats (agy, deepseek, minimax), and agy's version is the useful one: CLOSE it as a completed review record while SPLITTING OUT the luna fuse-write defect it contains. A process record is not a defect row, but a real defect buried inside one must not be dropped with its container.\n\nrtcnarrow: deepseek would do it first. It is the design item filed earlier tonight, whose own three-device premise was already re-derived and largely dissolved.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 9.5 KB, grok 9.8 KB, kimi 81.4 KB, glm 8.4 KB, deepseek 4.0 KB, minimax 12.3 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, ninth consecutive wall, proven arithmetically again: '21348b = brief 20660b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: deepseek would do it first. Filed earlier tonight, and its own three-device premise was re-derived and largely dissolved in the filing itself: dev_jazz has been bounded by construction since b679170 (#257) and dev_vr41xx by an unsigned divide by 0x8000, so the wide-domain narrowing case that justified a four-device round does not exist. The surviving item is the SILENT FLOOR at the other end -- smaller and differently shaped than what was filed.",
