HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:15476:     "note": "RECORDS / PIPELINE CLUSTER TRIAGE: nine rows about the bookkeeping itself. The brief carried an explicit caution drawn from earlier the same night -- 'a panel voted 5-0 to drop a row as already fixed and every seat was reading the SAME STALE SENTENCE; if a row here claims something is already done, treat that claim as the thing most in need of checking'.\n\n*** ONE SEAT ACTED ON THAT CAUTION AND IT PAID IMMEDIATELY. ***  grok opened files AGAINST the brief's own 'do not plan to read files' line, disclosed that it was doing so, and gave the reason: the 'already shipped / already covered' claims 'are the ones a stale hold can launder'. Its FIRST reported observation was not about any row -- it was 'Checkpoint is stale versus git log'. TRUE: CHECKPOINT.md named 5bd2ffa while HEAD was 9bf3be3. That file carries its own staleness rule and nothing enforced it; it had already drifted once tonight by six commits. Now checked mechanically (tools/pipeline/check_checkpoint.py, precommit section M, SOFT because the file is untracked and outside the git root). A seat that breaks the brief's letter to serve its purpose, and says so, is doing the job.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- a clean two-way split:\n  floodclass  agy ('the only guest-reachable defect with host-harm potential') and minimax, which scoped it EXACTLY right: 'a DETECTOR round on WHICH OF THE 543 CALLS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE'. That is precisely the gap the census declared and declined to fill -- it does no reachability analysis and says so on every run. NOTE agy's ranking rests on a reachability claim STRONGER than the census itself makes; the census's numbers are bounds, not defect counts.\n  smdatefmt   glm ('the only live measured false pass that one line closes') and deepseek. grok's reading refines it: the severity is a GATE BLIND-SPOT, not guest data -- the expiry check is a single string compare with no format predicate, so a missing date or an unpadded 2026-8-1 never expires.\n\nDROP: ledgerwitness -- THREE seats (glm, deepseek, minimax).\n\ngrok also declined to rank ieeeupgrade highly with a distinction worth keeping: it is 'the louder STORY (largest differential, weakest control, far-future date) but it is NOT a false pass of a check -- the 2099 date is doing what it was written to do'. A loud story and a live defect are different things.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 22.8 KB, grok 11.9 KB, kimi 46.1 KB, glm 10.7 KB, deepseek 4.8 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY -- the seat check identified it arithmetically this time: '16694b = brief 16006b + 688b'. Eighth consecutive wall.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: No seat prioritised or dropped it. The instrument shipped tonight and 12 soft rows remain for per-row adjudication.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:15593:     "note": "RECORDS / PIPELINE CLUSTER TRIAGE: nine rows about the bookkeeping itself. The brief carried an explicit caution drawn from earlier the same night -- 'a panel voted 5-0 to drop a row as already fixed and every seat was reading the SAME STALE SENTENCE; if a row here claims something is already done, treat that claim as the thing most in need of checking'.\n\n*** ONE SEAT ACTED ON THAT CAUTION AND IT PAID IMMEDIATELY. ***  grok opened files AGAINST the brief's own 'do not plan to read files' line, disclosed that it was doing so, and gave the reason: the 'already shipped / already covered' claims 'are the ones a stale hold can launder'. Its FIRST reported observation was not about any row -- it was 'Checkpoint is stale versus git log'. TRUE: CHECKPOINT.md named 5bd2ffa while HEAD was 9bf3be3. That file carries its own staleness rule and nothing enforced it; it had already drifted once tonight by six commits. Now checked mechanically (tools/pipeline/check_checkpoint.py, precommit section M, SOFT because the file is untracked and outside the git root). A seat that breaks the brief's letter to serve its purpose, and says so, is doing the job.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- a clean two-way split:\n  floodclass  agy ('the only guest-reachable defect with host-harm potential') and minimax, which scoped it EXACTLY right: 'a DETECTOR round on WHICH OF THE 543 CALLS ARE GUEST-REACHABLE'. That is precisely the gap the census declared and declined to fill -- it does no reachability analysis and says so on every run. NOTE agy's ranking rests on a reachability claim STRONGER than the census itself makes; the census's numbers are bounds, not defect counts.\n  smdatefmt   glm ('the only live measured false pass that one line closes') and deepseek. grok's reading refines it: the severity is a GATE BLIND-SPOT, not guest data -- the expiry check is a single string compare with no format predicate, so a missing date or an unpadded 2026-8-1 never expires.\n\nDROP: ledgerwitness -- THREE seats (glm, deepseek, minimax).\n\ngrok also declined to rank ieeeupgrade highly with a distinction worth keeping: it is 'the louder STORY (largest differential, weakest control, far-future date) but it is NOT a false pass of a check -- the 2099 date is doing what it was written to do'. A loud story and a live defect are different things.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 22.8 KB, grok 11.9 KB, kimi 46.1 KB, glm 10.7 KB, deepseek 4.8 KB, minimax 9.9 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY -- the seat check identified it arithmetically this time: '16694b = brief 16006b + 688b'. Eighth consecutive wall.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: LEAD CANDIDATE, two seats, and minimax scoped the round correctly: a DETECTOR round on WHICH of the 543 calls are guest-reachable. That is the gap census_floods.py explicitly declines to fill -- it does no reachability analysis, and its numbers are bounds rather than defect counts. agy ranked it first on a reachability claim STRONGER than the census makes; recorded as agy's claim, not the census's.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:15731:     "note": "8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.\n\n*** Q0 ANSWERED UNANIMOUSLY: NO MERGES. FIVE SEPARATE ROUNDS. ***  agy: 'split pitflip, pitlatch, pitlsb and isaorder into separate candidate rounds'. glm: 'none merge under the one-fix-design test'. deepseek: 'no merges'. minimax sequenced them rather than combining. **This OVERRULES the measure seat's own suggestion that pitflip was 'the natural companion' to i8253zero** -- it argued same-device and same-semantic-class (counter-load byte routing), and the panel applied the remaining prongs and said no. The measuring seat found the defects; the panel scoped them, and the two jobs came apart cleanly.\n\nWHAT IS FIRST -- pitlatch, 3 to 1:\n  pitlatch    deepseek, glm, minimax. glm's clause is the decisive one: 'a real guest reaches it in microtime()'. That is this project's ordering rule applied exactly -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- and pitlatch is the only one of the five demonstrated to be on a path a shipped guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: SECOND in the panel's sequence, and NOT merged with i8253zero despite the measure seat proposing exactly that. It is the row that makes i8253zero reachable at all, which is why it precedes it.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:15955:     "note": "8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.\n\n*** Q0 ANSWERED UNANIMOUSLY: NO MERGES. FIVE SEPARATE ROUNDS. ***  agy: 'split pitflip, pitlatch, pitlsb and isaorder into separate candidate rounds'. glm: 'none merge under the one-fix-design test'. deepseek: 'no merges'. minimax sequenced them rather than combining. **This OVERRULES the measure seat's own suggestion that pitflip was 'the natural companion' to i8253zero** -- it argued same-device and same-semantic-class (counter-load byte routing), and the panel applied the remaining prongs and said no. The measuring seat found the defects; the panel scoped them, and the two jobs came apart cleanly.\n\nWHAT IS FIRST -- pitlatch, 3 to 1:\n  pitlatch    deepseek, glm, minimax. glm's clause is the decisive one: 'a real guest reaches it in microtime()'. That is this project's ordering rule applied exactly -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- and pitlatch is the only one of the five demonstrated to be on a path a shipped guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: *** THE ROUND, 3 to 1. ***  The only one of the five demonstrated on a path a shipped guest actually takes: OpenBSD's microtime() issues the counter-latch command, and dev_8253.c treats it as an error, returns 0, and emits an unlatched fatal(). Ranked first on severity-and-reachability, not on count.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16109:     "note": "*** DISSENT, RECORDED AND NOT ADOPTED -- and it is the ninth seat arriving after the fix shipped. ***  Codex was ECHO-ONLY when this panel fired (7042b = brief 6354b + 688b); it answered a health test later the same day and was re-fired against the byte-identical brief.\n\nITS VERDICT: 'SPLIT -- suppress the false read-side error here, document the zero fallback, and reopen write-side state and real-count semantics separately.'\n\nSo it would have shipped pitlatch's read-side half alone and made pitclobber its own round. THE SHIPPED FIX PAIRED THEM. The pairing is justified rather than assumed, which is what the adjudicator required: one fix design -- 'the latch disturbs no state' -- covers both arms of one command, which is the reopening rule's third prong. Six seats on this design panel treated them together because the brief carried both, and the adjudicator ruled the pairing 'plausibly passes; it must say so'.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT ADOPTED, stated so a reader can disagree with me: under the stopping rule a review finding is fixed in-round only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and this is a SCOPE PREFERENCE -- codex names no defect in what shipped. Unpicking a gated, probe-witnessed change to satisfy a scope opinion would trade measured safety for tidiness. *** BUT IT AGREES WITH THE PIT CLUSTER PANEL'S OWN 'no merges' INSTINCT, and that is worth a reader's attention: two independent panels leaned toward splitting this device's defects and one brief -- mine -- put two of them together. If a future round finds the pairing caused trouble, this entry is where the warning was. ***\n\nIts other half, 'real-count semantics separately', is already the standing position: the file's own TODO at line 35 and #439's header both say the count is unmodelled.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16120:     "note": "PASS 2 ON #439 -- 7 OF 7 SCRIPTABLE SEATS, THE FIRST FULL SCRIPTABLE PANEL OF THIS CAMPAIGN. Codex answered for real (20,699 bytes) after ten consecutive echo-only walls.\n\n*** SIX SEATS SOUND; CODEX DEFECTIVE -- AND CODEX WAS RIGHT. ***  'The latch is never consumed, so the selected counter returns zero indefinitely.' Verified in source: latched[] is SET at :212, cleared ONLY at :219 by a non-latch mode write, and NEVER cleared by the read at :163. After one latch command, every subsequent read of that counter returns 0 until the guest happens to rewrite the mode byte. A real 8254 CONSUMES the latch per the programmed RW format.\n\nThe seat that had been WALLED for the pass-1 panel is the one that found it, which is the clearest argument this campaign has produced for discharging a wall rather than recording it and moving on. glm gestured at the same thing ('the sticky latch').\n\nNOT A REGRESSION: pre-#439 a latch clobbered mode_byte and subsequent reads also fell to the default arm returning 0, plus an unsilenceable flood. But it is a real defect in the fix, and SHIP-WITH-FIX is this project's practice on a pass-2 finding.\n\nHANDED TO THE IN-FLIGHT pitflip ROUND rather than fixed separately, and the reason is technical not merely practical: the consume semantics INTERACT WITH THE LSB/MSB FLIP-FLOP. In 16-bit mode a real latch delivers TWO bytes before reverting -- and the selector is exactly what pitflip is fixing. Two rounds editing dev_8253.c at once would also contend.\n\nTHE REFUSAL OF THE PASS-1 MAJORITY IS VINDICATED. The six SOUND verdicts converged on the brief's substantive question -- agy: 'returning 0 avoids guest kernel divide-by-zero crashes, preserves programmed mode state'; deepseek: 'the least harmful of three wrong answers and not a guest-visible regression'; minimax: 'the write-side fix matches the 8254 and removes a real bug'. Nobody defended the reload-value design that the implementing seat built and measured harmful. What was missed by everyone, including me, was the consume.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16301:     "note": "8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.\n\n*** Q0 ANSWERED UNANIMOUSLY: NO MERGES. FIVE SEPARATE ROUNDS. ***  agy: 'split pitflip, pitlatch, pitlsb and isaorder into separate candidate rounds'. glm: 'none merge under the one-fix-design test'. deepseek: 'no merges'. minimax sequenced them rather than combining. **This OVERRULES the measure seat's own suggestion that pitflip was 'the natural companion' to i8253zero** -- it argued same-device and same-semantic-class (counter-load byte routing), and the panel applied the remaining prongs and said no. The measuring seat found the defects; the panel scoped them, and the two jobs came apart cleanly.\n\nWHAT IS FIRST -- pitlatch, 3 to 1:\n  pitlatch    deepseek, glm, minimax. glm's clause is the decisive one: 'a real guest reaches it in microtime()'. That is this project's ordering rule applied exactly -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- and pitlatch is the only one of the five demonstrated to be on a path a shipped guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: SPLIT OUT, separate round. A different arm (LSB-only recomputes nothing); no seat argued to fold it and no seat prioritised it.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16428:     "note": "8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.\n\n*** Q0 ANSWERED UNANIMOUSLY: NO MERGES. FIVE SEPARATE ROUNDS. ***  agy: 'split pitflip, pitlatch, pitlsb and isaorder into separate candidate rounds'. glm: 'none merge under the one-fix-design test'. deepseek: 'no merges'. minimax sequenced them rather than combining. **This OVERRULES the measure seat's own suggestion that pitflip was 'the natural companion' to i8253zero** -- it argued same-device and same-semantic-class (counter-load byte routing), and the panel applied the remaining prongs and said no. The measuring seat found the defects; the panel scoped them, and the two jobs came apart cleanly.\n\nWHAT IS FIRST -- pitlatch, 3 to 1:\n  pitlatch    deepseek, glm, minimax. glm's clause is the decisive one: 'a real guest reaches it in microtime()'. That is this project's ordering rule applied exactly -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- and pitlatch is the only one of the five demonstrated to be on a path a shipped guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: DROP AS A ROUND -- two seats explicitly, a third as 'filed-only pending a reproduction'. It is the one item filed at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason: latent, unmeasured, different file. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held. If it survives, it needs its own reproduction first -- an ordering bug in what construction itself does is what a rung-2 witness is for.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16596:     "note": "*** DISSENT, RECORDED AND NOT ADOPTED -- and it is the ninth seat arriving after the fix shipped. ***  Codex was ECHO-ONLY when this panel fired (7042b = brief 6354b + 688b); it answered a health test later the same day and was re-fired against the byte-identical brief.\n\nITS VERDICT: 'SPLIT -- suppress the false read-side error here, document the zero fallback, and reopen write-side state and real-count semantics separately.'\n\nSo it would have shipped pitlatch's read-side half alone and made pitclobber its own round. THE SHIPPED FIX PAIRED THEM. The pairing is justified rather than assumed, which is what the adjudicator required: one fix design -- 'the latch disturbs no state' -- covers both arms of one command, which is the reopening rule's third prong. Six seats on this design panel treated them together because the brief carried both, and the adjudicator ruled the pairing 'plausibly passes; it must say so'.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT ADOPTED, stated so a reader can disagree with me: under the stopping rule a review finding is fixed in-round only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and this is a SCOPE PREFERENCE -- codex names no defect in what shipped. Unpicking a gated, probe-witnessed change to satisfy a scope opinion would trade measured safety for tidiness. *** BUT IT AGREES WITH THE PIT CLUSTER PANEL'S OWN 'no merges' INSTINCT, and that is worth a reader's attention: two independent panels leaned toward splitting this device's defects and one brief -- mine -- put two of them together. If a future round finds the pairing caused trouble, this entry is where the warning was. ***\n\nIts other half, 'real-count semantics separately', is already the standing position: the file's own TODO at line 35 and #439's header both say the count is unmodelled.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16605:     "note": "PASS 2 ON #439 -- 7 OF 7 SCRIPTABLE SEATS, THE FIRST FULL SCRIPTABLE PANEL OF THIS CAMPAIGN. Codex answered for real (20,699 bytes) after ten consecutive echo-only walls.\n\n*** SIX SEATS SOUND; CODEX DEFECTIVE -- AND CODEX WAS RIGHT. ***  'The latch is never consumed, so the selected counter returns zero indefinitely.' Verified in source: latched[] is SET at :212, cleared ONLY at :219 by a non-latch mode write, and NEVER cleared by the read at :163. After one latch command, every subsequent read of that counter returns 0 until the guest happens to rewrite the mode byte. A real 8254 CONSUMES the latch per the programmed RW format.\n\nThe seat that had been WALLED for the pass-1 panel is the one that found it, which is the clearest argument this campaign has produced for discharging a wall rather than recording it and moving on. glm gestured at the same thing ('the sticky latch').\n\nNOT A REGRESSION: pre-#439 a latch clobbered mode_byte and subsequent reads also fell to the default arm returning 0, plus an unsilenceable flood. But it is a real defect in the fix, and SHIP-WITH-FIX is this project's practice on a pass-2 finding.\n\nHANDED TO THE IN-FLIGHT pitflip ROUND rather than fixed separately, and the reason is technical not merely practical: the consume semantics INTERACT WITH THE LSB/MSB FLIP-FLOP. In 16-bit mode a real latch delivers TWO bytes before reverting -- and the selector is exactly what pitflip is fixing. Two rounds editing dev_8253.c at once would also contend.\n\nTHE REFUSAL OF THE PASS-1 MAJORITY IS VINDICATED. The six SOUND verdicts converged on the brief's substantive question -- agy: 'returning 0 avoids guest kernel divide-by-zero crashes, preserves programmed mode state'; deepseek: 'the least harmful of three wrong answers and not a guest-visible regression'; minimax: 'the write-side fix matches the 8254 and removes a real bug'. Nobody defended the reload-value design that the implementing seat built and measured harmful. What was missed by everyone, including me, was the consume.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16736:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE PREMISE IS TRUE AND THE FRAMING AS A DEFECT IS WRONG -- the limitation is DELIBERATE, DOCUMENTED AND REASONED. But there is a narrow mechanical subset the stated reasoning does NOT cover, and I hit it myself today. ***\n\n**The tool says so in its own header** (`check_witness.py:38-41`):\n  > *\"The check enforces the SHAPE, not the truth: it cannot tell whether `probe:foo.py` really preserves routing. **That judgement stays with the panel, which is the honest division** -- a script that claimed to validate a witness would be exactly the false comfort the ladder was written against.\"*\n\n**That reasoning is correct and should not be overturned.** *Does this artefact actually witness the defect?* is a judgement about routing and controls; a script asserting it would manufacture confidence, which is the precise failure the witness ladder exists to prevent.\n\n*** BUT THE EXEMPTION IS BROADER THAN ITS REASON. *** The reason covers a JUDGEMENT. Two adjacent facts are not judgements at all:\n  * **does the named file EXIST?** -- one `os.path.isfile`;\n  * **does ANY gate reference it?** -- one grep over `regress/gate_*.sh`.\n**Neither claims the witness is valid. Both catch a witness that cannot possibly be valid**, and a witness naming a file no gate ever runs is in that category.\n\n**I PRODUCED A LIVE INSTANCE TODAY.** Back-filling [[exitsweep]]'s witness I wrote that its probe was *wired into TWO gates ... so it actually runs*. It is run by ONE (`gate_m88k_rounding.sh`); the other only counts its constructs in a census. **Section K passed that field**, because the string's shape was valid. A gate-reference grep would have reddened my overclaim in the commit that made it.\n\n**DISPOSITION: not a defect in the tool's design; a candidate for a NARROW additive check** -- existence plus at-least-one-gate-reference, reported as WARN rather than FAIL so it cannot become the switched-off kind, and explicitly NOT claiming to validate the witness. **The addition licence wants a measured delta: today's overclaim is one.** Filed, not built -- it is a change to the script every commit runs and owes negative controls in both directions.\n\n"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:16761:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, LATENT, and now QUANTIFIED EXACTLY -- 12 of 14. The remedy is already in the same file, applied to the other 2. ***\n\n**The shape**, `precommit_check.sh:710-718` (the witness checker, representative of all 14):\n```sh\nif [ ! -f \"$WITCHK\" ]; then\n        warn \"check_witness.py MISSING -- witness naming NOT verified\"    # SOFT\nelif out=$(python \"$WITCHK\" 2>&1); then  good ...\nelse    bad \"A ROW CLOSED WITH COMMITS AND NO ADMISSIBLE WITNESS:\"        # HARD\n```\n**A checker that FAILS is a hard failure. A checker that is ABSENT is a warning.** So deleting the file converts a hard gate into a soft one, and the commit proceeds.\n\n**THE CENSUS (deriver: `grep -cE '\\[ ! -f .*(CHK|check_)'` then the following line):**\n```\n  checkers guarded          14\n  MISSING -> warn (soft)    12\n  MISSING -> bad  (hard)     2   check_row_states.py, check_cited_artifacts.py\n```\n\n> *** THE TWO HARD ONES ARE THE FINDING. *** Somebody already decided this question for two checkers and implemented it in this exact file, so **the remedy is a transferred warrant, not a design** -- make the other twelve match the two.\n\n**NOT guest-reachable:** it is a host-side pre-commit script; no emulated guest can delete a worktree file. **Observable:** the precommit process exit status -- 0 when broken, non-zero when fixed.\n\n**BUT DO NOT SWEEP IT UNILATERALLY -- there is a real argument on the other side, and it is the one this project keeps citing:** a hard failure on absence means a degraded or partial checkout cannot run the gate AT ALL, and *a check that has to be switched off is worse than no check*. The counter-argument is that all fourteen live in the same repo, so absence is not a partial-checkout condition -- it is a deletion. **That is an adjudication question, not a unilateral edit**, and it changes the script every commit runs, so it also owes negative controls both ways.\n\n*Directly relevant to today: I relied on this gate all session and it caught three real obligations. This does not undermine those catches -- a checker that RAN and failed is hard. It bounds what a green gate proves: it proves the checkers PRESENT are satisfied, not that the intended set ran.*\n\n  OWNER DECIDED 2026-09-13.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:17302:   "witness": "probe:regress/sh4_bsc_width_probe.py (rung 3, unmodified -E landisk; symptom on pre-fix HEAD at 2/13 and 13/13 after, same script; liveness, device-signature and BCR1 negative controls all green in both)"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18397:   "note": "  *** AND THE DESIGNED END STATE STILL BLOCKS THE COMMIT -- FILED AS `selftestaddition`, NOT FIXED HERE. ***  With the EXEMPT table EMPTY (the outcome the table exists to drive toward) the selftest reports `SELFTEST_FAIL 12/22`, because seven mutants are built by SUBTRACTING from live input data.  The harness still punishes progress; the punishment has only moved to the LAST discharge instead of the first.  **The way out is measured, not theorised:** the pass-2 seat built `M3x` -- a STALE EXEMPTION mutant made by INSERTING an exemption for the already-wired `arm_flags_probe.py` -- and it scored `ok` on a tree whose EXEMPT table is completely empty.  *The root cause of both the original defect and its replacement is that the selftest MUTATES LIVE INPUT DATA IT DOES NOT NEED.*  Deliberately not done in this round: it rewrites seven mutants at once and owes two negative controls each, so it ships as its own harness round.  *** THIS ROW IS CLOSED AND THE FINDINGS BELOW ARRIVED AFTER IT CLOSED.  DISPOSED UNDER THE LATE-DISSENT RULE, ALL THREE ARMS, AND THE CODE CHANGE IS A **FOLLOW-UP COMMIT** -- NOT AN AMENDMENT UNDER THIS ROW'S NUMBER. ***  The rule's own words: *closure changes the LICENSE, never the truth-value*, and *never amend shipped code under a closed round's number*.  **(1) WRONG RECORD -> L4, corrected in place:** `_pick_exempt_detector`'s docstring described `_exline()` behaviour that the very commit closing this row had replaced.  **(2) MEASURED FALSE PASS against the shipped detector's floor -> L2, repaired in a follow-up:** the frozen-clock edit is vacuity-floor clause (d) exactly -- the guard kept, its repair nulled -- and it scored a CLEAN SWEEP (`SELFTEST_PASS 21/21`, production `PROBEWIRING_PASS`) while making every dated exemption immortal.  `M5f` closes it, controlled both directions.  **(3) EVERYTHING ELSE -> FILED as a new queue item:** the addition-versus-subtraction redesign, `selftestaddition`, carrying the seat's measurement verbatim.  *This line exists so a future reader never sees false unanimity on a closed row: the closing commit's own claim -- that deriving the exemplar had removed the harness-punishes-progress class -- was measured TRUE for the first discharge and FALSE for the last one, by a seat fired after the fact.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:20339:   "witness": "probe:regress/sh_intprio_probe.py rows `irq14 deliv` / `irq14 imask` (rung 3, SHIPPED). One guest store to the mp IPI register on an unmodified -E testsh, free-running with a breakpoint at VBR+0x600: measured NOT DELIVERED pre-fix, DELIVERED post-fix, with two negative controls (no trigger store -> no spurious breakpoint; IMASK=15 -> refused). The imask row brackets the level to exactly 1, which is what pins IRQ14 rather than 'some line delivered' -- IRQ13 and IRQ0 both deliver and were measured escaping a delivery-only witness.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:20510:     "note": "VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN, 108 KB -- AND THIS ANSWER ONLY EXISTS BECAUSE THE OWNER NOTICED. The first invocation WALLED mid-answer (403, usage limit) after 36 KB, ending immediately after 'especially where intc_intpri08 gets written, which decides Q1' -- it had identified the crux and was cut off. panel.sh could not see that wall at all (it only caught the echo-only shape); seat_verdicts.py could, by position. Re-fired an hour later it answered in full. A wall is a MOMENT, not a state.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:21593:     "outcome_note": "The prose false positive and missing negative controls were confirmed, as fable5 review 2026-08-23 records. The prescribed line-start-only remedy was insufficient: the closure and fable5 regress 2026-08-23 document that it rejected 52 of 58 Grok answers and needed sentence-boundary support. [graded by codex gpt-6-astra max, n7, 2026-09-19]"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:21600:     "note": "[panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP WITH CHANGES. Named the same-line-prose case (`VERDICT: SHIP I think this is ready.` -> `SHIP I THINK THIS`), conflicting verdicts, and fenced-code quoting as missing rows -- the first two measured true and are FILED as verdictprose; the split reporting makes them loud rather than silent. Its structural ask is the sharpest: provenance for a score claim should come from SYNTAX, not numerical proximity -- an explicit docstring marker rather than a distance heuristic. That is what the '<N>-row' era escape now does, arrived at independently. Asked that section U not be blocking until parsing is fail-closed and the checker has tests; the checker now has both a pinned coverage floor and negative controls, so the substance is met even though the check stays hard.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:22671:     "note": "VERDICT: **CLOSE as `harness:`-witnessed -- BUT NOT YET: one seat is not the mandated\nroster, and the seat said so itself.  PROVISIONAL on 1 of 9.**  Every claim below was\nre-verified by me against the files before being written down.\n\n*** MY 'ESTABLISHED TWICE' WAS AN UNDERCOUNT.  IT IS THE FIFTH OCCURRENCE AGAINST FOUR\nPRIOR WRITE-UPS. ***  Two more, both verified by opening them:\n  * `CHANGELOG.md` (#477-era) -- the identical differential, X=hello then echo of [$X]\n    printing empty brackets, and it **cost the Opus seat TWO wrong measurements that both\n    looked like results**.\n  * `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` (`asanbaselinedrift`, 2026-08-28) -- *\"make reported MAKE_RC=0\n    on every one of the failed builds.  It was caught only because the check was\n    [ -x gxemul ] rather than the exit status.\"*\n\n**Five occurrences, four records, zero prevention.**  That is the structure-first rule's\nconversion condition met with room to spare -- prose is MEASURED ineffective for this class.\n\n*** WHY THIS CLASS IS GENUINELY UNLIKE THE TWO CHECKERS THIS PROJECT REJECTED ON\nMEASUREMENT (counts, citation-form), and the distinction is not special pleading. ***\nThose rejections turned on: nothing consumes the value functionally, and a drifted value\nSELF-REFUTES at the point of use.  **Invert every clause here.**  The corrupted value IS the\nfunctional observable (an rc, a mutant variable).  It SELF-CONFIRMS -- 0 is exactly what a\ngreen build prints, and an emptied loop variable prints a reassuring '(no binary)'.  And the\npre-specified escalation trigger those classes are still waiting on -- *the first MEASURED\ndecision consumed* -- **has already fired here, twice**: a REGRESS_FAIL run read as exit 0,\nand a kill-table control that scored itself green on unmodified source.\n\n*** BUT THE OBVIOUS CHECK HAS ZERO RECALL, MEASURED, AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\nA census of the committed tree finds the wsl-bash-c form **exactly four times, and all four\nare QUOTATIONS OF THE DEFECT** (two records, the nightly_battery header, the carrier's nohup\nincident).  **Zero live committed instances.**  All five occurrences were interactive,\nad-hoc invocations.  So a precommit grep scores zero on the entire recorded history -- the\nsame profile that killed the citation checker -- and its false positives would be honest\ncorrection-records, which is the quoted-marker-re-arms-the-tool trap.\n\nSO THE STRUCTURE GOES AT THE TYPING SURFACE, NOT THE COMMIT SURFACE:\n  * **FILED, not done: a PreToolUse hook** warning when a wsl invocation's -c string carries\n    an unescaped dollar.  The hook layer is this project's standing precedent for 'does not\n    depend on remembering'.  Two negative controls owed: it must FIRE on the bare form, and\n    stay SILENT on the script-file and backslash-escaped forms.\n  * **A zero-population ratchet** over tracked scripts -- grandfathers nothing (population\n    measured zero), fails on the first violation.  Its header must state its recall honestly.\n\n**And the primary defence already exists and already fires**: the binary-newer-than-source\ncheck, the object-count assertion, and inside-computed sentinels caught all five occurrences\nafter the fact.  The hook would catch them before.\n\n*** (2) THE WITNESS-FIELD CORRECTION IS RIGHT, AND THE FIELD IS WRONG IN A FOURTH WAY I DID\nNOT CLAIM. ***  All three of my claims verified against the file, plus: **'Called by\ngate_asan_sweep.sh' is FALSE.**  That gate REFERENCES it three times -- one gate_skip guard\nand two comments -- and never calls it; no committed caller invokes it at all.\n\n**AND THE POLARITY IS BACKWARDS, which is the sharpest correction here.**  The field\ncondemns the existence check as weak.  On 2026-08-28 that exact check is what **CAUGHT** the\nfailed builds while MAKE_RC read 0.  *The record condemns the guard that is on record\ncatching the defect.*\n\nClaim (c) is also STRONGER than I put it: the gxemul link is the LAST action of `make all`,\nso in a freshly wiped directory there is no post-link failure window -- existence is\nEQUIVALENT to make having succeeded, not merely correlated with it.\n\n*** (3) CORRECT THE HEADER'S REASON, DO NOT DROP IT -- AND MY OWN ARTIFACT PROVES MORE THAN\nI CLAIMED. ***  I wrote that the expanding layer is 'NOT established and not guessed'.\nSection D licenses more than that: I typed the backslash-escaped dollar inside SINGLE quotes,\nso git-bash passed it through literally -- and had the inner bash received it verbatim it\nwould have printed the two characters, not 1.  **The measured 1 proves something between\ngit-bash and the inner shell consumed the escape and left the dollar live.**  With a bare\ndollar-query expanding to 0 and quoted whitespace surviving, that layer exhibits POSIX\ndouble-quote semantics.  That much is ENTAILED BY THE ARTIFACT.  Only the component's\nidentity stays open.\n\nTwo 2-second discriminators, NOT run (a measurement holds the machine): echo of a\nsingle-quoted dollar-zero through wsl (a relaying shell prints its own name), and the same\nrc test via `wsl -e bash -c` (predicted 1 if the relay is the expander).\n\n*** ALSO FOUND, VERIFIED, AND FILED RATHER THAN FIXED: `build_asan.sh` ALWAYS EXITS 0. ***\nThe trailing BUILD_ASAN_DONE echo launders build_one's `return 1`, and the catch-all arm\ndiscards the head build's rc entirely.  **Latent, not live** -- no caller consumes its exit\nstatus -- so the stopping rule says FILE.  It is the carrier's own failure-laundered-into-a-\nclean-completion shape, one scripted && away from mattering.\n\n*Fairness, since I asked for it: the seat found the sharpest fact cuts AGAINST my framing.\naf4884c's receipt says 're-run from a script file on disk, PER THE DOCUMENTED FIX' -- so the\n08-20 seat HAD read the first write-up and the class still bit first.  That indicts\nmemory-as-trigger, not any seat.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:22699:   "state_note": "*** HALF FIXED AS #470: the FOREIGN-QUOTE half is closed, measured and self-defending. The INCOMPLETE-SEAT half stays filed with its nine designed controls. ***\n\n**THE FIX:** a line beginning `path:NNN:` or `path-NNN-` is TOOL OUTPUT the seat streamed into its own file -- a grep match or context line -- never its own prose. `seat_verdicts.py` now skips those before the verdict regex sees them.\n\n**MEASURED FOOTPRINT across every panel dir on disk (1,310 seat files):** 5,341 lines contain 'VERDICT'; **1,062 are ACCEPTED as verdicts by the extractor; the rule kills 29.** Zero packet-fed seats affected -- an Ollama seat cannot grep, so its output contains only its own words. Every kill is on a file-reading seat, which is the predicted population.\n\nCONTROLS, all run against REAL panels: the motivating panel now reports codex as CHANGES REQUIRED with no SHIP-WITH phantom; `panel_20260825_112845`'s split drops from four verdicts to two, losing exactly the two grep-quoted phantoms (one of which the extractor read as **'the DECLINE is the'**); and the clean panel is untouched at 7 answered.\n\n**SELFTEST: 27 rows, 14 mutants, every mutant red** (was 24/13). M14 reddens if a future edit drops the guard, so the rule cannot silently rot.\n\n*** THE SELFTEST CAUGHT ME SHIPPING A VACUOUS TEST, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. *** My first fixtures LOOKED like the defect -- `path:NNN:  VERDICT: SHIP` -- and M14 measured them **GREEN with the guard disabled**, proving something else already rejected them. It did: a colon satisfies neither anchor branch, so the anchor killed them anyway. **A test that cannot fail proves nothing, and I would have shipped one had the mutant not existed.** The real lines carry EMPHASIS AND SENTENCE PUNCTUATION after the grep prefix (`...r52_kimi.txt:450:  **1. VERDICT: DOCUMENT-ONLY**`), which is what satisfies the anchor and exactly why the guard is needed. Fixtures are now real lines from real panel files -- which is what the farmed design recommended.\n\n*And the fix took THREE attempts, all defeated by the same thing: the BACKSLASH. The first pattern wrote `\\-` (an escaped hyphen) where backslash-then-hyphen was meant, so WINDOWS PATHS NEVER MATCHED and I measured a footprint of 2 instead of 29. The file already defines ST/US/DQ/BQ/NL as chr() constants for precisely this reason; I added BS beside them rather than fight the escaping a fourth time. Third instance of that trap today.*\n\n**STILL OWED -- the other half:** do not extract from a seat that has not FINISHED. That needs a COMPLETION RECORD, and the design proved a stable file size is NOT one (`stable_thinker`: a thinking model stalls its output while still running). Nine controls are designed and recorded; the implementation is not done.\n\n*** FIX DESIGNED 2026-08-26 BY A FARMED codex JOB, WITH NINE NAMED CONTROLS KEYED TO REAL FILES FROM TODAY'S PANELS -- AND IT REFUTED THE SECOND SIGNAL I PROPOSED. ***\n\n**IT RESOLVED THE CRUX I could not.** The obvious fix is a POSITION check, but this project's briefs demand VERDICT LINE FIRST precisely so truncation cannot destroy the answer -- so 'last match wins' is exactly wrong here. The control `first_real_then_late_quote` pins that: a file with a real verdict FIRST and a quoted bad verdict LATER must return the FIRST, *'proving last-wins was not reintroduced.'*\n\n*** AND MY 'A GROWING FILE HAS NOT CONCLUDED' HEURISTIC IS INSUFFICIENT, which the seat showed with a control rather than an argument: *** `stable_thinker` -- two identical size reads while the process is STILL ALIVE must remain unsettled, *'proving \"stable size means done\" cannot pass.'* **A thinking model can stall its output while running**, so stability proves nothing. The real discriminator is a COMPLETION RECORD / final-message artifact, not file growth. `growth_between_stats` covers the other direction: append between the two stats and extraction must be suppressed even if a plausible verdict was already present.\n\n**THE FULL CONTROL SET -- four positive, five negative, most keyed to files on disk today:**\n  POSITIVE: `completed_codex_with_foreign_middle` (the real `panel_20260826_222557` codex transcript must return CHANGES REQUIRED, **not the middle SHIP-WITH** -- that is the exact measured defect); `agy_verdict_first` (must return GO); `grok_single_line_first` (must retain its opening verdict despite a no-newline streaming form); `legacy_trailing` (a completed artifact with a trailing VERDICT must still be found).\n  NEGATIVE: `active_foreign_only` (in-flight, foreign line present, no completion record -> `RUNNING/UNSETTLED`, verdict `-`); `completed_tool_quote_no_answer` (process exited, no final assistant message -> still no verdict); plus the two above.\n\n**THAT IS A COMPLETE, SHIPPABLE DESIGN THAT NEEDS NO EMULATOR BUILD** -- which is why this row and `probewiringscope` were farmed while the machine is embargoed. The remaining work is implementation plus running the controls.\n\n*Note the seat used TODAY'S PANEL FILES as its fixtures rather than synthetic strings, so the positive controls are regression tests against the exact failure that motivated the row.*\n\n*** THIS ROW HAD AN EMPTY state_note UNTIL NOW -- I flagged it this morning as unworkable BECAUSE its evidence would have to be re-derived from scratch. It got its instance the same day, and the instance is WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS. ***\n\nThe title says the extractor 'sweeps trailing prose into the captured value'. **It also attributes ANOTHER SEAT'S VERDICT, FROM ANOTHER PANEL, TO A SEAT THAT HAS NOT FINISHED THINKING.**\n\nMEASURED on `panel_20260826_222557` (the `#466` pass 2): `seat_verdicts.py` printed\n`codex  909882  95%  SHIP-WITH  answered`. Codex had issued no verdict line at all -- `grep -n '^VERDICT' codex.txt` returns NOTHING -- and it was **still running**, its file growing from 909,882 to 965,763 bytes during the check with its tail mid-grep.\n\nThe matched string is at `codex.txt:924`, and it is not codex's text. It is a line codex GREPPED OUT OF `regress/pass2_ledger.tsv`: a receipt from 2026-08-18 recording a DIFFERENT panel's `SHIP-` verdict. **The extractor read a file-search result as the seat's own conclusion.**\n\n*** WHY THIS IS THE SEAT-FAILURE-COUNTED-AS-AGREEMENT SHAPE IN ITS PUREST FORM, arriving through the tool built to prevent it: *** the round record would have carried 'codex: SHIP-WITH' -- a FABRICATED RATIFICATION from a seat that had not answered. The panel would have been reported complete with its most substantial seat still thinking, and nobody reading the record could tell.\n\n**AND IT IS SPECIFIC TO THE FILE-READING SEATS, which is what makes it dangerous.** A packet-fed seat cannot grep, so its output contains only its own words. Codex, agy and grok stream their SEARCH RESULTS into the same file as their answer -- so the richer the seat's investigation, the more other people's verdicts it quotes, and the more likely a position-blind matcher latches onto one.\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR THE EXTRACTOR ALREADY HAS AND DID NOT APPLY HERE: POSITION.** `seat_verdicts.py` uses a position check to catch the kimi wall-in-a-huge-answer class. A verdict is a seat's CONCLUSION, so it belongs at the END of its output -- `:924` of a file that is now past 965 KB is nowhere near it. **A match in the first 1% of a still-growing file is a quotation, not a conclusion.**\n\nSECOND SIGNAL, free and independent: **a file that is still GROWING has not concluded.** The extractor read a snapshot of a live seat. Comparing size across two reads costs nothing and would have refused this outright.\n\nNOT FIXED IN-ROUND: this is a checker change and owes negative controls in both directions -- a real trailing verdict must still be found, and a quoted one must not be. Filed with the instance, which is what the row was missing.\n\n"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:22719:     "note": "*** THE SHARPEST SCOPE FINDING OF THE ROUND. *** Every one of the 24 selftest rows exercises verdict extraction. The wall / DOWN / echo-only classification has none -- and the three misclassifications this file records all happened there: a walled codex producing the LARGEST file in the panel, a LIVE seat reported as walled because its reasoning mentioned a quota word, and three Ollama seats filed as 'answered, format miss' when the daemon was not running at all. So precommit section V can fire, PASS, and prove nothing about the part of the file that has actually failed before. Rows are cheap and the positive controls already exist as committed panel dirs. Same seat also found section V is EDGE-TRIGGERED with no level check (commit a defective revision in a session that skips precommit and every later run reports 'extractor unchanged' forever, because the defect is now the baseline -- the selftest is sub-second, so the fix is to run it unconditionally), and that a `git mv` takes the missing-file branch, which is a SOFT warn, so precommit can PASS with the selftest never having run."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:23503:     "note": "THE SEAT THAT FOUND WHAT NO OTHER SEAT DID, AND IT IS INSIDE THIS FILE'S OWN TRANSLATION UNIT. *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:46 INCLUDES thirdparty/dreamcast_sysasicvar.h, AND EVERY SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT() CALL EXPANDS SYSASIC_EVENT_RANGE_OK -- A C99 COMPILE-TIME BOUND ASSERT. FOUR of them compile on every build, two hundred lines below a comment saying the technique is unavailable to this tree. *** Census of the idiom, multi-line aware (it flagged that its own first single-line grep undercounted): FOUR sites -- cpu_mips.c x2 (#388), memory_rw.c (#430), sysasicvar.h (#468/#469, bitfield form). Chronology airtight: #430 ships the idiom 08-15; gdrom's C99 rationale written 08-23; #468 REFUTES that rationale by measurement 08-26; this code ships 08-27. THREE FURTHER L4s NOBODY ELSE RAISED, all corrected this round: the trigger comment SURVIVED ITS OWN DISCHARGE verbatim and in the PRESENT TENSE (still saying 'SIX OF EIGHT BITS' when it is six of 32, and 'truncates to 0 in a uint8_t' when the field is uint32_t); the C99 rationale at the GDROM_UNIMPL_FIRST comment; and 'THE PRE-SET TRIGGER ABOVE HAS FIRED' pointing the wrong DIRECTION (the trigger is below). MEASURED: GDROM_W_NBITS had ZERO consumers -- it sized nothing, bounded nothing, and DELETING it compiled clean -- so 'correct BY CONSTRUCTION' rested on a decorative symbol, and the shipped assert is its FIRST CONSUMER, which makes it L4 repair rather than accretion. 16-variant kill table with four legal configurations as negative controls: the brief's own proposed `GDROM_W_NBITS <= 32` ESCAPES the 9-bits-over-uint8_t defect -- MEASURED VACUOUS against floor clauses (b)/(d). CONCEDED the shipped form's clause 2 as a real hole in its own proposal (its one-clause form accepts 40 positions over a uint64_t field, where `1u << 40` is UB). Verified 'emits no object code' with the filename held constant: the object differs in exactly TWO bytes, both __LINE__ immediates displaced +50 by the comment block. Found the dev_sh4.c ASYMMETRY: SH4_VAL_NCLASS SIZES an array and bounds a runtime check, so ITS by-construction claim is true unaided, while GDROM_W_NBITS's was not -- the precedent transfers on shape but NOT on the property it was cited for. Population CONFIRMED at twelve and the truncation hunt NEGATIVE: no cast, no %hh anywhere in src/, the struct never escapes the file, and the tree has no save-state machinery at all. Disclosed its own apparatus failure: two of its scripts raced on one scratch directory and one deleted the other's inputs; it re-ran isolated and stands behind only the clean table.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:23692:   "state_note": "*** AND THE FEATURE'S VALUE WAS DEMONSTRATED THE SAME HOUR, BY A SEAT THAT BUILT ONE. ***\n\nWhile measuring Round 2's mutants the opus seat wrote its own probe WITH session selection\n(`tprobe2.py <bin> <img> 'S1 badfmt,S4 acceptA'`) and used it immediately: for the Site A\nfallthrough mutant it ran TWO sessions instead of eight, because only those two can\ndistinguish a decline from an accept.\n\n**That is the argument for the feature and, read carefully, also the argument for why it\nis a separate round.**  Selection is CHEAP in a purpose-built probe where it is designed\nin; the cost measured here is RETROFITTING it to a 1200-line probe whose 36 sessions run\nat module level.  The two facts are not in tension -- they are the same fact seen from\neach end, and a future round should read them together rather than citing one.\n\n*** A FOURTH COST FACT, MEASURED 2026-08-28, AND IT IS ABOUT THE FIX RATHER THAN THE COST: A ROW FILTER IS NOT A SMALL CHANGE TO THIS PROBE. ***\n\nRound 2 was going to fold one in -- the owner approved it on a cost I stated -- and the estimate was wrong by roughly 10x.  Measured before building:\n\n    session() calls at MODULE level (column 0)   36\n    session() calls inside a block                3\n                                                 --\n                                                 39\n\n**Skipping a row therefore means skipping TOP-LEVEL STATEMENTS.**  A filter in `row()` alone changes what is SCORED and saves NOTHING, because the expensive part -- the session -- has already run by the time row() is called.  Real per-row selection needs either a label threaded through all 36 call sites with an early-return in session(), or the probe restructured into per-row functions.\n\n**WITHDRAWN FROM ROUND 2 BY OWNER DECISION** so the strengthening diff stays about the replacement channel, which is what its pass 2 reviews.  It ships as its own round with its own panel and its own negative controls -- a filter that silently selects nothing is this project's most-recorded failure class, and it must not ride in on a diff reviewed for something else.\n\nWHEN IT IS BUILT, the guards are already specified: print a DENOMINATOR (`selected N of 58`) beside every result; HARD-FAIL on zero matches; HARD-FAIL on a selector naming a row that does not exist (so a renamed row cannot silently stop being tested); and unset MUST be byte-identical to today, which is the negative control the gate itself provides.  Name derived from the tree rather than invented: `GDROM_DEF_ROWS` -- the house idiom is `os.environ.get(\"<TOOL>_<KNOB>\")` (SELFMUTANT_RUN_TIMEOUT and friends) and this probe already owns the GDROM_DEF_ namespace via GDROM_DEF_RESULT= / GDROM_DEF_VECTOR=.\n\n*** AND THE GATE PINS THE DENOMINATOR AT 58: *** gate_sh_rounding.sh names rows in a `for v in ...` list and checks the ratio, so any filter must leave the unset-path denominator untouched.\n\n*** A THIRD COST VARIABLE, MEASURED 2026-08-27: COMMANDS PER SESSION. ***\n\nThis row's headline is *\"cost is SESSIONS, not rows\"*, and that was derived from probes\nwhose rows each run one short session. **A row added to `asic_badaddr_probe.py` today is\nONE session -- cheap by this row's own measure -- and took roughly SIX MINUTES.**\n\n```\n  the other 13 rows   ~15 s each      one session, a handful of debugger commands\n  `sweep 256`          ~6 min         one session, 768 debugger commands\n```\n\nIt walks all 256 offsets of the SYSASIC window with `r1=<addr>; pc=<code>; step 1` per\noffset. Each of those is a pty round-trip, and the round-trips dominate: the emulator work\nitself is 256 byte loads.\n\n**SO THE RULE NEEDS A SECOND TERM.** A probe's wall clock is roughly\n`sessions x boot_cost + total_debugger_commands x round_trip`, and the two terms are\ntraded against each other by DESIGN CHOICES that look unrelated: folding N rows into one\nsession removes N boots and keeps every command.\n\n**THE CHEAPER SHAPE, not built:** drive the walk from GUEST CODE -- a loop, or an unrolled\nblock written once with `put h` -- so one `step N` replaces hundreds of round-trips. Not\ndone today because hand-assembling SH is this project's documented encoding trap, and a\nwrong encoding yields a probe that measures the wrong thing while looking green.\n\n*Recorded rather than acted on: the six minutes buys a row that defends 256 offsets where\nnine were defended before, and the alternative risks a silently-wrong instrument.*\n"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24552:   "hold": "The 2026-08-29 adjudication moved the row-filter SPEC here from `probeslow` and added the fifth guard, so this row carries a design that has never been panelled. The owner already ruled the filter ships as its own round with its own panel and its own negative controls; that panel has not run.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24654:     "note": "[HELD AWAITING SEAT: codex]  The 2026-08-29 adjudication moved the row-filter SPEC here\nand added a FIFTH GUARD, so this row now carries a design that has never been panelled.\nThe owner has already ruled the filter 'ships as its own round with its own panel and its\nown negative controls' -- this stage is that panel, and it has not run.\n\nQUESTION FOR THE PANEL: **is the five-guard spec sufficient, and is SESSION-gating the\nright granularity?**\n\n  1  nonzero matches            4  the unset path's denominator is untouched\n  2  every named row exists     5  CONTROL ROWS ARE EXEMPT FROM FILTERING (or the control\n  3  denominator prints 'N of M'   line hard-fails on an empty control set)\n\nGuard 5 is the one that was DROPPED from the 08-28 spec and is the reason this is not a\nrubber stamp: `GDROM_DEF_CONTROL` prints OK on `all(rows)`, and **`all([])` is True**, so a\nfilter excluding every control row passes guards 1-4 while the control line reports OK\nhaving measured NOTHING.  The first use of the feature would manufacture a vacuous liveness\nclaim automatically.\n\nAnd the granularity constraint the panel should test rather than accept: selection must\ngate SESSIONS, because 36 of 39 are module-level -- a `row()`-level filter passes all five\nguards and saves nothing.  Merge BY NAME, never by vector position.\n\nDISCHARGE CRITERIA already set by the adjudication, so this stage cannot close on the\nsibling's work: session-gating selection, all five guards, and a MEASURED single-mutant\nre-score at sessions-proportional cost.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24702:     "note": "ASSESS by owner decision (panel_20260918_135107): BUILD-IT-NARROWER.  Helper + new-probe policy + strengthened contract in its own round with negative controls; existing module-level probes unchanged until a named conversion; do not fold into probeslow."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25076:     "note": "VERDICT: RATIFY WITH CHANGES (6).  The row's decode defect is closed BY CONSTRUCTION today: both ends of [0,95] refuse at compile time, a non-constant e hard-errors, the 21 events are byte-identical, a five-mutant table of the guard all killed (M1-M5).  Six changes: the BITMASK remedy is INERT (fix 1u <<; filed sysasicbitmask31); opus5 answered #466's pass 2 (7/9, so the hold is wrong); the two-ended guard is #469 4d7b2f41, not 29b450ce; #466/#468/#469 have no CHANGELOG blocks; the header's '352 values' is 288; no durable artifact of the guard's negative controls exists -- close FIXED with a detector: path once one does."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25210:   "state_note": "*** TWO MORE INSTANCES 2026-08-26, BACK TO BACK, AND THEY CARRY A CORRELATION THIS ROW DID NOT HAVE: THE BURN TRACKS BRIEF COMPLEXITY, NOT THE SEAT. ***\n\n    panel_20260826_090604    4,032 b   normal answer\n    panel_20260826_190055    2,845 b   normal answer\n    panel_20260826_222557  142,525 b   BURNED  done_reason=length  eval_count=65536\n    panel_20260826_230207  222,981 b   BURNED  done_reason=length  eval_count=65536\n\n**The two burns are on the day's two most COMPLEX briefs** -- one asking it to verify band arithmetic across three separate claims, one asking five questions spanning two rounds. The two clean runs were simpler briefs to the same seat, same transport, same day.\n\n*** THAT REFRAMES THE CLASS. *** The row recorded it as a SEAT property -- something to detect and work around. Today's evidence says it is at least partly a BRIEF property, which is actionable in a way a seat outage is not: **I can shorten the brief.** That matches the carrier's oldest lesson about packet-fed seats -- their behaviour is governed by the brief, and this project once spent DAYS misdiagnosing a brief defect as a seat outage.\n\n**AND THE SECOND BURN SHOWS WHAT IS BEING LOST, which the first instance could not.** Its output ends mid-sentence: *'So verdict likely NOT RATIFY due to #467. But maybe the question is from a panel'* -- **it had REACHED a verdict and was second-guessing itself when the budget ran out.** That is a substantive dissent destroyed by a token limit, not a seat with nothing to say. On that same panel the other dissenting seat found a LIVE DEFECT, so a lost dissent here is not hypothetical cost.\n\n**DETECTION IS ALREADY SOLVED AND SHOULD NOT BE RE-ARGUED:** `seat_verdicts.py` reports these as `SEAT DOWN -- spent its whole budget on hidden reasoning (length, N tokens)`, by reading `done_reason` rather than size. Both of today's burns were caught and named correctly.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OWED is the MITIGATION, and it is now cheap to state:** keep a packet-fed brief to ONE question with the facts inlined, rather than a multi-part audit. Splitting a complex brief across two firings costs one extra invocation and preserves the answer.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-25 on the #463 consistency audit. `deepseek-v4-pro:cloud` returned **`\"response\": \"\"` -- completely empty -- inside a 265,845-byte JSON object whose `thinking` field is 260,678 bytes, with `done_reason: 'length'` and `eval_count: 65536`. It spent its ENTIRE output budget on hidden reasoning and never emitted an answer. *** IT IS DISTINCT FROM ALL FOUR RECORDED CLASSES. *** Not a WALL (no quota text, no 403/429). Not the ECHO-ONLY shape (it echoes nothing). Not a REFUSED CONNECTION (the daemon served it fine, for 267 seconds). Not the shared-transport outage (the other three cloud seats answered normally in the same firing). *** AND IT DEFEATS EVERY SIZE-BASED CHECK IN THE WORST DIRECTION: at 265 KB it is the LARGEST file in its panel and would be scored the best answer. *** The carrier already warns 'verify the CONTENT, never the size; the failure mode is a big file' -- this is that lesson in a new variant, where the bulk is not an echoed brief but the model's own reasoning. **It was diagnosable only by accident**: `panel_ollama.py` does `body = resp.get('response') or json.dumps(resp)`, so an empty response falls back to dumping the raw JSON. Had it written the empty string, the file would have been 0 bytes and indistinguishable from a transport failure. That fallback should be made deliberate, and documented as the thing that preserves the diagnosis. THE FIX, and it is cheap: the seat check must parse any Ollama output that starts with `{`, and report SEAT DOWN when `response` is empty -- naming `done_reason` and `eval_count`, because `length` says 'budget exhausted' while `stop` would say something else entirely. A working checker exists (used to produce this row) and needs wiring into `panel.sh` and `seat_verdicts.py`. CONTRIBUTING CAUSE worth recording separately: the brief was the ENTIRE 18 KB round-block draft. The carrier's rule is to INLINE every fact for packet-fed seats, and that rule is sound -- but inlining an 18 KB artifact and asking for exhaustive cross-checking gives a thinking model enough to reason itself past its own budget. **Inline the facts; do not inline an entire document and ask for an audit of it.**  *** AND A SIXTH CLASS, MEASURED THE SAME SESSION on the matrix-review panel: THE SEAT'S OWN TRANSPORT DIED MID-ANSWER. *** `agy` returned 293 bytes reading `I have launched the topic cluster analysis script ... Waiting for background script completion.` followed by `Error: the connection to the agent was interrupted before the response finished: subscriber fell behind updates, stalled for 6s`. It is none of the five: no quota text (not a wall), it connected and worked for minutes (not a refused connection), it echoed nothing, it emitted a real partial answer rather than an empty response inside vast thinking, and the other seats on the same panel were unaffected (not the shared-Agent-tool outage). **The distinguishing shape: the seat DELEGATED to a background script and its own streaming connection dropped while it waited.** Two consequences. (1) The keyword set every seat check in this project uses -- quota / rate limit / balance / 403 / 429 / refused -- does NOT contain `interrupted`, `stalled`, or `subscriber fell behind`, so this scored as `answered`. Widened. (2) **It is worth RE-FIRING, unlike a wall**: a wall is the provider refusing and you wait it out; this is a dropped connection and the work simply did not survive. The re-fire rule the carrier already states for walled seats applies here with better odds.  *** THE CONTRIBUTING CAUSE THIS ROW FILED WAS MEASURED FALSE THE SAME SESSION. *** It said the trigger was inlining an entire 18 KB document and asking for an exhaustive audit of it, and prescribed 'inline the facts; do not inline a document'. **A SECOND burn then happened on a brief a fraction of that size** -- 11 ledger rows, truncated narratives, ~10 KB -- with the identical signature: `response: \"\"`, `done_reason: 'length'`, `eval_count: 65536`, and 275,778 bytes of `thinking`. Larger thinking than the first burn, on a much smaller brief. **So SIZE IS NOT THE CAUSE**, and the prescription derived from it was wrong. What the two share is the TASK SHAPE, not the input size: both asked for an EXHAUSTIVE PER-ITEM AUDIT with several independent sub-questions per item (four categories over a document; four questions over each of eleven rows). The seat answered a 6.5 KB pass-2 REVIEW brief normally in the same session -- one subject, one set of questions. **The working hypothesis is now COMBINATORIAL: N items x M orthogonal questions, where the seat appears to explore the cross-product before emitting anything.** That is a hypothesis, not a measurement, and it is recorded as such -- the honest state is that the cause is NOT ESTABLISHED and the first stated cause is REFUTED. OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, which does not depend on the cause: **deepseek is unreliable for per-item audit tasks and reliable for single-subject review**, so route it accordingly rather than by brief size. And the detection rule is unchanged and now doubly warranted: parse the JSON, report SEAT DOWN on an empty `response`, and never score by file size -- both burns produced the LARGEST file in their panel.  *** AND THE TEST THAT LOOKED LIKE A CONFIRMATION IS CONFOUNDED -- CORRECTED IMMEDIATELY RATHER THAN LEFT STANDING. *** deepseek ANSWERED a #463 research-stage brief cleanly (1,724 bytes, a real dissent) after burning on two audits, and that was momentarily reported as confirming the task-shape hypothesis over the size hypothesis. **It confirms nothing.** The brief was 3,614 bytes -- SMALLER than both that burned it (19,395 and 10,408) -- so it varies BOTH suspected causes at once and cannot discriminate between them. The honest state of the evidence: burns at 19,395 (per-item audit) and 10,408 (per-item audit); clean answers at 8,583 (single-subject review) and 3,614 (single-subject research). **Size and shape are perfectly correlated across all four data points**, so nothing here separates them. A discriminating test exists and is cheap -- a LARGE single-subject brief, or a SMALL per-item one -- and until someone runs it the cause remains NOT ESTABLISHED. *Recorded because the first stated cause was already refuted once; a second unearned cause would be the same defect with better manners.*\n\n**RESIDUAL-REMAINS.**  `a4d054d` recorded occurrences and brief mitigation, and recognition of the\nempty-response signature already existed before it.  **Exhausting the reasoning budget remains an\noperational failure; shortening briefs is mitigation with no demonstrated general prevention.**"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25296:   "state_note": "*** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-26: NO CHECK. The proposed WARN was REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT, and THREE STATEMENTS I put in this row yesterday are WRONG RECORDS, corrected here. ***\n\n**(a) 'So today's `:733` passed GREEN' is FALSE, and it was the load-bearing claim of my diagnosis.** Section D's regex is `\\b[a-z0-9_]+\\.(c|h)\\b:[0-9]+` -- it requires a FULL `file.c:` prefix. The stale citation is BARE: `\":720 is :733, ...\"`. VERIFIED: D's extraction from `5b5089d` yields `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:307/338/515/611/722/889/922` and no 733. **It did not pass green -- it was never looked at.** That is a materially different defect from the one I recorded.\n\n**(b) 'never by a gate' contradicts this row's own instance 5**, which the row says section X caught -- and the CHANGELOG headlines it *'The last thing a gate caught was a citation that decayed by MULTIPLYING.'*\n\n**(c) 'MEASURED: fires on the real instance' is true at COMMIT granularity only, not CITATION granularity** -- the rule fires on that commit, but not on the citation that went stale.\n\n*** THE PROPOSED WARN FAILS ITS OWN NEGATIVE CONTROLS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. *** Measured over 429 commits since 2026-07-20: it fires on **57 commits / 263 citations (~11 per week)** and its **recall on the eight recorded instances is ZERO**, because every recorded instance is a BARE `:NNN` that D's extraction cannot see. On the motivating round itself it emits 15 warns, none of them the stale one. **A checker policing the visible minority of a class while blind to its dominant form IS the check-that-cannot-fail shape -- the exact defect I had attributed to section D.**\n\n*** AND THE CLASS DOES NOT CLEAR THE `countderiver` BAR. *** The adjudicator hunted with denominators -- 135 'stale' mentions in the CHANGELOG, 19 citation-flavoured, every region read -- and **no stale line citation has ever consumed a decision.** The records say so themselves: *'Nothing the citations said was wrong -- only the numbers had moved'* (#408); *'The rows were right, the table number sent a reader to the wrong page'* (#457).\n\n**AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL, which is why this class is WEAKER than the counts one that was already declined: NO GATE, PROBE OR SCRIPT IN THIS TREE READS A LINE CITATION FUNCTIONALLY** -- gates key on grep anchors and content. A citation is consumed only by a READER, and a reader using one opens the file, so a drifted number SELF-REFUTES AT THE POINT OF USE. A carried count does not. That is why all eight were caught by a seat or a re-read.\n\n*** THE REMEDY HAS BEEN ADOPTED FOUR SEPARATE TIMES, EACH ROUND-LOCAL, NEVER CARRIED -- the `countderiver` 'already shipping, uncodified' shape exactly. *** The #340-era round (*'replaced by symbol-anchored citations instead of a third set of numbers'*), #408 (*'every citation now names the construct'*), the fourth-instance note (*'the arms are named by what they ARE'*), and my own grep-anchor conversion today. **Section D1's own header already says 'prefer naming the CONSTRUCT over the line'.** The class keeps re-biting because each adoption stayed inside one round's records.\n\n**THE RULE, NOW STANDING RATHER THAN ROUND-LOCAL:** line numbers only ACROSS files, with the qualifier ON the citation; SAME-FILE references positional or by CONSTRUCT NAME; durable cross-file references as VERIFIED-UNIQUE grep anchors. **It removes the measured population -- 7 of 8 instances were same-file or bare forms the rule eliminates outright** -- and it is structure in the sense that matters: it lives where citations are WRITTEN and fails at the point of USE, because an anchor that matches nothing is loud.\n\n**PRE-SPECIFIED ESCALATION, so nobody re-argues it: the first MEASURED decision consumed by a stale line citation -- a fix mis-aimed by one, a gate passing falsely, a seat verifying green against wrong content -- converts the form rule into an anchor-verification check bounded to ADDED citations, forward-only from a cutoff. It audits nothing retroactively and builds nothing until that trigger fires.**\n\n*** TWO MORE INSTANCES 2026-08-26, BRINGING THE RECORDED COUNT TO EIGHT -- AND THE SECOND IS THE WORST SHAPE THIS CLASS HAS PRODUCED. ***\n\n**(7) A CORRECTION NOTE WENT STALE INSIDE THE COMMIT THAT WROTE IT.** #465's probe gained a note reading *\":720 is :733, :475-480 is :486-494\"*. Re-derived: `d->cond = idata;` is at **:736** -- :733 was true at `4d05cf0` and was invalidated by `5b5089d`, **the very commit carrying the note** -- and the tail is :491-496, which was NEVER right, since even at `4d05cf0` it began at :488. Fourth generation of drift on one file: pre-fix numbers, invalidated; a note written against the previous commit, invalidated again.\n\n**(8) AND THE FIX FOR (7) WAS DEFEATED BY ITS OWN EXPLANATION.** The repair stripped the `panel_` prefix from a backlog list so the names could not false-cite -- then wrote the full name in the sentence EXPLAINING the circularity, so it false-cited anyway. Caught by the count moving 78 -> 77 only after a second pass.\n\n*** THE REMEDY IS NOW MEASURED, NOT PROPOSED: RE-KEY TO GREP ANCHORS. *** Six drifted carrier citations were converted to greppable tokens earlier the same day (`grep -n 'LOGDIR/mutation'`, `'rm -rf \"$dir\"'`, the \"honour $LOGDIR\" comment, gate_hygiene's own \"READS THE RAW PTY LOGS ON PURPOSE\" header) and **each was verified to resolve UNIQUELY** -- because a key that matches nothing is worse than the stale number it replaced, which I then proved by shipping an anchor that matched ZERO and having to fix it.\n\n*** AND THE DIAGNOSIS THE ROW WAS MISSING: THE MECHANISM ALREADY EXISTS AND IS BLIND BY CONSTRUCTION. *** `precommit_check.sh` section D extracts every added `file.c:NNN` and checks the file has at least that many lines. **Its own success message says so: \"cited $c exists (READ THE LINE -- existence is not correctness)\".** So `:733` passed GREEN today -- the file has more than 733 lines -- while the content had moved to `:736`. That is the third check-that-cannot-fail-for-its-class found in one day.\n\n**A BOUNDED STRENGTHENING IS AVAILABLE, and it is verified against the real instance:** for each added `file.ext:NNN`, if THAT FILE IS ALSO MODIFIED BY THE SAME DIFF, warn -- the commit's own hunks move the lines, so the citation is at risk BY CONSTRUCTION. **MEASURED: `git show --name-only 5b5089d` lists `src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`, so the rule fires on today's case.** It must be a WARN and not a FAIL: correctness is undecidable, and a self-citation into an edited file is sometimes right. Cost is a few lines beside the existing D loop.\n\nNOT BUILT -- put to the adjudicator, because it changes a checker every commit runs and owes negative controls in both directions (a same-file citation into an edited file must WARN; one into an untouched file must stay silent).\n\nThe carrier already states the rule (*'a line number in a comment is invalidated by your own edits to that file; re-grep every same-file citation AFTER the last edit'*). **#463 broke it SIX TIMES while knowing it**, which is the argument for mechanising rather than restating: (1) `:200,:203` for the debug() calls, stale when the #463 comment was first inserted; (2) the SAME cite stale AGAIN after the comment was trimmed three lines shorter -- **a correction that went stale a second time**; (3) `:230` for `default:` in the round block, found by a records-audit seat; (4) `:144-152` for the mask_13 handler in the probe docstring; (5) four BARE artifact names with seven candidates each -- citation decay by NAMESPACE rather than by movement, caught by section X; (6) `:227` for `default:` going stale inside the very edit that corrected a different overstatement. *** THE SHAPE IS ALWAYS THE SAME AND ALWAYS INVISIBLE: the text does not change and stops being true. *** Section D checks that ADDED citations EXIST; it cannot see a citation that still resolves to a line of the wrong content, and existence is not correctness -- its own output says so. PROPOSED CHECK, cheap and mechanical: for each `:NNN` in a source comment, require an adjacent ANCHOR TOKEN (a short quoted fragment of the cited line) and verify the token is actually ON line NNN. That converts every citation into a self-verifying pair and catches movement, trimming and namespace decay alike. Cost: one token per citation, and the round that wrote six stale ones would have caught all six at commit time.  *** A SEVENTH INSTANCE, AND IT PRODUCED A BETTER FIX THAN THE CHECKER THIS ROW PROPOSED. *** Adding seven lines about transaction closure moved `default:` again, and `:229` came to point at `d->mask_9[r] = idata;`. Caught only because the citation was re-derived immediately after the edit -- the manual discipline works exactly as long as someone remembers it, which is the definition of a check that eventually does not happen. **THE REAL FIX IS CHEAPER THAN AN ANCHOR-TOKEN CHECKER: a SAME-FILE citation does not need a line number.** `default:` is in the same function, below the comment; `the default: arm below` and `the struct above` are unambiguous, need no maintenance, and CANNOT DECAY -- there is nothing there to go stale. Applied: all same-file `:NNN` citations in this comment are now positional, and the only numbers left are CROSS-FILE -- and the ENUMERATION here named four when there are SIX (`core/debugmsg.c:375`, `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:790-798`, `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:72`, `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:74`, `configure:312-314`); the CLAIM that all remaining are cross-file is true and zero bare `:NNN` remain, but the list was not exhaustive -- which edits to THIS file cannot move. **THE RULE THAT FALLS OUT: cite a line number only ACROSS files; within a file, cite by POSITION or by the name of the thing.** That removes the entire class from same-file comments at zero cost, and leaves the checker worth building for the cross-file remainder, which is a much smaller surface -- seven instances this round were ALL same-file.  *** AN EIGHTH INSTANCE, AND THIS ONE REFUTES A JUDGEMENT RATHER THAN AN EDIT. *** After converting every same-file citation to a positional reference, two bare cross-file numbers remained, each following a QUALIFIED cite to the same file. I inspected them and judged the idiom clear enough -- `core/debugmsg.c:384-390, against debug()'s at :375`. **A pass-2 seat then read `:375` as a SAME-FILE citation and reported claim 3 as failing**, with a second seat independently flagging one `:NNN` it 'cannot prove cross-file'. **The judgement was refuted by demonstration, which is stronger than any argument I had for it**: the question was never whether the idiom CAN be read correctly but whether it IS, and two of six readers did not. Fixed by repeating the filename rather than relying on proximity. THE RULE SHARPENS: **a citation's qualifier must be ON the citation, not merely NEAR it.** Proximity is not qualification -- it depends on the reader carrying context across a line break, which is exactly what a reader skimming a 60-line comment does not do."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25670:   "state_note": "*** FILED 2026-08-26 BY THE CARRIER EXEMPLAR AUDIT. The FALSE MESSAGE is already fixed; what is filed is the missing BEHAVIOUR. *** `panel.sh` printed **'re-firing once (a wall can be minutes long) ...'** and then `return 2`. Nothing consumed that status and nothing was ever relaunched. *** WHY THIS IS WORSE THAN A STALE CITATION, and it is the reason it was fixed the same hour it was found: *** a stale citation misleads a reader who chooses to follow it. This printed a claim of ACTION, to the operator, at the exact moment a seat was lost -- so the one person who could have re-fired it by hand was told it had already happened. **It is the failure panel.sh itself warns about sixty lines earlier: 'a documented check that cannot fail, which is worse than no check because it reassures.'** DONE: the message is now an INSTRUCTION ('RE-FIRE THIS SEAT ONCE BY HAND ... NOT done automatically') rather than a false report, and the carrier's 'both applied' claim is corrected in place. OWED, and deliberately not done in the same edit: **the automatic retry.** It is a behaviour change to the script EVERY panel runs, so it owes two negative controls before it ships -- (i) prove the retry actually fires on a walled seat, and (ii) prove a seat that ANSWERED is not retried. Without (ii) the retry could double every panel's cost silently, which is the mirror failure. Ranking: harness coverage, below live defects. But the value is concrete -- the carrier records a real instance where a walled seat was recovered only because the owner happened to notice it available in their own UI, which is precisely the human-noticing this harness exists to stop depending on.\n\n**SEVERITY SET LOW, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: the false message is already corrected; what remains is the unbuilt automatic re-fire, which owes two negative controls.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25671:   "note": "*** panel.sh printed `re-firing once (a wall can be minutes long) ...` AND RE-FIRED NOTHING. ***  Every caller is a bare `check \"name\" \"$file\"` whose exit status is discarded, so the `return 2` never had a consumer.  **Worse than a stale citation**: it printed a CLAIM OF ACTION at the exact moment a seat was lost, so the one person who could re-fire it by hand was told it had already been done -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape, reassuring rather than merely wrong.  **DONE (2026-08-30):** the message is an INSTRUCTION and now also says WHERE the command lives.  It stopped at *re-fire this seat by hand* and did not say how, leaving the operator to find one of SEVEN differently-shaped launches 200 lines up -- at the worst possible moment to go looking.  It **points rather than duplicates**: copying the invocation into the message would create a second copy that drifts from the launch, and this project has already carried a FALSE SEAT ROSTER for days for exactly that reason.  Section J holds the carrier's seat table byte-identical to its tracked copy, so a pointer there cannot go stale in silence.  CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, 9 checks, `WALLMSG_PASS`: a walled seat gets the instruction, the pointer, and `$BRIEF`/`$f` EXPANDED TO REAL VALUES; a healthy seat gets none of it and scores `answered`.  *The control's first version used a 59-byte fixture, hit the SIZE branch before the wall branch, and tested nothing -- it would have reported a pass.  The fixtures are now padded past that threshold and the first check asserts the wall branch was reached at all.*  *** STILL OPEN, AND THIS IS THE REMAINING WORK: AUTOMATIC RE-FIRING IS NOT DONE. ***  It cannot be bolted on -- the seven seat launches are inline, in seven different shapes, 200 lines above the checks, so re-firing needs each refactored into a re-runnable unit.  That is a behaviour change to the script EVERY PANEL RUNS and owes two negative controls of its own: prove the retry fires on a walled seat, and prove a seat that ANSWERED is not retried.  **Its own harness round.**  Recorded here rather than half-built, because a partial auto-refire is precisely the class this row is about.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25744:   "state_note": "*** FIXED AS #471, COMPLETE RATHER THAN PARTIAL -- which is what the farmed design demanded: 'land a complete path-aware fix WITH an explicit non-shipping classification and the controls above. Do not land only re-key + walk.' ***\n\n**RESULT: 43/47 detectors gated (was 42/46), 60 artefacts seen (was 57), selftest 21/21 mutants red (was 18/18).** The blind spot that let `#465` ship a detector no gate ran is closed, with controls that catch its return.\n\n**FOUR COORDINATED CHANGES, and each was found by the previous one failing:**\n  1. dropped the `\"/\" not in f` filter -- `git ls-files` already yields paths RELATIVE to `regress/`, so top-level files stay bare basenames and **all 57 existing MANIFEST keys keep matching unchanged**. Only nested files gain a path key. That made the re-key far smaller than 'convert 57 entries'.\n  2. the on-disk fallback `listdir` -> `walk`, because a check whose coverage depends on which branch it took is the shape this file refuses.\n  3. **`exec_sites()` path-keyed** -- a nested invocation was being classed as EXTERNAL and IGNORED, so the detector `#465` wired by hand still reported UNWIRED once the scan could see it. The design predicted this exactly: *'provided exec_sites() is path-keyed too'*.\n  4. **the selftest's OWN COPY was flat**, so every nested MANIFEST entry became a GHOST inside it and reddened the unmutated control. **The same blind spot one level up, inside the harness that tests for it** -- a flat copy cannot exercise a recursive scan.\n\n**`CLASS_RESEARCH` earned itself immediately.** Without it the two preserved instruments (`mism_probe.py`, `mutate.py`) face a false choice: MISFILED as tools because of the `_probe.py` suffix, or UNWIRED as detectors owing a gate they should never have. They are EVIDENCE, not tests. *And I created that conflict this morning by preserving them under the `preserveinstrument` rule -- my fix for one row blocking the fix for another.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST FINDING IS ABOUT THE HARNESS, NOT THE CHECKER: M14's FIRST VERSION MEASURED GREEN. *** I aimed it at the `git ls-files` branch -- but **the selftest copy is not a git repo**, so the header reads `[on-disk (git could not answer)]` and the WALK FALLBACK is what actually runs. **A mutant aimed at a code path the test never takes proves nothing.** Re-aimed at the fallback, it reddens correctly. **Consequence worth carrying: the git branch is UNTESTED by this selftest, and it is the branch that runs in production.**\n\nTHREE MORE SELF-INFLICTED TRAPS, all of which the file had already solved:\n  * `\\\\n` became a REAL NEWLINE inside a string literal -- the backslash trap, **fourth instance today**. Fixed with `chr(10)`.\n  * my COMMENT quoted the mutant's own find-text, so `_st_apply` saw it twice and REFUSED -- the harness correctly rejecting an ambiguous mutant, and the **third time today** that documentation broke a tool which greps for what it documents.\n  * the file already had **`_lit()`** for exactly that: *'Join fragments, so the assembled search string appears nowhere in this source.'*\n\n*The pattern across all four: the file had already solved each problem, and I re-derived each solution the hard way. `_lit()`, the `chr()` constants, the mutant-applies guard -- every one was built by someone hitting exactly what I hit.*\n\n*** FIX DESIGNED 2026-08-26 BY A FARMED codex JOB -- AND IT FOUND THAT THE NAIVE FIX WOULD BREAK SOMETHING I CREATED EARLIER THE SAME DAY. ***\n\n**THE NAIVE FIX -- re-key `MANIFEST`/`EXEMPT` from basename to relative path, swap `listdir` for `walk` -- MUST NOT LAND ALONE.** Recursion immediately pulls in `regress/artifacts/gdrompionull/opus_p2_20260826/mism_probe.py`, which is an ARCHIVED RESEARCH instrument, not a shipping detector. Its `_probe.py` suffix means classifying it as `tool` trips the checker's MISFILED rule, while classifying it as a detector demands a gate or an exemption it should never have. **It is a file with no honest class.**\n\n*** AND I PUT IT THERE. *** It was preserved this morning under the `preserveinstrument` rule -- filed one hour earlier, applied on its first opportunity. **My fix for one row created the conflict that blocks the fix for another**, and neither row could see it: the population is exactly TWO nested probes, the gated detector and the instrument I archived.\n\n**SO THE PROJECT'S OWN 'do not alarm on the rest of the class' OBJECTION APPLIES HERE, and the seat said so explicitly** -- the naive fix would force an archival file into a false choice between MISFILED and UNWIRED. **The recommendation is all-or-nothing: land a complete path-aware fix WITH an explicit `research`/`nonshipping` classification, or do not land it. Do not land only re-key + `walk`.**\n\nTHREE SELF-TEST CONTROLS SPECIFIED, for the file's existing 18-mutant `--selftest` harness:\n  * **NESTED GREEN** -- the unmodified copied tree must show the nested detector as RUN by its gate, not IGNORED or UNWIRED.\n  * **NESTED RED** -- delete its real invocation from the copied gate; require exit 1 naming the FULL RELATIVE PATH as UNWIRED.\n  * **COLLISION** -- add a second classified detector with the SAME BASENAME in a different subdirectory, gate only the original, require the second to remain UNWIRED. **A regression back to basename keying would incorrectly turn BOTH green** -- which is the silent failure the re-key exists to prevent.\n\n*Checked: NO same-basename collision exists in `regress/` today, so that hazard is PROSPECTIVE. The existing duplicate-literal guard still works with path keys, provided `exec_sites()` is path-keyed too.*\n\n**DISPOSITION: still worth fixing** -- the seat was asked whether it might not be, and answered that `#465`'s escape is direct evidence the blind spot matters even though today's detector is now wired by hand. But it is a LARGER change than the row implied, and shipping half of it is worse than shipping none.\n\n*** THIS IS THE STRUCTURAL LANDING SPOT FOR #465's HEADLINE LESSON, AND THE ROUND MISSED IT. *** #465 found that its detector was wired into NO GATE, recorded 'a detector no gate runs is not a detector', and discharged it by a HUMAN GREP. **The mechanical check for exactly that class already exists and was BLIND BY SCOPE** -- `gdrompionull` lived at `regress/artifacts/gdrompionull/`, one level down, where the checker cannot look. **So the round wrote its lesson as prose while the structure that should have carried it sat one directory away, silently not covering the case.** That is the carrier's structure-first rule failing in its own terms, and it is a CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL for a whole subtree -- the class this project named twice today (`panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire, `grok models` as a tier check). **EXPOSURE IS ZERO TODAY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT FIXED IN-ROUND:** 1 of 57 tracked `regress/**.py` sat below top level, and it is now gated by hand. The defect is latent, not live. THE FIX, as the seat scoped it: re-key `MANIFEST`/`EXEMPT` from BASENAME to RELATIVE PATH and replace `os.listdir` with `os.walk`. **It owes negative controls in both directions** -- prove a subdirectory detector with no gate is now RED, and prove a gated one stays GREEN -- and the file already carries an 18-mutant `--selftest` harness to hang them on. A basename->path re-key can silently collide (two files with the same basename in different directories), so that needs its own row. *A checker whose docstring overclaims its scope is worse than one that admits a limit: the docstring is what a future round reads when deciding whether the class is already covered.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25818:   "state_note": "*** FILED 2026-08-27 as the successor to [[m8sarseq]], carrying its dissent VERBATIM as provenance. The spec below was MEASURED by an opus5 assess on 2026-08-26 and existed in exactly ONE place -- that row's entry text -- until this row was created. ***\n\n**THE MUTANT (M1):** `dev_m8820x.c:108`, `cmmu[d->cmmu_nr]->reg` -> `cmmu[0]->reg`. **One identifier.** Verified at that line today: it reads `uint32_t *regs = cpu->cd.m88k.cmmu[d->cmmu_nr]->reg;`.\n\n**IT SWEEPS BOTH SHIPPED DETECTORS CLEAN: 79/79** -- `diff_m8820x` 46 rows plus `diff_m8invread` 33 rows, denominators self-reported by the harnesses. Each file carries deliberate `@@SELFCHECK@@` FAIL lines as negative controls, so a small non-zero raw grep IS the green baseline. *(I flagged a discrepancy here and there is none: a grep counts THREE markers per file, but one is the explanatory comment -- *\"THE PROBE ROWS ARE NAMED @@SELFCHECK@@ SO THEY CANNOT BE MISTAKEN FOR A ROW\"* -- and the other two are the deliberately-mismatched rows `check(\"@@SELFCHECK@@/str\", \"a\", \"b\")` and `check(\"@@SELFCHECK@@/num\", \"1\", \"2\")`. **The entry's two is correct; my three counted the comment.** Same substring trap as counting `exit(1)` matches that are prose.)*\n\n*** THE CONTROLS ARE WHAT MAKE THIS EVIDENCE RATHER THAN AN ASSERTION, AND THEY SHOW THE BLINDNESS IS STRUCTURAL: ***\n  * **M3** (`sar = 0`) reddens D1/D3 -- the harness CAN see a CONSUMER-side SAR defect;\n  * **M4** (drop the SAR store) reddens E7/E8/G6/G7 -- it CAN see a PRODUCER-side one.\n**Both ends are covered; only the SEAM is blind.** The reason is mechanical: every SCR-flush row runs after `fresh()`, which memsets the device so `cpu_nr = cmmu_nr = 0`, and issues from cpu0 -- and at (0,0) every candidate base for `regs` coincides.\n\n*** M1 IS A LIVE-DEFECT CLASS ON THE SHIPPED RIG, NOT A SHAPE PIN -- and the load-bearing facts were re-verified from source today rather than trusted from a harness comment: ***\n  * `dev_luna88k.c:1041-1042` calls `add_cmmu_for_cpu(devinit, 0, ...)` AND `(devinit, 1, ...)`, so **both CMMUs are registered on every boot**;\n  * `dev_m8820x.c:541-543` -- *`// cmmu nr 0 = instruction, 1 = data.`* then `d->cmmu_nr = devinit->addr2 & 1;` -- so **`cmmu_nr = 1` is guest-addressable on every luna88k boot**;\n  * the data CMMU takes **318,457 of 613,342 SAR writes (51.9%)**.\n**So under M1 roughly HALF the machine's PATC flushes never dispatch at all** -- the silently-under-invalidating TLB that the file's own comment calls *'not a cosmetic gap'*.\n\n*(M2, the `(cpu, d)` swap, is by contrast a SHAPE PIN: the booting rig is single-CPU.)*\n\n*** THE FIX IS THREE ROWS, EACH WITH ITS OWN MEASURED KILL, NONE DECORATION: ***\n```\n  K1   matching entry dropped            reddens under M1 / M1b\n  K2   non-matching entry retained       reddens under M5\n  K3   flush from a NON-OWNER cpu        reddens under M2\n```\n**THE TWO AXES ARE INDEPENDENT: K1 does NOT catch M2, and K3 does NOT catch M1.** The original filing asked for *'cmmu_nr 1 AND from a non-owner CPU'*; the measurement shows both halves are load-bearing and **neither subsumes the other** -- sharper than the filing knew.\n\n**WHERE IT GOES:** `regress/diff_m8invread.c` (778 lines today), which already has the two-cpu / two-CMMU fixture. **~20 lines.** *The entry says a prototype was committed to the artifact dir; it is NOT in `regress/artifacts/`, so the prototype must be rebuilt from this spec.*\n\n**RANKING: coverage debt on a closed round whose SHIPPED CODE IS CORRECT** -- below live defects, per the severity rule. But it is a 20-line row spec with three measured kills rather than a paragraph, which is why it earns a row instead of a note.\n\n*An L4 was found and fixed in passing by the same seat: `diff_m8invread.c:639` cited `dev_luna88k.c:1001-1004` for the both-CMMUs-every-boot claim, and that range is an INTERRUPT-TEMPLATE LOOP -- claim true, citation ~70 lines off. Corrected to `:926-957 (add_cmmu_for_cpu)`, verified by reading before the edit.*\n\n**SEVERITY SET MED, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: structural blindness with a MEASURED mutant: one identifier sweeps both offline detectors.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27016:     "note": "*** MY MARKER SWEEP MANUFACTURED A SEAT ANSWER ON THIS ROW, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE OF 30\nDEFANGS THAT DID.  Restored 2026-08-29. ***\n\n**The mechanism is worth more than the instance.**  Marker entries carry a `seat` field,\nand tools classify an entry by its NOTE CONTENT.  Once defanged, the note no longer\nmatches the held literal -- so the entry stops being a marker and **falls through into the\nseat tally as an ANSWER**.  For 29 of 30 defangs that is harmless, because the seat really\ndid answer that stage.  Here it was not: the defanged entry became the sole evidence that\nfable5 answered the REGRESS stage, and fable5 never has.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE: my first sweep was ROW-SCOPED.**  It asked whether the named seat answered\n*anywhere on the row*, so a RESEARCH answer discharged a REGRESS hold.  regress is\nFABLE-ONLY by standing directive, which is exactly why cross-phase credit is wrong here.\nMy second sweep used same-stage matching and did not repeat it.\n\n**AND MY OWN CHECK FOR THIS FAILED IN THE OTHER DIRECTION.**  Hunting phantoms, I filtered\nreal entries with `\"HELD\" not in note` -- which excluded two GENUINE answers that merely\nQUOTE the word (one of them discussing held markers), turning a true finding of ONE into a\nclaimed THREE.  **Third time today that literal has fooled a check of mine.**  The seat's\ncount was right; mine was inflated.\n\nFILED, not done: entry-counting tools should exclude the defanged literal from seat\ntallies, or future defangs should be written as SEATLESS entries.  Either kills the class;\nboth are tooling changes owing negative controls."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27162:   "state_note": "*** FOUND BY THE codex SEAT ON `#473`'s PASS 2, and confirmed by grepping both files. ***\n\nSection P (`check_bugfile_sync.py`) fails when an OPEN LEDGER ROW has no entry in\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`. **Nothing checks the other direction.**  On 2026-08-27 four rows --\n`wdcaddtoinbufbound`, `wdcrearmwalk`, `fbclearidempotent`, `fbclearaliases` -- existed as\nprose entries with no ledger row at all.\n\n**Why that direction matters more than it looks.** The ledger is the single source of truth\nand `matrix.html` renders it, so a row filed only in prose is **invisible to every tool in\nthe pipeline**: it gets no seat coverage tracking, no stage panel, no dashboard line, and\nsection H never asks whether it ran a panel.  *A row that exists only in prose is filed in\nthe sense that a reader could find it and unfiled in every sense a tool cares about.*\n\nThis is the unread-review class one level up: the work is recorded, and the machinery that\nexists to act on records cannot see it.\n\n**THE CHECK IS THE SAME SHAPE AS P AND SHOULD REUSE IT** -- scan `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` for\nrow-id tokens (the backticked ``id`` convention already used), and fail when one names\nno ledger row.  **Dated-cutoff and forward-only**, like `check_bugfile_sync.py` itself: a\nrule that retro-fails the whole file blocks the next commit on reconstruction nobody\nscheduled, and a rule that does that gets switched off.\n\n**NOT BUILT YET, deliberately.**  It is a behaviour change to the script every commit runs,\nand it owes two negative controls: prove it reds on a bugfile-only row, and prove it stays\ngreen when the ledger row is added.  The four instances are already fixed by hand.\n\n**SEVERITY SET LOW, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: a one-directional records check; the gap admits drift, no measured instance yet.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27711:     "note": "*** A SEAT OUTCOME THE TAXONOMY DOES NOT NAME: INVESTIGATION WITHOUT CONCLUSION. ***\n\n462 KB, 7240 lines, 98% novel, and NO VERDICT ANYWHERE.  The answer TERMINATES INSIDE A FILE\nIT WAS QUOTING -- its last line is the closing brace of a scratch generator of mine that it\nhad opened to read.  It did real work: 1111 lines of grep/file output across the repo.  It\nsimply never landed.\n\nIT IS NONE OF THE RECORDED CLASSES.  No quota text, so not a WALL.  98% novel, so not the\necho-only shape.  Substantial output, so not the minimax-style stall.  A CLI seat, so not the\ndeepseek thinking-burn (`done_reason: length`).  Not a refused connection.\n\n*** AND seat_verdicts.py FILED IT AS 'answered, NO VERDICT LINE (format miss)'. ***  That is\nthe daemon case's conflation again: a seat that produced NO ANSWER recorded as a seat with\nbad FORMATTING.  Scoring it 'answered' credits a verdict that does not exist, and this panel\nwould read 6 answered when the honest count is 5 plus one unfinished.\n\nA CHECKABLE SIGNATURE EXISTS AND IS CHEAP: does the answer END INSIDE A QUOTED FILE OR CODE\nBLOCK rather than with a sentence?  A finished answer ends in prose.  This one ends in `}`.\nThat is a different question from 'is there a verdict line', which is a FORMAT question --\nand the two are being answered by one check.\n\nNOT GENERALISED FROM ONE INSTANCE, deliberately: the same seat produced a real verdict with a\nreal finding on #484's pass 2 (566 KB) and a substantive answer on #485 (2.2 MB, the landisk\nunmapped-write finding at its tail).  So this is not 'codex is broken'.  It is one firing\nthat ran out mid-investigation, and the value of recording it is the SIGNATURE, not the\nseat.\n\nFILED, not fixed: teaching seat_verdicts.py the ends-inside-a-quote discriminator is a change\nto the tool every panel runs, and it owes two negative controls -- prove it fires on this\nfile, and prove it does NOT fire on #484's codex answer, which ends in prose and has a\nverdict.  That is its own small round."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27822:   "state_note": "[2026-10-02] COMMITS FIELD LEFT EMPTY by #557, on purpose: c7b32355 is the records commit that RECORDED the closure; the closure was a local build-tree back-fill, not a tree change, so the field stays empty on purpose (a populated field for work that did not ship is the opposite failure, and this ledger has one on record: ns16550).  *** AND ITS FIRST RUN PAID FOR IT THE SAME HOUR. ***\n\n#489's bare-deletion mutant under the shipped 19-row detector: **7 x heap-buffer-overflow, every one a WRITE**, five `0 bytes after 407-byte region` and two `0 bytes after 1-byte region`.  The stack runs from `main` through `sh_run_instr` and a guest `mov.w` store into `handle_command`, onto a buffer from `alloc_data`'s `malloc(guest cnt)`.  `regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/asan_m0_20260828/`.\n\n*The FIRST attempt reported `6/19, zero reports` -- noise, not a negative.  LeakSanitizer fires at exit on every session and ASan's default exit code is 1, so every survival row read as a death while the genuine reports went to a pty the probe does not retain.  The tell was `ok surv` reading exit1 on the LEGAL length, which the mutant cannot affect.*\n\n*** FIXED 2026-08-28.  THE TREE BUILDS, RUNS, AND HAS ASAN LINKED. ***\n\nFour build attempts, each peeling one layer, every layer INVISIBLE to the propagation gate:\n  1. stale HEADERS   -- diskimage.c vs a diskimage.h missing nr_of_tracks, DISKIMAGE_MAX_SPT,\n                        struct diskimage_track.  Fixed by back-filling 474 .c/.h from SEC.\n  2. stale GENERATORS -- new sources against an OLD makeautodev.sh emitted\n                        `int devinit_Makefile.skel(...)`, a glob expansion the script's own\n                        comment warns about.  Fixed by syncing 16 scripts/.index/Makefile.skel.\n  3. stale GENERATED MAKEFILE -- OBJS predating dev_ne2000.c (a SEC-only file), giving\n                        `undefined reference to devinit_ne2000`.  Fixed by re-running\n                        ./configure with CFLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined\n                        -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g'; COPTIM verified to still carry the\n                        sanitizer afterwards, and the 15 prior Makefiles were backed up first.\n  4. none -- it built.\n\n*** MAKE_RC REPORTED 0 ON EVERY FAILED BUILD. ***  Caught only because the check was\n`[ -x gxemul ]`.  The carrier already says the exit status lies here; this is three more\ninstances in one session.\n\n**THE CAUSE IS STRUCTURAL AND OUTLIVES THIS TREE: AN INCREMENTAL PROPAGATION CHECK CANNOT\nSEE A STALE BASELINE.**  precommit_check.sh enforces propagation for `$CHANGED` -- the files\nTHIS commit touches -- which is deliberate and right (*a gate that hard-fails on a tree\nsomeone chose not to build is a gate that gets switched off*).  But the tree was ALREADY\nstale when the 2026-08-27 ruling landed, and nothing back-fills.  diskimage.h and\nmakeautodev.sh are touched by no recent round, so no gate ever required them.\n\n**The tree passed every commit's check while compiling nothing at all** -- worse than the\ngate comment's own fear of *a detector that has silently stopped detecting, worse than no\ndetector because it reassures*.\n\n*** OWNER DECISION 2026-08-28: a SOFT-WARNING full `diff -rq` of the .c/.h set, SKIPPED\nENTIRELY when the tree is absent. ***  Warning rather than hard-fail, and absent-is-skipped,\nso the absence-is-not-a-failure rule that keeps the gate switched on is preserved.  Filed as\n`asanbaselinedrift` to ship as its own harness round with its two negative controls.\n\nNOW UNBLOCKED: #489's headline claim.  Its bare-deletion mutant scored 12/19 with the host\nSURVIVING a 408-byte write past a malloc(0) -- which shows the write does not FAULT, not\nthat it is ABSENT.  An ASAN run settles that, and the tree to run it on now exists.\n\n*A check of mine scored the finished binary as having no ASan: `nm | grep -c __asan` = 0.\nWrong test -- the sanitizer symbols are in the SHARED LIBRARY.  `ldd | grep libasan` is the\ntest, and the build I nearly discarded was already correct.*\n\n*** DIAGNOSED AND FIXED 2026-08-28, AND THE CAUSE IS A GAP IN THE HARNESS RATHER THAN A\nSTALE FILE. ***\n\nMEASURED FIRST: `make -j8` in build-asan/ reported **MAKE_RC=0 AND PRODUCED NO BINARY** --\nthe documented trap, caught only because the check was `[ -x gxemul ]` rather than the exit\nstatus.  The real errors were in diskimage.c: `struct diskimage has no member named\nnr_of_tracks`, `DISKIMAGE_MAX_SPT undeclared`, `invalid use of undefined type struct\ndiskimage_track` -- a current .c against a STALE .h.\n\n*** THE CAUSE: THE PROPAGATION RULE IS INCREMENTAL AND THE TREE NEEDED A BASELINE. ***\nThe owner ruled on 2026-08-27 that build-asan/ joins the propagation set, and\nprecommit_check.sh implements that for `$CHANGED` -- the files THIS commit touches.  That\nis deliberate and well-reasoned (its own comment: *a gate that hard-fails on a tree someone\nchose not to build is a gate that gets switched off*).  **But the tree was already stale\nwhen the ruling landed, and nothing back-fills.**  `diskimage.h` is touched by no recent\nround, so no gate ever required it -- and it is what broke the build.\n\n**So the tree could pass every commit's check and still not compile.**  That is worse than\nthe gate comment's own fear -- it says a stale ASAN tree is *a detector that has silently\nstopped detecting, worse than no detector because it reassures*.  This one did not build at\nall, so it detected nothing, while every commit touching it reported green.\n\nMEASURED DIVERGENCE at diagnosis: **111 differing .c/.h files, 1 present only in SEC**\n(360 paths total, the rest .o and generated Makefiles).  No divergence is permitted for\nshared files -- unlike est/, build-asan has no allowlist -- so all 111 were staleness.\n\nFIXED by back-filling every .c/.h from GXEMUL-SEC/src (474 files copied, source divergence\nmeasured 0 afterwards), under a `.MUTANT` sentinel so an interrupted sync could not be\nmistaken for a clean tree.  Rebuilt with `rm src/cpus/*.o` first.\n\n*** WHAT REMAINS OPEN IS THE HARNESS GAP, NOT THIS TREE: an incremental propagation check\ncannot detect a stale BASELINE, and there is no periodic full comparison.  The next\nbaseline drift will be just as invisible. ***  A full `diff -rq` of the .c/.h set is cheap\nand would have caught this in one command; whether it belongs in the gate (and at what\ncadence, given the absence-is-not-a-failure rule) is an owner decision, not a round's.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS: `#489`'s strongest single result is the bare-deletion\nmutant scoring 12/19 with the host SURVIVING a 408-byte write past a `malloc(0)`.  The\nmeasuring seat stated its own limit -- that shows the write does not FAULT, not that it is\nABSENT.  An ASAN run is exactly what closes that gap.\n\n*** `build-asan/` DOES NOT BUILD AND HAS NO BINARY. Found 2026-08-27 while propagating\n#484, and it PREDATES that round -- `dev_wdc` appears ZERO times in the failure log. ***\n\n    make        103 errors, and it reported EXIT 0 -- the documented false-zero, again\n    build-asan/gxemul   DOES NOT EXIST\n    stale objects       222 .o files with no binary to link them into\n\nROOT CAUSE, verified rather than guessed: `build-asan/src/disk/diskimage.c` is IN SYNC\nwith SEC while `build-asan/src/include/diskimage.h` is NOT.  The new .c uses members the\nold .h does not declare -- `nr_of_tracks`, `total_disc_sectors`, `first_data_track_lba`,\n`DISKIMAGE_MAX_SPT`, `struct diskimage_track` -- each present in SEC's header and absent\nfrom this one.  **A .c was propagated without its header**, which is the no-VPATH rule\nobeyed halfway.\n\nAND IT IS NOT ONE FILE: **363 files under `build-asan/src` differ from SEC.**  This tree\nis broadly abandoned, not narrowly stale.\n\n*** THE CONSEQUENCE IS SILENT COVERAGE LOSS, THE SAME CLASS AS A GATE THAT SKIPS. ***\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` exists to run the machine-construction sweep under ASAN.  With no\nbinary it cannot do that -- and this project has already been bitten once by a gate\nskipping on a missing binary and scoring the skip as a pass.\n\nNOT FIXED IN #484, and deliberately: the stopping rule fixes a finding in-round only if it\nis a MEASURED FALSE PASS of this round or a WRONG RECORD.  This is neither -- it is a\npre-existing tree defect in a tree #484 does not measure against.  Folding a 363-file\nresync into a ring-buffer round is exactly how rounds stop converging.\n\nREMEDY when it is worked: re-derive the tree from SEC rather than patching the header.\nA tree 363 files out of date has no property worth preserving, and a header-only fix\nwould leave the other 362 to be discovered one build at a time.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27838:   "state_note": "*** A SEAT OUTCOME THE TAXONOMY DOES NOT NAME, AND THE CHECKER FILES IT AS A FORMATTING\nPROBLEM: INVESTIGATION WITHOUT CONCLUSION. ***\n\nMeasured 2026-08-27 on `panel_20260827_182018/codex.txt`: 462 KB, 7240 lines, 98% novel, no\nverdict anywhere, and the answer TERMINATES INSIDE A FILE IT WAS QUOTING -- its last line is\na closing brace from a scratch generator it had opened.  1111 lines of real grep/file output\npreceded it.  The seat worked and never landed.\n\nIt is none of the recorded classes: no quota text (not a WALL), 98% novel (not echo-only),\nsubstantial output (not the minimax stall), a CLI seat (not the deepseek thinking-burn), and\nthe transport was up (not a refused connection).\n\n`seat_verdicts.py` reported it as *answered, NO VERDICT LINE (format miss)* -- the daemon\ncase's conflation repeated: **a seat that produced no answer, recorded as a seat with bad\nformatting.**  Scoring it `answered` credits a verdict that does not exist, and that panel\nreads 6 answered when the honest count is 5 plus one unfinished.\n\nTHE DISCRIMINATOR IS CHEAP AND IS NOT THE ONE ALREADY CHECKED: **does the answer END INSIDE A\nQUOTED FILE OR CODE BLOCK rather than with a sentence?**  A finished answer ends in prose;\nthis one ends in a closing brace.  *Is there a verdict line* is a FORMAT question and *did the\nseat finish* is a COMPLETION question -- one check is currently answering both.\n\nNOT A CLAIM ABOUT THE SEAT.  The same seat produced a real verdict with a real finding on\n`#484`'s pass 2 and a substantive answer on `#485` (the landisk unmapped-write finding).  One\nfiring ran out mid-investigation.  The value here is the SIGNATURE, not the seat.\n\nREMEDY: teach `seat_verdicts.py` the ends-inside-a-quote discriminator and report a distinct\nstate -- UNFINISHED -- rather than folding it into the format miss.\n\nOWED BEFORE IT SHIPS, because it changes the tool every panel runs: TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS.\nProve it fires on this file, and prove it does NOT fire on `#484`'s codex answer, which ends\nin prose and carries a verdict.  This project has already shipped one check that could not\nfail; a completion check that fires on every long answer would be worse than none.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28485:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-08-28 BY OWNER DECISION, from the round that fixed `asanunbuildable`.\n\n**THE GAP:** `precommit_check.sh` enforces build-asan propagation for `$CHANGED` -- the files\nTHIS commit touches.  That bound is deliberate and correct; its own comment says *a gate that\nhard-fails on a tree someone chose not to build is a gate that gets switched off*.  **But a\nfile no round happens to touch is never checked**, so a tree that was already stale when the\n2026-08-27 ruling landed stays stale forever.  Measured: 111 divergent .c/.h files, of which\n`diskimage.h` and `makeautodev.sh` broke the build -- neither touched by a recent round.\n\n**THE SHAPE, worth stating because it generalises past this tree: an INCREMENTAL sync check\ncannot detect a stale BASELINE.**  The tree passed every commit while compiling nothing.\n\n*** THE DECISION: a SOFT-WARNING full `diff -rq` over the .c/.h set, SKIPPED ENTIRELY when the\ntree is absent. ***  Warn, do not hard-fail -- and skip on absence -- so the\nabsence-is-not-a-failure property that keeps this gate switched on is preserved.  Chosen over\na hard fail (which is the exact shape the gate's author warned gets disabled) and over doing\nnothing (which leaves the next drift to be found the way this one was: by someone trying to\nuse the tree).\n\n**TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS ARE OWED, as for any gate change:** (1) with a source file deliberately\nreverted in build-asan/, the check WARNS and names it; (2) with the tree absent entirely, the\ncheck is SILENT and the gate stays green.  Ships as its own small harness round -- a gate edit\nmust not ride in on a diff reviewed for something else.\n\nSCOPE NOTE: `Makefile`s legitimately differ (they carry the ASan COPTIM) and must be excluded;\nthe measured expectation after a correct sync is **14 differing files, all Makefiles, zero\nothers**.  `est/` is NOT in scope -- it has a declared divergence allowlist\n(`tools/est_divergence.txt`); build-asan has none, which is why any .c/.h divergence there is\nstaleness by definition.\n\n**SEVERITY SET MED, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: same family: the propagation check is INCREMENTAL, so a stale BASELINE is invisible and the tool reports green over it.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28504:     "note": "FOUND BY RUNNING THE DEAD-MAN SWITCH WHEN THE OWNER ASKED ABOUT CRON STATUS -- not by any\ngate, and not by anyone reading the battery.  `nightly_check.sh` has been reporting\nNIGHTLY_CHECK_FAIL for five days.\n\nTHE 2026-08-23 WEEKLY BATTERY WENT RED: RUN_SH_EXIT=1, VERDICT_TOKEN=REGRESS_FAIL,\npassed 14 / failed 1 / skipped 1, on a DIRTY tree (`*** VERDICT DOES NOT DESCRIBE A\nCOMMIT ***`).  The Windows task's own LastTaskResult is 1 and has been since.\n\n*** THE FAILING CHECK IS ONE LINE OF TEXT, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IN gate_build PASSED: ***\nboth trees built, 223 and 224 objects, binaries produced, zero errors, zero warnings.\n\n    FAIL no stale allowlist entries    got=1 want=0\n         STALE (listed as divergent, but the files now agree): promemul/arcbios.c\n\nAND IT CASCADED, exactly as the carrier's serialisation note predicts: gate_build withdraws\nthe shared arc binary at gate start and republishes ONLY on success, so its failure left the\nbinary unpublished and **gate_mips SKIPPED** -- a whole gate's coverage lost, scored as SKIP\nrather than as a failure.  One stale line cost two gates.\n\nALREADY FIXED IN THE TREE, by accident rather than by anyone chasing it: `867c697` (#481)\nremoved the arcbios entry.  So a re-run should be green -- but nothing re-ran it, and the\ndead-man switch cannot tell 'fixed' from 'never looked at'.\n\n*** THE DEFECT THAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS THE ONE WORTH A ROUND: gate_build.sh PINS ITS OWN\nALLOWLIST. ***  `tools/est_divergence.txt` was created precisely to stop that -- its own\nheader says 'This file exists because the allowlist used to be PINNED inside the checker and\nwent stale TWICE'.  `precommit_check.sh` reads the file.  **`gate_build.sh` does not --\n`grep -n est_divergence regress/gate_build.sh` returns NOTHING** -- and its pinned copy is\nwhat went stale.\n\nThe two disagree TODAY, measured:\n    gate_build.sh DIVERGENT  (5)  devices/{Makefile.skel,autodev.c,dev_jazz.c,dev_ne2000.c}\n                                  machines/machine_arc.c\n    est_divergence.txt       (6)  the same five PLUS devices/.index\n\nThe `.index` gap looks benign today -- the 8/23 run reported 'no divergence outside the\ndocumented set: 0', so `list_diffs` does not surface it -- but benign-by-luck is the state\nthe file was written to end, and the stale entry proves the pinned copy drifts.\n\nFIX (not taken here, because changing a gate owes negative controls): make gate_build.sh\nREAD est_divergence.txt, as precommit_check.sh already does.  Controls owed in both\ndirections -- prove it fails on an injected stale entry, and prove it passes unchanged on\nthe real list.\n\n*And a scheduling hazard worth its own sentence: the battery fires on a fixed schedule and\ncaught the tree MID-EDIT (five modified files, including the device being worked that\nafternoon).  A verdict that does not describe a commit cannot be attributed to anything,\nwhich is the one thing a weekly battery exists to do.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28559:   "note": "`artifacts/makercfalse/hook_controls_20260829/hookctl.py` is an 18-row control suite (9 must FIRE, 9 must stay SILENT) for the PreToolUse wsl-dollar guard.  It is classified CLASS_RESEARCH -- evidence -- because no emulator gate can run it: it tests a Claude-Code hook, not the emulator.  **So the guard that fires on every Bash call has no regression test that anything executes.**  *** THE CASE FOR GATING IT IS MEASURED, NOT HYPOTHETICAL: the hook shipped 2026-08-29 with FOUR defects and nobody noticed for hours. *** Two false negatives (a multi-line `bash -c` block whose dollar sits below the -c -- the shape that actually bites, and which cost two confident wrong readings the same day) and two false positives that line-scoping was adopted to fix AND NEVER FIXED: a control of the very shape the hook's own comment cited as its motivating case still fired.  It scored 14/18; the rescoped form scores 18/18.  AGAINST gating: an emulator gate running a harness-tool control suite is a category mixture, and the suite depends on a file OUTSIDE the git root's normal scope.  A `tools/`-level check is likelier right than a `regress/` gate.  Cost is small (the suite runs in about a second) and it owes the usual two negative controls -- prove it reds on a broken hook, prove it greens on the shipped one.  *** CLOSED 2026-08-30.  The 18 controls are run by something now: a conditional section in `precommit_check.sh`. ***  CONDITIONAL on purpose -- the suite takes ~28 s, which is too slow for every commit and is exactly how a gate earns being switched off.  It fires when the hook, or the suite itself, is in the changed set, which is when it can regress.  Not a regress gate: it tests a Claude-Code hook rather than the emulator, so no battery gate can host it.  **NO SENTINEL IS NOT A PASS.**  The section fails when `HOOKCTL_DONE` is absent, because the suite REFUSES outright if the hook is missing and that refusal must read as a failure rather than a skip.  *** AND RUNNING IT SURFACED A DEFECT IN THE SUITE ITSELF: `HOOK` WAS ONE MACHINE'S HARDCODED ABSOLUTE PATH, IN A TRACKED FILE. ***  That is the defect the whole harness migration fixed on 2026-08-17 -- *scripts DERIVE the project root from their own location; they used to hardcode one machine's absolute path, which is only half-tracked* -- and this suite was written twelve days later carrying it.  Now derived from `__file__` with a `GXHOOK` override, and it REFUSES if the target is absent instead of reporting a clean sweep against nothing.  Controlled both ways.  It still points at the `.claude/` path rather than the tracked file, deliberately: that is what Claude Code actually invokes, so testing it exercises the shim AND the hook behind it.  Pointing at the tracked file would stop testing the delegation, which is the part that can silently break.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28940:   "note": "*** MEASURED 2026-08-29: a complete pass-1 brief was written and NINE SEATS FIRED to redo work that had shipped two days earlier as #479/#480/#481/#482. ***  Three dev_wdc.c rows carried a full 9/9 RESEARCH stage; the work shipped under four commit numbers; ZERO entries landed back on the rows; the ledger froze at 'in work'; and the main loop, correctly treating the ledger as the queue, drafted a fresh brief.  The brief's own 'mandatory' mutant was already in the preserved kill table, KILLED.  **The CHANGELOG knew -- its Round 479 block names all three row ids -- and the ledger did not.**  *** THE EXISTING GATES ARE BLIND TO THIS BY CONSTRUCTION. ***  precommit section H reads LEDGER ENTRIES and SKIPS STAGES THAT NEVER STARTED; the implement and review stages never started on those rows.  So a round that ships without writing back cannot be seen by the check built to notice missing panel coverage.  This is the unread-review class one step worse: an unrecorded ROUND.  TAKEN ALREADY (2026-08-29): a §C0 block in BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md -- two commands, `git log -1 -- <file>` and `grep <rowid> CHANGELOG.md`, pasted into the brief's settled premises.  It lives in the TEMPLATE because the carrier's own finding is that the one piece of prose which demonstrably fires is the one in the artifact present at the moment of decision.  FILED HERE, NOT BUILT: a checker warning when a row id named in a CHANGELOG round block is still 'in work' with no post-ship entry.  Existence-only, forward-only from a cutoff, owing two negative controls (a staged unrecorded round must warn; a properly-closed row must not).  **It changes what every commit must pass, so it is an owner decision rather than a round's** -- and this project has measured that a checker policing a minority of a class while alarming on prose gets switched off, so the population it would fire on should be counted before it is written.",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29652:   "note": "*** THIS EARNED ITS SECOND INSTANCE IN ONE DAY, IN THE SAME ARTIFACTS DIRECTORY, WHICH IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN TRIGGER FOR PREFERRING STRUCTURE OVER MEMORY. ***  **Instance 1, `runswap.sh`:** the base leg was `git show HEAD~1:`.  True when written.  An unrelated commit (a citation repair) then landed on top, pushing `#495` down to `HEAD~1` itself, so BOTH legs would have been built from code CONTAINING the fix.  Pinned to `67120fd^`.  **Instance 2, `killswap.sh`:** LEG 0's pre-extension probe was `git show HEAD:`.  True while the round was in flight -- HEAD held the 11-row probe and the working tree held 15.  **The round then SHIPPED**, HEAD became the 15-row probe, and the script refused a run in which there was no extension left to measure.  Pinned to `e61ccd8^`.  *** THE SHAPE IS SPECIFIC AND WORTH NAMING: a round's artifacts are written while the round is UNCOMMITTED, so every relative ref in them is written from a vantage point the round itself destroys by shipping. ***  The artifact is durable; the reference frame is not.  **BOTH WERE CAUGHT BY THE SCRIPTS' OWN GUARDS, NOT BY A READER**, and that is the reassuring half: each refused to measure rather than measuring the wrong thing.  A reproduction months from now would fail loudly instead of producing a confident wrong table.  PROPOSED STRUCTURAL FIX, filed rather than built: a precommit check warning when a file under `regress/artifacts/` contains `HEAD~`, `HEAD^` or `git show HEAD:`.  Bounded, existence-only, forward-only from a cutoff.  **NOT built in the round that found it** -- a tooling change owing two negative controls (it must fire on a relative ref and must NOT fire on a pinned SHA), which ships as its own harness round.  *The population is probably larger: only two files were exercised today.  Other rounds' artifacts have never been re-run, so a stale ref there would not yet have announced itself.  The count is unknown and is deliberately not guessed.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29801:     "note": "HARNESS-ROUND PLAN, farmed codex, read-only (brief PLAN_brief_20260910.md).  'VERDICT: NO-GO (6 items) -- the two wirings can be sketched concretely, but they are not yet verified, and two wirings plus four re-dates do not close the separate fbpending_bound deadline.'  IT RE-DERIVED EVERY NUMBER AT THIS HEAD rather than trusting DERIVED_20260909.txt (which was derived at b4d4177): the six exemptions stand, the two-versus-four exit-status split stands, and the off-by-one holds -- `d < today' is FALSE on 2026-09-20 and TRUE on 2026-09-21, so the gate first reds on the 21st, ELEVEN DAYS from this entry.  It also QUALIFIED the 'always exit 0' claim correctly: measurement failures do not control those four exits, but an uncaught apparatus exception still can -- so the honest statement is not 'cannot fail' but 'cannot fail FOR THE REASON THE GATE CARES ABOUT'.  DELIVERED, ready to implement: hpcmips_ctor_probe.py goes into gate_asan_sweep.sh immediately after gate_begin and BEFORE the ASan-build skip (with `ulimit -c 0' hoisted there, because that probe deliberately exercises aborting controls), pinning the complete result line so numerator AND denominator are both fixed; fbpending_drain_probe.py goes at the TAIL of gate_ab.sh, after every LUNA run and comparison, with an explicit cap of 762 derived from the machine's own emulated_hz and the integer conversion rather than transcribed.  Each wiring comes with BOTH negative controls named as concrete edits -- a mutant that must red the new row, and a HEAD run that must stay green -- which is what this project requires before any gate row ships.  Two cost corrections: the exemption's '~8 seconds' is historical and not remeasured at this HEAD, and the real configured limit is 40 seconds per subprocess rather than an 8-second total.  NOT IMPLEMENTED: this is a tooling change owing its own negative controls, and it ships as its own harness round, not as a rider on a fix round."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30888:   "note": "*** MEASURED 2026-08-30: A 38 KB BRIEF LOST TWO SEATS, AND `panel.sh` PRINTED A WARNING AND FIRED THEM ANYWAY. ***  `WARNING: brief is 38983 bytes (>30KB). Codex/Kimi take it on argv and may fail with 'Argument list too long'. Trim, or point those seats at the file path instead of piping its contents.`  Then it launched all seven, and:      codex.txt  ->  `.../codex: Argument list too long`   (123 bytes)      kimi.txt   ->  `.../kimi: Argument list too long`    (97 bytes)  **grok survived because it uses `--prompt-file`**, which the carrier already names as the one CLI seat immune to this; the three Ollama seats POST and have no argv at all.  So the loss is exactly the two seats that take the brief on the command line.  *** THE DEFECT IS THAT THE WARNING IS ADVISORY. ***  It correctly diagnoses the problem, correctly names the remedy, and then does the thing it just warned about.  That is one step milder than the check-that-cannot-fail this project names first -- it INFORMS and does not ACT -- and the operator it informs is the same party who just wrote the brief and is about to walk away while nine seats run.  **The failure surfaces minutes later as two ~100-byte seat files, which the seat check reports as a seat failure rather than as a launcher failure.**  **THE REMEDY IS ALREADY IN THE WARNING'S OWN TEXT and costs nothing at run time: when the brief exceeds the cap, pass codex and kimi a SHORT PROMPT NAMING THE FILE PATH instead of the contents.**  Both accept it -- recovered by hand in this incident, in one command each, and both then answered.  The file-reading seats can read the brief; only the packet-fed ones need it inlined, and those are the ones with no argv limit.  *** NOT FIXED IN THE MOMENT, DELIBERATELY: a panel was running, and editing a running shell script corrupts it mid-execution -- bash reads incrementally by byte offset. ***  That is a documented defect class here and the reason this is a filed row rather than an in-passing edit.  **WHAT THE FIX OWES, since it changes the script every panel runs:** two negative controls, in the shape this project already requires -- prove an oversized brief now reaches both seats and they ANSWER, and prove a normal-sized brief still goes on argv unchanged (so the switch does not silently become the default and change what every seat receives).  *Note the cap is not exactly 32 KB and the warning threshold is 30 KB; the measured failure was at 38,983 bytes and no bisection was done, so the true boundary is not established and this row does not claim one.*  *** FIXED 2026-08-30.  `panel.sh` WARNED ABOUT THE ARGV CAP AND THEN LAUNCHED ANYWAY -- the remedy its own message recommended was one the script could simply TAKE. ***  A 38 KB brief killed BOTH argv-fed seats with `Argument list too long`; codex and kimi had to be recovered BY HAND, while this block printed advice sixty lines above the launch that ignored it.  **IT IS THE CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL FAMILY'S SIBLING: a message reporting an ACTION the code does not take.**  The re-firing message (`re-firing once (a wall can be minutes long) ...` followed by a `return 2` every caller discards) is the same defect, and its recorded lesson applies unchanged -- telling the operator it was handled is WORSE than silence, because the one person who could fix it by hand is told not to.  **THE FIX IS THAT BOTH SEATS ALREADY READ FILES.**  Grok has been immune all along (`--prompt-file`), agy too (`--add-dir` plus a pointer prompt); codex and kimi were the only two still fed the brief on argv.  Above the cap they now get `PTR_PROMPT` -- read `$BRIEF_ABS` and review it as its own Questions section directs -- and below it the inline form is byte-unchanged.  **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, and the control READS THE REAL FILE rather than a re-typed copy** -- `sed` extracts the decision block and the prompt-build out of `panel.sh` and `eval`s them, because a verification that shares the defect of the thing it verifies always passes, which this project has measured.  POST-FIX **6/6, rc=0**, including both boundary rows (exactly 30000 -> inline, 30001 -> pointer) and a both-seats-agree column.  PRE-FIX the same script reports **EXTRACT FAILED, rc=9** -- *the old form has no cap branch at all* -- which is the failability proof: the test can fail, and it fails on the code this fix replaces.  `PANEL_ARGV_CAP` exists ONLY so the branch is testable without keeping a 38 KB fixture, and the comment says so; it is not a tuning knob.  *** AND THE GATE CAUGHT A RECORD DEFECT I MADE WHILE CLOSING THIS ROW: I wrote the fix up as a ledger ENTRY with `seat: mainloop`. ***  There is no such seat -- the declared vocabulary is the nine, and `mainloop` appeared exactly ONCE in 2,278 entries, mine.  **A seat entry is what sections G and H COUNT for panel coverage, so inventing one inflates the roster with a seat that never answered** -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape reached from the other side, by manufacture rather than by omission.  Main-loop work belongs in the row's note, which is where this sentence is.  *Caught by `precommit_check.sh` naming the declared list back at me, not by review.*",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31468:   "note": "*** THE HARNESS PUNISHES PROGRESS, AND TWO REPAIRS HAVE NOW MOVED THE PUNISHMENT WITHOUT REMOVING IT. ***  The selftest first broke when `pit8253_latch_probe.py` was wired and its exemption deleted; `_pick_exempt_detector()` fixed that by DERIVING the exemplar.  It broke again when `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` was discharged; deriving M5c-M5e and M12 fixed that.  **Both repairs kept the same shape -- pick a live exemption and take something away from it -- so the tripwire moved from the FIRST discharge to the LAST one.**  **THE ROOT CAUSE, named by the seat in one sentence: the selftest MUTATES LIVE INPUT DATA IT DOES NOT NEED.**  A mutant that must find a real exemption to delete is coupled to a table designed to empty; a mutant that INSERTS its own fixture is not.  M9's machinery already does exactly this for the gate list, so the pattern is in-tree rather than invented.  *** THE END STATE IS REACHABLE AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS WORTH A ROUND: *** six exemptions remain, all dated 2026-09-20.  When the last is discharged -- which is the point of the table -- `precommit_check.sh` prints THE PROBE-WIRING CHECKER CAN NO LONGER BE SHOWN TO FAIL and blocks the commit that did the right thing.  *** SCOPE CORRECTED 2026-08-30 BY THE ADJUDICATING SEAT, AND THE CORRECTION IS MEASURED: AS FIRST FILED THIS ROW SHIPPED AN INCOMPLETE FIX. ***  **NINE mutants depend on the live table, not seven: M1, M2, M2b, M3, M5c, M5d, M5e, M5f, M12.**  I had excluded `M5f` on the ground that it *\"uses a COMPUTED date rather than a live table entry, so it is addition-shaped already\"*.  **That is false and the code says so in one line:** `_st_apply(chk, _exline(EX), '    \"%s\":   (\"%s\", \"x\"),' % (EX, _yest, ...))` computes the DATE and takes the ENTRY from `_exline(EX)` -- a LIVE PICK, exactly like the seven I did scope.  On an empty table `EX is None` and M5f falls into the byte-identical `results.append(False)` arm that **M12 has, which I DID scope.**  *I excluded one twin and scoped the other.*  **AND THE EXEMPLAR WAS WRONG IN THE SAME BREATH.**  The row held M5f up as *\"the pattern the other seven should follow\"*.  The genuinely addition-shaped exemplar is **`M3x`** -- the seat's insert-a-fixture-for-`arm_flags_probe.py` STALE EXEMPTION mutant, which scored `ok` on a FULLY EMPTY table.  A future implementer reading the old text would have converted seven, skipped M5f for the reason I wrote, copied the wrong exemplar, and **the selftest would still fail on an empty table** -- the exact failure this row exists to prevent, shipped inside the row that prevents it.  **SCOPE: convert the NINE table-dependent mutants from SUBTRACTION to ADDITION, using `M3x` as the pattern.**  Each owes two negative controls (it must still red on the rule it tests, and it must NOT red on a clean tree), which is why this is its own harness round and not a rider: it is fourteen controls, and a botched one silently weakens the check that guards every probe in the tree.  *Filed rather than fixed under the stopping rule: the shipped selftest is above the vacuity floor today (22/22, and the frozen-clock control proves it can fail), so this is COVERAGE DEBT with a known trigger date, not a false pass.*  *Found by the adjudicating seat when asked the load-bearing question -- WHICH of my accepted dispositions is wrong, with the count named.  It found four of six.  A round that accepts everything has either had a very good panel or has stopped arguing, and only a seat can tell you which; this one had stopped arguing.*  *** AND THE CORRECTION ABOVE WAS ITSELF WRONG, TWICE, IN THE TWO WAYS THIS PROJECT NAMES MOST OFTEN.  Both found by the adjudicating seat, one of them against its own earlier answer. ***  **(a) THE CORRECTION LEFT ITS REFUTED CONCLUSION STANDING IN THE SAME NOTE.**  A diff of this note between `43106c2` and `9ccb47b` shows the correction block INSERTED and the scope sentence rewritten -- and the region after it UNCHANGED, so the note read: scope is NINE including M5f ... then, in the closing position of authority, *'M5f is deliberately NOT in scope ... the pattern the other seven should follow'*.  Not a quotation; the original text, live.  **A future implementer reading to the END does exactly what the correction says reproduces the failure.**  That is the carrier's superseded-conclusion rule and mechanical rule 2 (grep for siblings) failing together, with the sibling 200 characters away in the same field.  Deleted, not demoted.  **(b) NINE WAS AN UNDERCOUNT.  THE SET IS TEN, AND THIS ROW'S OWN WITNESS FIELD HELD THE REFUTATION ALL ALONG.**  `M5b` mutates nothing but runs the checker with `--today 2026-09-21` wanting rc=1 and `EXPIRED EXEMPTION`; on an empty table there is nothing to expire, the checker prints `PROBEWIRING_PASS` rc=0, and M5b FAILS.  **The arithmetic proves it without running anything: the witness records `SELFTEST_FAIL 12/22`, which is TEN failures, and 22 - 9 = 13.**  A recorded count that contradicts the measurement recorded beside it -- the carried-number shape, in the row written to fix a counting defect.  **AND THE CORRECTION REPEATED THE ERROR'S SHAPE:** M5f was excluded on a FALSE ground (*addition-shaped already*); M5b was then excluded on a TRUE-BUT-IRRELEVANT one (it is not subtraction-shaped) -- when the failure class is *the table emptying breaks the selftest*, and M5b is squarely in it.  Its repair is the same insert-a-fixture pattern.  *** (c) AN ELEVENTH, OF A WORSE KIND: `M5a` GOES VACUOUS-GREEN. ***  It wants `PROBEWIRING_PASS` with `--today 2026-09-19`; on an empty table it gets PASS while testing nothing.  It does not fail, so no count reveals it -- **the check-that-cannot-fail shape, arriving inside the fix for a different defect.**  **SCOPE, RESTATED: TEN failing mutants (M1, M2, M2b, M3, M5b, M5c, M5d, M5e, M5f, M12) plus M5a's vacuity, all converted to fixtures, with `M3x` as the pattern.**  The control count is deliberately NOT restated as a literal here -- the previous figure, 'fourteen controls', was 7 rules x 2 and went stale the moment the rule count moved.  Derive it when the round is scoped, and name its unit."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31596:     "note": "(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5 -- DISCHARGED 2026-08-31)  The hold was for the rung-3 witness ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires before a round may record itself unwitnessed.  **It did not merely attempt: it BUILT one, 32/32**, with liveness, device-signature, neighbour-device and negative controls, and widths enumerated rather than argued.  It also found THREE escapes, corrected a row of its own that failed for an ADEL rather than a code reason, and diagnosed a defect in the shared probe harness that made one of its rows unable to fail.  *The provisional-adjudication rule was right to hold: the design converged 8/9 and the ninth seat still changed the round -- an accept row at a window BASE cannot see a far-bound move, which no reading seat had noticed.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31893:   "note": "*** FILED BECAUSE A COMMENT ALREADY CLAIMED IT WAS.  The #497 gate comment ended 'the right answer is a gate_devices.sh split ... Filed, not done' -- and it was NOT filed: no ledger row, no queue entry, under any phrasing.  Caught by the pass-2 seat while the comment was STAGED. ***  *That is the gap-closes-itself shape: a claim that a process step is complete, shipping in the same commit as the thing it excuses.  A future reader greps for the row, finds nothing, and cannot tell whether the split was considered and rejected or simply forgotten.*  **AND THE PRECEDENT CLAIM WAS ONE STEP SHORT, which the same seat measured.**  I wrote that `gate_arm.sh` already runs footbridge and PIT probes, 'neither an ARM instruction test'.  True -- **but both run `-E cats`, this gate's OWN ARM machine, and the PIT wiring comment says it was placed there BECAUSE 'this is the only gate whose machine it shares'.**  So `#497`'s probe is not the third instance of an existing accretion; it is **the FIRST probe in this gate on a foreign machine AND CPU family**.  My words were true and my implication was generous to my own decision.  **THE FORCING CRITERION, so this row cannot sit forever:** *the NEXT device probe wired into `gate_arm.sh` converts this from FILED to OWED.*  The accretion story guarantees each addition makes the next cheaper, which is exactly how a least-bad accommodation becomes permanent.  **THE OPERATIONAL COST, named rather than left to be discovered:** a red row in `gate_arm` can now mean an SGI/MIPS device regression.  That is an attribution trap for whoever reads the battery output at 3am.  *** OWED AS OF 2026-08-31, BY ITS OWN FORCING CRITERION, AND RAISED low -> med. ***  The #497 gate block filed this row with the criterion written into the gate itself: *\"the NEXT device probe wired into this gate converts it from filed to OWED.\"*  #498's `sgi_de_width_probe.py` is that probe, wired in the same commit that records this.  **A pre-specified trigger that fires and is ignored is worse than one never written, because the next reader trusts it.**  `gate_arm.sh` now runs THREE device detectors that have nothing to do with ARM -- the pit8253 block, #497's sgi_re and #498's sgi_de -- purely because it is the only gate with probe infrastructure and a machine they share.  *The split was not done in this round on purpose: it is a harness round owing its own negative controls, and holding a HIGH device fix hostage to a gate refactor would be the tail wagging the dog.  What was not acceptable was compounding it silently.*",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36086:     "note": "REGRESS (fable-only), fired the same minute the row closed rather than batched.  MEASURED in its own scratch clone with the LIVE probe (md5 f7385ca0): committed fix 46/46; the verbatim exit(1) arm reinstated 7/46 with a 39-row red list byte-identical to table 4's M_EXIT.out -> replaying the gate's count pattern, 41 gate checks fail: gate 14 goes red on a regression today.  Gate name list == probe rows byte for byte; the real name-length bound under `%-16s' + the two-space anchor is 15, not 14 (records said 14 -- corrected).  Lethal-only carve-out: the seven pure-survival rows are correctly lethal-only (fate(alive, how) cannot have a non-lethal kill; they discriminate by arm and class); ONE content row, `alias msg', has no non-lethal kill in any table -- the fold mutant is FILED (pcc2anngrid), LOW, outside the fix's diff.  `genctl rt'/`t1cmp word rt' are BASE-anchored negative controls (said so in the contract now).  Every contract line maps to a measured non-lethal kill except the fold line; disclosed == measured escapes.  Hygiene verified: clone cmp-clean + sentinel cleared after a rebuild; nothing written to any project tree.  Artifacts: measure.sh/.log, verify.sh, FABLE_BASE.out, FABLE_MEXIT.out beside FINDINGS.md."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36137:   "witness": "probe:regress/m88k_exc_probe.py (rung 3: real m88k guest instructions through real decode on the COMMITTED testm88k machine; HEAD 0/9 probe2_head.out, 0/10 probe10_head.out, 3/22 probe_v3_head.out -- the 3 are the two HALT-expected negative controls and the pre-existing user vector; fix 22/22 probe_v3_fix.out and probe_v3_fix_final.out; artifacts regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/)",
commit eb26590b0a8bcaa0039045ee52d4d152bd6edd3c
Author:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Sat Sep 12 12:14:45 2026 -0500
Commit:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Sat Sep 12 12:14:45 2026 -0500

    #526 (xscaleinten): a guest read of the i80321 interrupt-enable register ended the emulator after loading the right value
    
    arm_coproc_i80321_6(), coprocessor 6 on -E iq80321 and Iyonix, loaded
    cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten into r[rd] and then called fatal() and exit(1).  The
    register is modelled in both directions -- the store arm writes the same field
    and dev_i80321.c consumes it -- and the sibling crn-4 read (isteer) has no exit.
    Fix: ACCEPT.  Delete the two lines, keep the value; no latch, because the read
    is implemented.  The isteer non-zero write, the isrc write and the unknown-tuple
    exit in the same handler are retained and rowed as controls.
    
    WITNESS, rung 3, unmodified in-tree iq80321: committed 11/26, fix 26/26
    (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/agy_xscale_witness_20260911.py).  Test-first ORDER
    is not established by the artifacts (witness_fix.out has the earlier mtime);
    every kill table's HEAD leg reproduces the death from a clean rebuild.  The
    ledger witness field, which said "TEST-FIRST SATISFIED", is corrected.
    
    DETECTOR: regress/xscale_inten_probe.py, 30 rows, CONTRACT and NOT COVERED in its
    header; wired into gate_arm.sh with parse-before-score and every pin a literal 30.
    Kill table 3 (artifacts/xscaleinten/killtable3_20260912/): 12 legs, all on
    prediction, BASE 30/30, HEAD 12/30, ten mutants killed.
    
    PASS 2 found two exits the 29-row instrument could not see, both MEASURED 29/29
    and live by the Opus seat (artifacts/xscaleinten/opus_pass2_20260912/):
    `if (rd == 0)` and `if (inten & 0x00400000)`.  One added row, RD0_ALLONES,
    kills both, each alone.  The seats split on floor vs disclosure; Fable 5.1,
    fired last and verified by its own model name, ruled clause (e) for both, the
    row L1 construction, and closed the repair chain on the §F2 pass
    (artifacts/xscaleinten/fable_adjudication_20260912.md).  The CONTRACT's M5 line
    (it redirects BOTH directions) and a "4th read" comment were corrected.
    
    Gate 14 PASS, 922 checks (914 + this section's 8), every xscale row ok, run from
    a frozen copy of gate_arm.sh (artifacts/xscaleinten/gate_arm_20260912.log).
    Two-part clearance run before the gate: cpu_arm_coproc.c p1=CLEAN p2=FRESH in
    build/, build-asan/ and /tmp/gxsec-build; est/ p1=CLEAN, p2 N/A (source tree).
    
    Panels copied durably into artifacts/xscaleinten/ (xscalepass2, xpadjud).  agy
    deliberately not fired (measurement reservation).  The ledger in this commit
    carries the xscaleinten row only; the PPC round's entries land with it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

