closure {"by": "opus5-mainloop", "badge": "A", "note": "CLOSED by its closure-records commit (2026-10-18): #528 (ca3be4e, 2026-09-12) stopped an ordinary guest read of the Dreamcast Maple DMA-address register (MAPLE_DMAADDR, case 0x04) from ending the emulator after the model had computed the right value -- the read arm now breaks into the common tail, which returns the modelled d->dmaaddr.  Detector: regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py, 37 rows, wired into gate 10 (gate_sh_rounding.sh, pin 37; gate PASS 391 checks, regress/artifacts/mapledmaaddr/gate_sh_rounding_20260912.log).  Kill table 7: the fix 37/37, the pre-fix HEAD leg 11/37 (killtable7_20260912/SCORES.txt).  The repair chain CLOSED on table 7 by Fable 5.1's closing ruling (regress/artifacts/mapledmaaddr/fable_close_20260912.md, 21/21 on prediction); M_TICKPERIODIC4 and M_FILE are disclosed outside the contract.  The row stayed `in work` after that ruling only because nothing moved it; this commit does.  Split out earlier: mapledmaadvance (MED, filed)."}
armbfiwidth
state "open"
state_note "FILED 2026-09-07.  **`#510` fixed TWO of THREE sites of one idiom and left the third, whose undefined\ndomain is WIDER than either it repaired.**  Found by reading an UNREAD codex answer\n(`regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_bitsbytes_20260907.txt`) that had been sitting on disk,\ncited nowhere.  Every element below was VERIFIED by opening the file, not taken from the seat.\n\n*** THE DEFECT. ***  `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c`, `X(bfi)` (Bit-Field Insert):\n\n    int lsb = (uint8_t)ic->arg[2];\n    int msb = ic->arg[2] >> 16;\n    int width = msb - lsb + 1;\n    x <<= lsb;\n    uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;        <-- UNDEFINED over most of its domain\n\n**THE DECODE GIVES msb AND lsb INDEPENDENTLY, each 0..31** (verified at the `bfi` arm of the\ndecoder, `(iword & 0x0fe00070) == 0x07c00010`):\n\n    int lsb = (iword >>  7) & 31;\n    int msb = (iword >> 16) & 31;\n    ic->arg[2] = (msb << 16) + lsb;\n\nso **`width` ranges over [-30, 32]** and `1 << width` is undefined three separate ways:\n\n    width <= 0    negative shift count                       C99 6.5.7p3\n    width == 32   count >= width of the promoted type        C99 6.5.7p3\n    width == 31   1 is `int'; 2^31 is not representable       C99 6.5.7p4 (signed overflow)\n\n*** THE DENOMINATOR, and it is nearly half. ***  Of the **1024** encodable (msb, lsb) pairs:\n\n    496   nonpositive width  (lsb >= msb + 1)   -- of which 465 are strictly negative\n      1   width == 32        (msb=31, lsb=0)\n      2   width == 31        (msb=lsb+30)\n    ---\n    499   of 1024 pairs reach an undefined shift\n\n**That arithmetic was INDEPENDENTLY DERIVED TWICE and the first attempt was mine and wrong.**  A\ncodex answer on a different brief (`codex_shiftub_20260907.txt`) corrected it: *\"`msb - lsb + 1 < 0`\nrequires `lsb >= msb + 2`, giving 465 negative widths.  Another 31 pairs produce zero; 496 is the\nnumber of nonpositive widths.\"*  Recomputed here from the decode: pairs with `lsb >= msb+1` sum to\n31+30+...+1 = **496**, and `lsb >= msb+2` sums to 30+29+...+1 = **465**.  *The seat was right.*\n\n*** WHY THIS IS A SIBLING MISS AND NOT A NEW CLASS. ***  `#510` repaired the identical expression at\nthe UBFX and SBFX handlers, which now read:\n\n    uint32_t mask = (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);\n\n**`X(bfi)` sits about fifty lines below the second of those, in the same file, and was not touched.**\nThe carrier's mechanical rule 2 -- *when correcting a claim, grep for its siblings before calling it\nfixed* -- is exactly the rule that was missed, and it is recorded there with a prior instance where\nan \"un-fakeable\" claim was corrected in one place and survived in three.  **The BFX round's own\ndetector cannot see this**: `regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py` plants UBFX/SBFX words only.\n\n**AND THE BFX FIX DOES NOT TRANSFER UNCHANGED.**  BFX decodes `width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)`, so\nits domain is {1..32} and a `width == 32` special case is sufficient.  **BFI's width can be zero or\nnegative, which no `== 32` test catches.**  A repair copied from the sibling would close one third of\nthis site's undefined domain and look right.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and it is the next step rather than a caveat. ***  **No witness has been\nbuilt and no host misbehaviour has been measured.**  UB is not by itself a defect this project ships\na fix for without a measurement -- `#510` shipped on a UBSAN differential (1 diagnostic pre-fix, 0\npost-fix) plus a source pin, and this row owes the same.  The vehicle is cheap and already exists:\n`arm_bfx_width_probe.py` plants instruction words and executes them on an unmodified machine, so the\nwitness is a planted `bfi` with `msb < lsb` under a UBSAN build.\n\n**ARM architecturally leaves `msb < lsb` UNPREDICTABLE for BFI**, so a conforming guest does not emit\nit.  *That is an argument about which guests reach it, not about whether the emulator may invoke host\nUB when one does* -- which is the same argument `#510` made and shipped on.\n\n**PROPOSED FIX (not adjudicated -- no panel has seen this):**\n\n    uint32_t mask = (width <= 0) ? 0 :\n        (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);\n\nwith `mask = 0` for the nonpositive case leaving `rd` unchanged, which is a defensible reading of\nUNPREDICTABLE and the only one that inserts no bits.  **The `x <<= lsb` on the line above is SAFE**\n(`lsb <= 31` by construction) and must not be \"fixed\" alongside it.\n\n*** CORRECTED 2026-09-08 BY THE codex SEAT, AND THE ERROR WAS MINE.  Re-verified by brute force\nbefore this edit. ***\n\n**499 WAS WRONG.  THE TRUE FIGURE IS 468, AND I HAD CONFLATED TWO DIFFERENT QUANTITIES.**\n\n    width < 0     465   UNDEFINED -- negative shift count\n    width == 0     31   *** DEFINED.  `(1 << 0) - 1' is 0.  A shift count of ZERO is legal. ***\n    width 1..30   525   defined\n    width == 31     2   UNDEFINED -- signed left-shift result, 6.5.7p4\n    width == 32     1   UNDEFINED -- count >= width, 6.5.7p3\n    ----\n                  468   pairs reaching UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR\n                  496   pairs with ARCHITECTURALLY INVALID field ordering (msb < lsb)\n\n**Those are different sets and I summed the wrong one.**  Every nonpositive width is an invalid\nBFI encoding, but only the strictly NEGATIVE ones are undefined C.  *Three derivations agreed on 496\nearlier -- and 496 was the right answer to a question I was not asking.*\n\n**FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM THE SAME ANSWER:**\n\n* **P4 was partly wrong.**  `ddi0100i.txt` DOES contain zero `BFI`/`BFC` occurrences, but **its own\n  architecture list includes ARMv6** (verified: *\"ARMv4, ARMv4T, ARMv5T, (ARMv5TExP), ARMv5TE,\n  ARMv5TEJ, and ARMv6\"*), so calling it \"the ARMv5 manual\" is inaccurate.  BFI/BFC arrive with\n  ARMv6**T2**, which is why they are absent.  And *\"the broader claim that no other local reference\n  exists remains unestablished.\"*\n* **P5 CONFIRMED from the architecture, externally.**  ARM's BFI A1 description makes\n  `msbit < lsbit` UNPREDICTABLE, and its constrained behaviour permits **an undefined instruction, a\n  NOP, or an unknown destination value** -- so `mask = 0` is architecturally PERMITTED, not merely\n  defensible.  **That settles Q1.**\n* **P6 needs a host qualification, and it improves the FIX.**  `1U` is `unsigned int`, which C99\n  permits to be 16 bits, so `(1U << width)` is not universally portable either.  codex recommends\n  **`1UL` / `0xffffffffUL`**.  *** NOTE THIS MAKES `#510`'s SHIPPED REPAIR SUBOPTIMAL TOO -- it uses\n  `1U` -- though harmless on every host this project builds on. ***\n* **An early `return` IS sound here**, unlike the case the sketch rule was minted from: codex checked\n  the macros -- `X` declares a void function and `Y` generates separate conditional wrappers -- so\n  **there is no handler epilogue for a return to skip.**  *The sketch rule says to check the\n  enclosing control flow, not that early returns are always wrong; here the check comes back clean.*\n\n*** REACHABILITY IS ESTABLISHED: the BFI decoder branch has NO CPU-model or architecture-version\ngate ***, and `machine_test.c` registers `testarm` defaulting to SA1110 -- so the site is reachable\nin the simulator even though that CPU model predates the instruction.\n\n**THE WITNESS DESIGN, from Q2, and it kills my one-word plan:**\n* negative and excessive counts produce **DIFFERENT UBSan messages** (`shift exponent -1 is\n  negative` vs `shift exponent 32 is too large for 32-bit type 'int'`), and width 31 is a *signed\n  result* failure that plain shift-exponent checking MISSES;\n* **one instruction encoding cannot exercise both count failures** -- the immediate fields fix\n  `width`, and no register contents change it.  So the probe needs SEPARATE planted words per class.\n* **Two apparatus traps, and the first is the error I made twice this session:** *\"`session()`\n  consumes child output through its PTY, while callers print parsed results.  Grepping the Python\n  process's stderr can miss the sanitizer messages completely.\"*  And: UBSan suppresses repeat\n  reports at an already-reported location, **so diagnostic totals are not execution counts** --\n  require the intended site and diagnostic CLASS, not a total.\n\n*Still outstanding before this can land, in codex's own words: \"a fresh BFI sanitizer differential\nand integration results from binaries tied to this exact tree.  I have not measured those or\nestablished any host-visible wrong BFI result.\"*\n\n*** THIS ROW CARRIED TWO DIFFERENT DENOMINATORS -- 468 IN ITS TITLE AND 499 IN ITS NOTE -- AND THE\nNOTE'S WAS THE WRONG ONE.  Corrected 2026-09-08, and the correction is the THIRD generation of the\nsame arithmetic. ***\n\nThe note derived **499 = 496 nonpositive + 2 (width 31) + 1 (width 32)**.  **It counted the 31\nzero-width pairs as undefined, and they are not**: `width == 0` gives `1 << 0`, which is a perfectly\ndefined shift yielding `mask = 0`.  Only *strictly negative* widths are undefined shifts.\n\nRe-derived by enumerating all 1024 pairs rather than by arguing:\n\n    width < 0    465   undefined (negative shift count)\n    width == 0    31   *** DEFINED -- 1 << 0 is 1 ***\n    width 1..30  525   defined\n    width == 31    2   undefined (1 is `int'; 2^31 not representable)\n    width == 32    1   undefined (count >= width of the promoted type)\n    ------------------\n    UNDEFINED    468\n    msb < lsb    496   architecturally invalid ordering -- OVERLAPS the undefined set by 465\n\n**So the TITLE's 468 was right all along and the NOTE's 499 was wrong** -- which is the reverse of\nthe usual direction here, where the note corrects the title.\n\n**AND THE TITLE'S WORDING WAS ALSO WRONG, in a way the number hid:** it said *\"a DIFFERENT 496 have\narchitecturally invalid field ordering\"*.  **They are not different -- 465 of the 496 ARE among the\n468.**  The two sets overlap almost entirely; only 31 invalid-ordering pairs (width exactly 0) escape\nundefined behaviour, and only 3 undefined pairs (widths 31 and 32) have valid ordering.\n\n> *** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN \"A CORRECTION IS NOT SAFER THAN THE THING IT CORRECTS\", THIRD\n> INSTANCE. ***  My first arithmetic was wrong; a codex answer corrected it to 496/465 and I\n> recomputed and agreed -- **but neither of us asked whether `1 << 0` is undefined**, so the\n> corrected figure inherited a defect the original question never raised.  *A correction carries more\n> authority than the error, so it earns a second reading, not less of one.*\n\n*Found because a DIFFERENT codex brief -- on `armbfcdecode` -- volunteered that \"undefined for most\nencodable widths is incorrect for a 32-bit int: positive widths 1-30 are fine.\"  It was answering\nquestion 5 of five, about round scope.*\n\n*** FIX DESIGN COMPLETE, codex 2026-09-08 -- AND IT CORRECTED MY ARITHMETIC A SECOND TIME. ***\n\n**THE DOMAINS DIFFER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT.**  Derived from each decoder arm rather than\nassumed:\n\n    UBFX   width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)          domain 1..32\n    SBFX   width = 1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)          domain 1..32\n    BFI    msb, lsb decoded INDEPENDENTLY in 0..31   domain -30..32\n\nSo `#510`'s repair is CORRECT at its two sites and INSUFFICIENT here.\n\n*** MY \"467 OF 468 WOULD REMAIN UNDEFINED\" WAS WRONG -- IT IS 465. ***  `#510`'s form uses **`1U`**,\nso `1U << 31` is DEFINED (unsigned, no overflow), and the `width == 32` arm guards the other end.\n**Verbatim transfer therefore repairs THREE pairs -- two at width 31 and one at width 32 -- leaving\nthe 465 negative-width pairs undefined.**  I had assumed the `1U` did not help width 31; it does,\nand `#510` got that right for handlers that can reach 31.\n\n## THE PATCH\n\n    /*  Emulator choice: msb < lsb leaves the destination unchanged.  */\n    uint32_t mask = (width <= 0) ? 0U :\n        (width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1);\n\n    width   pairs   current C          proposed\n    -----   -----   ----------------   -----------------------------------------\n    < 0       465   UNDEFINED          zero mask; destination unchanged\n    0          31   defined            same zero mask; destination unchanged\n    1..30     525   defined            IDENTICAL mask and insertion\n    31          2   UNDEFINED          mask 0x7fffffffU\n    32          1   UNDEFINED          mask 0xffffffffU (lsb == 0, full-register copy)\n\n*** THE Q4 PROPERTY IS ESTABLISHED BY ENUMERATION, NOT ASSERTED -- and it is the part that matters\nmore than the fix. ***  *\"No currently defined pair changes behavior.  For all 556 such pairs the\nmask is numerically identical ... Enumeration also found zero mismatches against an independently\nconstructed, bit-by-bit field mask across all 1024 pairs.\"*  **So this is a UB repair and not a\nbehavioural change wearing one's clothes** -- which was the risk I flagged.\n\n## THE ORACLE: UBSan, and `build-asan/` ALREADY HAS IT\n\n*\"The useful runtime observable is a UBSan diagnostic at the offending expression -- not a\nparticular wrong register value.\"*  `build-asan/src/cpus/Makefile` already carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined`, and **ASan alone would NOT detect this arithmetic UB.**\n*(Confirmed independently: tonight's `build-asan` rebuild log shows `-fsanitize=address,undefined`\non the link line, and that tree is currently PART1 CLEAN / PART2_CLEAR.)*\n\n**A caution it volunteered and a detector must respect: \"468 counts INPUT PAIRS, not expected\ndiagnostic lines.\"**  A row asserting 468 diagnostics would be pinning the wrong quantity.\n\n## SCOPE: still TWO rounds with [[armbfcdecode]]\n\nThe decoder unconditionally supplies `&cpu->cd.arm.r[rm]` including source field 15; this patch\nmakes no operand-selection change, so it leaves that defect untouched -- *\"including at the 525\nordinary, already-defined pairs.\"*  **And it helps the other round: defined mask arithmetic removes\na confounder from any subsequent BFC check.**\n\n**STATUS: READY TO IMPLEMENT.**  Patch supplied, no-behaviour-change property enumerated, oracle\nnamed, tree for it already built.  **NOT implemented tonight because `#513` is mid-round with an\nuncommitted working set** -- mixing two rounds in one commit is not a shortcut worth taking.\n*Unlike [[armbfcdecode]], this round is NOT blocked on a manual: it is a C-semantics repair, and the\none architectural question (what `msb < lsb` should do) is answered by an explicit, documented\nemulator CHOICE rather than by a citation.*\n\n*** SIX SEATS, UNANIMOUS: THE `#510` FORM DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND THE ARM MANUAL IS NOW LOCAL. ***\n\n**All six confirmed the Section 2 difference** -- UBFX/SBFX encode *widthm1* so their width is\n`1 + ((iword >> 16) & 31)` and never negative (confirmed in the disassembler at `cpu_arm.c:2382`),\nwhile BFI encodes MSB so `width = msb - lsb + 1` reaches [-30, 32].  `#510`'s `(width == 32) ?\n0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1)` covers none of the negative widths.  glm re-derived the\n1024/468/465 arithmetic independently.\n\n*** THE PRIMARY SOURCE ARRIVED FROM THE SEAT, AFTER THREE URLS OF MINE FAILED. ***  The brief said\nplainly that no local manual covers BFI -- `grep -c -w BFI ddi0100i.txt` is **0**, because DDI 0100I\npredates ARMv6T2.  I had tried three candidate URLs for DDI 0406 and all three returned JSON or\nHTML.  **codex supplied the object id that serves the PDF**, and it is now local and extracted:\n`_scratchpad/ddi0406.txt`, ARM DDI 0406C.d (ID040418), 8.4 MB / 143,260 lines.\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING, not taken from the seat's anchor** -- `ddi0406.txt:18066`, A8.8.20 BFI:\n\n    if msbit >= lsbit then\n        R[d]<msbit:lsbit> = R[n]<(msbit-lsbit):0>;\n        // Other bits of R[d] are unchanged\n    else\n        UNPREDICTABLE;\n\n> **SO `msbit < lsbit` IS UNPREDICTABLE WITH NO REQUIRED DESTINATION VALUE.**  Preserving the\n> destination is therefore an EMULATOR POLICY CHOICE and must not be written up as architecture --\n> which is exactly how codex framed it, and it is the distinction the round has to carry.\n\n**THE PRESCRIBED FIX:**\n\n    uint32_t mask = (width <= 0) ? 0U :\n        ((width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1));\n\nThe unsigned shift then executes only for widths 1-31, and the later shifts by `lsb` stay in 0-31.\n\n*** AND A CAUTION THAT CHANGES THE ROUND'S CLAIM: THIS IS NOT DEFINEDNESS-ONLY AT WIDTH 32. ***\ncodex: *\"There is no C-defined old result to preserve at widths 31 and 32.  In particular, width 32\nmust copy the whole source into the destination.  Under the count-masked host lowering described by\n`#510`, the old BFI mask would instead be zero, leaving the destination UNCHANGED.  With different\nsource and destination values, that is a GUEST-VISIBLE BEHAVIOUR CORRECTION as well as a\ndefinedness repair.\"*  It has not measured that lowering at BFI itself, and neither have I.  **The\nmanual makes width 32 legal and meaningful** -- `<width>` is *\"in the range 1 to 32-<lsb>\"*, so\nwidth 32 implies lsb 0 and copies the whole register.  **So the round may NOT describe itself as\ndefinedness-only, and it owes either a measurement of the lowering or an explicit disclosure.**"
hold_question "`armbfiwidth` IS THE THIRD SITE OF AN IDIOM `#510` FIXED AT TWO, AND SIX SEATS AGREE ITS FORM DOES NOT TRANSFER -- so the question is not whether to fix it but WHAT THE ROUND MAY CLAIM.  SETTLED: UBFX/SBFX encode widthm1 so their width is never negative, while BFI encodes MSB so `width = msb - lsb + 1' reaches [-30, 32]; of 1024 encodable (msb,lsb) pairs, 468 reach an undefined shift and 465 of those also have architecturally invalid ordering.  The prescribed fix is `mask = (width <= 0) ? 0U : ((width == 32) ? 0xffffffffU : ((1U << width) - 1))'.  AND THE ARM MANUAL IS NOW LOCAL AND READ: ddi0406.txt:18066 (A8.8.20) gives `if msbit >= lsbit ... else UNPREDICTABLE', with NO required destination value.  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) THE ROUND'S CLAIM: codex warns this is NOT definedness-only -- at width 32 the manual says BFI copies the whole register (`<width>' is 1 to 32-<lsb>), while under the count-masked host lowering `#510' describes, the OLD mask would be zero and leave the destination unchanged.  So a guest-visible BEHAVIOUR CORRECTION may be hiding inside what I would otherwise write up as a definedness repair, and NEITHER of us has measured that lowering at BFI.  Does the round owe that measurement before it may ship, or is an explicit disclosure enough?  (2) POLICY versus ARCHITECTURE: for `msbit < lsbit' the manual says UNPREDICTABLE and mandates nothing, so `mask = 0' (preserve the destination) is an EMULATOR POLICY choice.  Is silently preserving right, or does an UNPREDICTABLE encoding deserve a once-only announcement?  The tree has precedent for both and no universal policy.  (3) THE DETECTOR: `#510`'s own finding is that `1 << 31' and `1U << 31' give the SAME mask after the `- 1', so NO guest-value row can kill a `1U' -> `1' regression -- only UBSAN or a source pin can.  Given that, what may this round's detector honestly claim to cover, and does the disclosed `ubsanshiftlane' debt have to land first?"
