1: # GXemul est/ — Outstanding bug candidates (not yet fixed)
2: 
3: > ## 2026-08-01 — OPEN LIST, at a glance
4: > **[#385 status, 2026-08-12]** This head is a snapshot dated 2026-08-01 that has drifted
5: > from the charter in the next paragraph: most entries below now carry ✅/strikethrough
6: > resolution marks (the charter says resolved items are REMOVED), and residuals found from
7: > 2026-08-02 onward lived only in the dated tail, invisible from here. What is true today:
8: > **the live queue is the harness TaskList, plus the "post-2026-08-01 open residuals" block
9: > just below, plus the un-ticked entries in this head.** Ticked/struck entries are kept in
10: > place — mass removal was judged too deletion-risky for a records round (an over-deleted
11: > open item is forgotten work; a kept-but-ticked one is only clutter) — and their full
12: > stories live in the dated tail and the CHANGELOG round blocks.
13: >
14: > **Only genuinely OPEN items live here.** Resolved ones are removed, not annotated: an
15: > index that accumulates its own history stops being an index, and a section headed
16: > "everything genuinely open" that is mostly ticks is the dishonest-listing class #270
17: > exists to prevent. The dated blocks below and the CHANGELOG carry what was fixed and
18: > why. Rounds 60–65 (#295–#300) cleared the entire floating-point feasibility queue;
19: > what follows is what they left, plus what they found on the way.
20: >
21: > ### OPEN -- filed 2026-09-11 from `#524` pass D: a cheap way to get more assertions per session
22: > ### silently moves every assertion after the first onto the FAST path
23: >
24: > **`multiaccesswarms`** (MED) -- **a probe row testing a SLOW-PATH fix with a MULTI-ACCESS
25: > instruction is vacuous from the second access onward.**  The first cold access goes through the
26: > slow path *and warms the page*; every later access in that session takes the FAST path, which the
27: > fix does not touch and a slow-path mutant does not alter.
28: >
29: > Measured on `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2`: a mutant that deviates at the second access left the row GREEN.
30: > Found by kimi (measuring); glm reached it from the other end by calling the kill table
31: > *"internally impossible as stated"* because a row it expected to red did not.
32: >
33: > **This is the CHANNEL check's own shape** -- *a row whose named channel cannot vary under its
34: > named mutant is vacuous by construction* -- **and it is knowable at WRITING time.**  It was missed
35: > by reasoning about the ARITHMETIC of the second access, which is genuinely different, instead of
36: > about WHICH PATH SERVICES IT.
37: >
38: > **THE RULE: a row testing a slow-path repair drives ONE access per cold page.**  More assertions
39: > mean more sessions, not more registers.  A multi-register transfer kept for register-list-length
40: > coverage must label its later assertions as FAST-path observations rather than count them as
41: > slow-path coverage.
42: >
43: > #### *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 BY THE grok SEAT. THE CLASS IS LIVE, THE POPULATION IS **THE FOUNDING
44: > #### ROW AND NOTHING ELSE**, AND THE RULE'S OWN REMEDY IS MORE EXPENSIVE THAN THE MECHANISM NEEDS. ***
45: >
46: > **DO NOT CLOSE THIS ROW AS "CENSUS EMPTY."**  The zero is a zero of *new unlabeled fully-vacuous
47: > rows*; the founding row is still shipped, still inside `gate_arm.sh`'s pinned `ARMBDT_RESULT`
48: > **43/43**, and still vacuous for the reason the rule names -- **a gate-counted always-green slot in
49: > its own denominator.**
50: >
51: > **MEMBERSHIP IS NOT MECHANICALLY ENUMERABLE, and the deriver-keyed-on-a-correlate rule predicted
52: > exactly why.**  A `LDM`/`STM`/`LDRD` grep fails BOTH halves of membership and sweeps in six
53: > non-members (the `#378` WARM/COLD pair, which is the *opposite* polarity -- a fast-path repair;
54: > the memcpy fold; `#389` LDRD, which is ONE `memory_rw(datalen=8)`, not two page checks; `#386`
55: > SWP, which never consults `host_load`; and a 1-register `LDMIA`).  **The grep-able CANDIDATE
18752: *** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, one screen away. *** `case 4:`
18753: (`i80321_isteer`) has the **identical structure** -- load arm assigns from the modelled field, store arm
18754: writes it -- and carries **no `fatal()` and no `exit()`**. So `crn 0`'s kill is an OUTLIER inside its own
18755: switch rather than a policy: the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times, where
18756: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing and not the sibling he did not. *That is a
18757: far stronger warrant than "it is a host kill", and it is one grep away* -- which is exactly what the
18758: option-set rule's sibling check is for.
18759: 
18760: Reachability is explicit rather than inferred: `machine_iq80321.c` installs it with
18761: `cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;`, and Iyonix does the same. Emulated firmware reading its
18762: own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no
18763: supervisor-only precondition.
18764: 
18765: Disposition: **member 4 (ACCEPT)**, two lines deleted, no latch, for the same reason as `mapledmaaddr`.
18766: *** L4, CORRECTED 2026-09-11: THAT SENTENCE READ "FAULT is unavailable regardless -- the failed-device
18767: exception path is MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM."  **IT IS WRONG, AND IT REASONS FROM THE WRONG
18768: MECHANISM.** ***  The MIPS/M88K claim is about `memory_rw.c`'s failed-DEVICE-ACCESS path, which is
18769: irrelevant here: a coprocessor register transfer is not a device access and never reaches it.  **ARM has
18770: undefined-instruction delivery and it is already used** -- `cpu_arm.c:2677`, in `arm_cdp()`:
18771: 
18772:     fatal("[ arm_cdp: pc=0x%08x, iword=0x%08x ]\n", (int)cpu->pc, iword);
18773:     arm_exception(cpu, ARM_EXCEPTION_UND);      /*  LIVE, not commented out  */
18774: 
18775: and `arm_exception()` at `:616` handles `ARM_EXCEPTION_UND` at `:634`.  **So member 5 (FAULT) IS
18776: AVAILABLE on ARM coprocessor sites.**  Note the near neighbour, which is the more interesting fact:
18777: `arm_mcr_mrc()`'s missing-handler branch at `:2660` carries **the very same call, commented out** --
18778: `// arm_exception(cpu, ARM_EXCEPTION_UND);` -- so an access to an UNINSTALLED coprocessor prints and
18779: falls through silently today.
18780: 
18781: **THE DISPOSITION IS UNCHANGED AND ITS REAL WARRANT IS BETTER: ACCEPT (member 4) stands on its own
18782: merits** -- the model already computes the correct value and then discards it, which is the footbridge
18783: IRQEnable precedent, and FAULT would be wrong for a register the model implements.  What was wrong was
18784: the claim that the alternative did not exist.
18785: 
18786: *** WHY THIS WAS WORTH CORRECTING RATHER THAN LEAVING: the false warrant is TRANSFERABLE, and there are
18787: SEVEN sibling guest-reachable host kills in `cpu_arm_coproc.c` waiting to reuse it *** (CP6 `unknown`,
18788: CP14 `unknown`, CP15 PID, CP6 ISTEER, CP15 endian, CP6 ISRC, CP15 `rd == ARM_PC`).  **For the two
18789: `unknown:` default arms, UND is very likely the RIGHT member**, and a sentence saying FAULT is
18790: impossible would have removed it from the option set before anyone looked.  Found by the fable5

